N2  - SESSION TITLE: Lung Pathology Case Reports Posters (B) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Pulmonary arteriovenous malformations (PAVMs) are structurally abnormal vascular communications between pulmonary arteries and veins that range in size and complexity and provide an anatomic right-to-left shunt. There is underrecognition of PAVMs in clinical practice. PAVMs become more significant in pregnancy when they enlarge and rupture, leading to important maternal complications including hemoptysis, hemothorax and/or maternal death. Patients may be asymptomatic, have dyspnea on exertion or hypoxemia according to the degree of right to left shunt. PAVMs of any size increases the risk for paradoxical emboli that may cause ischemic strokes, myocardial infarction, cerebral and peripheral abscesses, discitis and migraines. The normal hormonal changes with pregnancy that lead to increased blood volume, vasodilation, increase cardiac output and lead to increased shunting and development of symptoms. We discuss the case of a young woman at term pregnancy with history of pAVM presenting for vaginal delivery and an evidenced-based review on best practices during delivery to avoid life threatening complications. CASE PRESENTATION: 30 y.o woman G2P1011 at 39 weeks gestation with chronic hypertension, PAVMs, gestational diabetes, history of SVT presented to labor and delivery suite for elective induction of labor. She denied shortness of breath, cough, chest pain or headache. Vital signs and oxygen saturation were normal. Laboratory data was only significant for normocytic anemia. Pulmonary was consulted as a part of the multidisciplinary team to determine the safest method of delivery. Her antenatal evaluation included referral to High Risk Obstetric Clinic, Maternal Fetal Medicine (MFM), Cardiology and Pulmonary. In view of worsening dyspnea on exertion during climbing stairs at 5 months gestation: 6 Minute Walk Test done was normal; CT scan showed multiple pulmonary AVMs with mild interval increase in size compared with prior imaging. (Figures 1, 2 & 3). After discussion with the patient and the multidisciplinary team, vaginal delivery was deemed to pose greater risk of pAVMs complications.and recommendation for cesarean section made. Patient subsequently had an uneventful cesarean section and postoperative course with the delivery of a healthy baby. DISCUSSION: Deterioration during pregnancy in pAVM is multifactorial, related to hormonal changes, increased cardiovascular demand, increased blood volume and cardiac output which can lead to worsening of right to left shunt. These physiological changes can also result in increased pulmonary blood flow that can lead to dilation and eventually rupture of thin walled pAVM. The majority of morbidity and mortality from pAVMs occur during the second, third trimester and in the intrapartum period with the most common complications reported being hemothorax and heart failure. Recommendations are to manage these patients as high risk pregnancies involving multidisciplinary team discussion to define optimal approach to decrease the risk of complications. Valsalva maneuver during labor will increase intrathoracic pressure with high probability of rupture in pAVM, hence the vaginal route is contraindicated.Treatment of pAVMs in pregnancy is usually deferred until after the postpartum period due to risks of radiation and preterm labor. CONCLUSIONS: This case highlights the need for greater awareness and understanding of pAVM in pregnancy and its attendant complications and management during delivery. REFERENCE #1: Shovlin CL, et al. Thorax 2017;72:1154–1163. doi:10.1136/thoraxjnl-2017-210764 REFERENCE #2: Cartin-Ceba R, Swanson KL, Krowka MJ. Pulmonary arteriovenous malformations. Chest 2013;144:1033-44. REFERENCE #3: Ng CSH, Manlulu AV, Lee TW, et al. Pulmonary hemorrhage from arteriovenous malformations: implications and management in pregnancy. Hong Kong J Gynaecol Obstet Midwifery 2011; 11: 67–72. Esplin MS, Varner MW. Progression of pulmonary arteriovenous malformation during pregnancy: case report and review of the literature. Obstet Gynecol Surv 1997; 52: 248–253. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Hala Boktor No relevant relationships by Alicia Thomas
