N2  - SESSION TITLE: Pulmonary Manifestations Case Reports Posters (B) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/07/2024 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Pregnancy and the peripartum state represent vulnerable phases, prompting comprehensive efforts to anticipate and proactively address potential complications, the most dreaded of which is pre-eclampsia. Awareness, early treatment, and prevention of possible end-organ damage are paramount. Significant emphasis is placed on neuroprotective mechanisms, induction of labor through magnesium therapy, and even mini-dose aspirin.In sharp contrast, there is decidedly less anticipation and knowledge in navigating sudden respiratory collapse in the same populace. Acute pulmonary edema, though rare, is a known devastating complication of pre-eclampsia and qualifies as a "severe feature" of the disease spectrum. It is postulated to complicate up to 8 per 10,000 pregnancies, most commonly in the immediate postpartum period. CASE PRESENTATION: A 39-year-old female, gravida 3 para 0-1-2 with history of chronic hypertension and gestational diabetes sent in at 34 weeks 3 days gestation was admitted for elective induction of labor due to concerns of SIPEC (superimposed pre-eclampsia) with severe features after reporting headache and high blood pressure readings at home. She was admitted, started on magnesium and administered Cytotec for induction. After noted fetal deceleration tracings, she underwent an uncomplicated caesarian section and was transferred to recovery. Six hours later, she developed sudden onset shortness of breath and chest pain.Vitally, she hypertensive (174/95 mmHg), tachycardic (HR 94 bpm), tachypneic (27/min) saturating 72% on ambient air. Arterial blood gas analysis revealed respiratory alkalosis with hypoxemia. Chest radiography showed bilateral alveolar infiltrates. Her oxygen requirements continued to escalate and was placed on high-flow nasal cannula oxygen and started on diuretics and broad spectrum antibiotics. ECG revealed sinus tachycardia with normal troponin level. Routine blood investigations (full blood count, liver and kidney function tests, serum electrolytes) were within normal limits. D-dimer (1700 ng·mL−1) and brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) (3424 pg·mL−1) were elevated. Computed tomography (CT) of the thorax revealed bilateral alveolar consolidation without pulmonary embolism, transthoracic echocardiogram ruled out peripartum cardiomyopathy. With continued diuretic therapy, her respiratory status improved and repeat chest X-ray showed resolution of pulmonary opacities, supporting a diagnosis of pulmonary edema rather than an infectious etiology. DISCUSSION: Physiological changes in cardiac output in immediate postpartum aftermath includes a 60–80% increase in volume due to redistribution of blood flow from placental to maternal circulation with relief of the inferior vena cava obstruction by the fetus; this returns to pre-labor levels approximately 12 hours later and pre-pregnancy level by 2 weeks. Pulmonary edema will be precipitated if there is either a further augmentation in cardiac preload (such as infusion of fluids) or increased pulmonary capillary permeability (such as in pre-eclampsia) or both. CONCLUSIONS: Acute pulmonary edema is the main cardiovascular complication of severe preeclampsia -- occuring onlin in 3% of total cases, of which70% are postpartum. Awareness of the physiological mechanisms can perhaps lead to preventative measures such as judicious use of tocolytics and IV fluids antepartum and strict blood pressure control. Other risk fators include advanced maternal age, multiparity and chronic hypertension REFERENCE #1: Bokslag A, van Weissenbruch M, Mol BW, de Groot CJ. Preeclampsia; short and long-term consequences for mother and neonate. Early Human Development. 2016;102:47-50. REFERENCE #2: Bhorat I, Naidoo DP, Moodley J. Maternal cardiac haemodynamics in severe pre-eclampsia complicated by acute pulmonary oedema: a review. The Journal of Maternal-Fetal & Neonatal Medicine. 2016;30(23):2769-2777. REFERENCE #3: Vaught AJ, Kovell LC, Szymanski LM, Mayer SA, Seifert SM, Vaidya D et al. Acute cardiac effects of severe pre-eclampsia. Journal of the American College of Cardiology. 2018: 72(1):1-114 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Muhammad Abbas No relevant relationships by Faiza Chaudhary No relevant relationships by Natasha Garg No relevant relationships by Aizaaz Khan No relevant relationships by Tahmina Kushan No relevant relationships by Mohammad Raja No disclosure on file for Uzair Sherza No disclosure on file for Peyman Ziai
