N2  - Background: Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death worldwide, and women who experience complications during pregnancy, such as pregnancy loss (miscarriage and stillbirth), hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, and gestational diabetes, have a significantly higher risk of future CVD. Increasing awareness of CVD risk among healthcare providers is crucial for effective engagement in risk reduction programs. Aim: This study aimed to identify knowledge gaps of Australian healthcare providers regarding CVD risk associated with one or more complications of pregnancy, and to explore healthcare providers’ practice towards the management of the risk. Methods: A cross-sectional surveys was conducted, recruiting 397 healthcare providers in Australia. Data were collected through face-to-face and online surveys and analysed using quantitative descriptive and inferential statistical procedures. Results: The study populations had suboptimal levels of knowledge about the increased risk of CVD associated with complications of pregnancy. Healthcare providers showed insufficient practice and attitudes towards the assessment, prevention, and management of CVD risk among these high-risk women. The study identified knowledge and practice gaps among healthcare providers regarding the increased risk of CVD associated with a complicated pregnancy. Mismanagement of CVD risk in this group of women was recognised by the participants. Conclusion: In conclusion, the findings of this study underscore the importance of moving midwifery forward in addressing the increased cardiovascular risk faced by women who have experienced complications of pregnancy. The identified knowledge and practice gaps among healthcare providers, specifically midwives as the first line in women health care, highlight a clear need for enhanced education, support, and research initiatives. By empowering midwives with comprehensive knowledge about the increased risk of cardiovascular disease associated with pregnancy complications, we can advance their role as health promoters and key advocates for the well-being of women. Equipping midwives with the necessary tools and resources to effectively assess, prevent, and manage cardiovascular risk in high-risk populations represents a significant step towards improving maternal health outcomes and reducing the burden of cardiovascular disease in women.
