N2  - PURPOSE: Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (HDP) such as preeclampsia, eclampsia, and gestational hypertension complicate 5–10% of pregnancies and affect maternal morbidity and mortality. Pregnancies with HDP increase HDP risk in subsequent pregnancies; however, little is known of the accumulated effect of recurrent HDPs on cardiovascular outcomes. We hypothesized recurrent HDP increased the risk of subsequent coronary heart disease (CHD) and stroke. METHODS: Deliveries to women with ≥2 singleton, live births in South Carolina (2004–2016) were evaluated for HDP, defined by birth certificate and/or hospitalization/ED visit data. Hospitalization/ED visit and death certificate data defined subsequent CHD and stroke. Adjusted hazard ratios (aHR) were generated with extended Cox models incorporating time at risk with 0, 1 or >1 deliveries with HDP, adjusting for significant sociodemographic (age, race/ethnicity, education, rural residence, payer type, BMI), behavioral (tobacco use), and clinical characteristics (pre-pregnancy hypertension or diabetes, gestational age and parity). RESULTS: Of 163,579 women with ≥2 deliveries, 2.5% had >1; 11.0% had one; and 86.5% had no HDP pregnancies. Compared with none, women with one (aHR=1.88; 95% CI=1.64–2.16) or >1 (aHR=2.13; 95% CI=1.69–2.69) HDP delivery were at greater risk of subsequent CHD. Similarly, women with one (aHR=1.46; 95% CI=1.14–1.89) or >1 (aHR=2.01; 95% CI=1.35–3.01) HDP delivery had greater stroke risk. CONCLUSION: Having at least one HDP increased risk of subsequent incident CHD and stroke. However, despite having higher point estimates, the risk in women with recurrent (>1) HDP did not statistically differ from women with one HDP. Keywords: Maternal outcomes; Coronary heart disease; Stroke; Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy; Pre-pregnancy hypertension Funding: DAW and AMM received financial support from NIH/NHLBI K01HL138273.
