N2  - Background and Aims: To study if women with familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) and their offsprings have excess risk of pregnancy-related or birth-related adverse outcomes, compared to age- adjusted women without FH and their offsprings. Further to study risks associated with statin use during pregnancy. Methods: A cross-sectional study of Norwegian women with genetically verified FH and age- matched controls. Data on women’s health during pregnancy, childbirth and in the maternity period, and children’s health at birth and up to one year of age were obtained from the Medical Birth Registry during 1967-2018. Data on statins (C10AA and C10BA) were obtained from the Norwegian Prescription database during 2005-2018. Results were presented as odds ratio (OR)s with (95% CI) adjusted for mother’s age at pregnancy, children’s birth year and parity. Results: We included 1903 FH-women with 3869 pregnancies and 33 976 control-women with 68225 pregnancies. Mean age at start of pregnancy was 28 years for both groups. A higher proportion of mothers with FH (3.4%) than controls (2.9%) experienced preeclampsia during any of their pregnancies (OR = 1.21 (1.00-1.46). No excess risk of gestational hypertension, but reduced risk of gestational diabetes (OR 0.58 (0.36-0.92) and large bleedings during birth (OR 0.81 (0.71-0.92) were observed for FH. There was no higher risk of low (<2500 grams) or high (>4500 grams) birthweight, heart malformation, stillbirths or neonatal deaths between infants born of mothers with FH compared to controls. In total 6.7% of women with FH had used statins anytime during pregnancy. The association between statin use in FH and pregnancy- and birth related outcomes will be presented. Conclusions: Based on the significant number of pregnancies in both cases and controls, our results suggests that except for a higher proportion of preeclampsia cases, genetically verified FH is not associated with any negative outcomes of pregnancy and birth.
