N2  - Introduction: Methaemoglobinaemia is a rare and potentially lethal disorder of haemoglobin with congenital and acquired causes. Given the rarity of the congenital form, there is a relative lack of data from which routine management strategies can be devised, particularly in pregnancy, providing a challenge for clinicians. We report the case of a new diagnosis of congenital methaemoglobinaemia in late pregnancy and her peripartum management. Case Report: A 39-year-old woman presented at 36 weeks gestation with palpitations and mild tachycardia. Her obstetric history included three previous miscarriages. Due to a history of pre-eclampsia and gestational diabetes, induction of labour had been incidentally planned for the following day. She was found to have an oxygen saturation of 92% in room air but showed no other signs or symptoms of hypoxia. The arterial blood gas revealed an PaO2 of 16.3 kPa and a methaemoglobin (MetHb) concentration of 33%. The patient denied any previous diagnosis or symptoms. Given the lack of exposure to linked toxins and her apparent stability with such a high MetHb concentration, haematology advised that this was likely chronic congenital methaemoglobinaemia. They recommended high flow oxygen during labour, intravenous fluids and ascorbic acid. Methylene blue was not recommended. After multidisciplinary discussion, and in part due to maternal choice, it was felt that a caesarean section (CS) rather than induction of labour with an early epidural would be the safest way to deliver. This was performed uneventfully under spinal anaesthesia and supplemental oxygen. Discussion: Due to hypoxia, methaemoglobinaemia during pregnancy can lead to fetal morbidity and mortality. As pregnancy progresses there is a higher oxygen demand, which may cause an acute attack, even in the absence of preceding symptoms [1]. As methylene blue is teratogenic, and can have numerous adverse effects on the fetus and newborn, its use in pregnancy is spared for extreme cases [1]. There may be an association between methaemoglobinaemia and preeclampsia, as well as increased propensity to miscarry, both of which were seen in this patient [2]. Despite the elevated MetHb, our patient appeared comfortable at rest. Nonetheless, it would have been difficult to predict the impact of the physiological stress of labour, even with an early epidural to mitigate this. It was felt that regional anaesthesia and a CS would provide the safest option for delivery.
