N2  - Introduction: Haemodynamic changes in pregnancy may exacerbate underlying cardiac disease, which remains one of the leading causes of maternal death [1, 2].We describe a category 1 caesarean section (CS) in a lady with a previously undiagnosed ASD resulting in right-to-left shunt. Case Report: A non-English speaking, 37-year-old primiparous woman with a background of hypothyroidism and gestational diabetes mellitus presented to labour ward via ambulance with spontaneous rupture of membranes and bleeding at term. She was confused, hypoxic and in circulatory shock. Differential diagnoses included abruption, pulmonary embolism and sepsis. She was contracting and 3 cm dilated on examination. She had features of a difficult airway including micrognathia. A decision was made for category 1 CS shortly after arrival due to prolonged fetal bradycardia. General anaesthesia was induced. Despite prompt intubation using videolaryngos copy, she desaturated rapidly to 70%. A baby girl was delivered in good condition. Partial placental abruption was noted, with blood loss of approximately 500 ml. This was felt to be insufficient to justify the patient’s instability alone. Invasive monitoring was established. The patient remained acidaemic with increasing vasopressor requirements and relatively hypoxic, requiring an FiO2 of 80% to maintain a PaO2 of 8kPa. She was transferred to ICU post-operatively. CT showed a left lower lobe consolidation, evidence of right heart strain, with an enlarged liver and dilated hepatic and portal veins. There was no PE. Echocardiography demonstrated a large ASD, and a subsequent bubble study while ventilated showed right-to-left shunt with a pulmonary artery systolic pressure of 26mmhg. She was extubated on day 3, and referred to a tertiary centre for ASD closure due to ongoing hypoxia. The patient subsequently reported knowledge of cardiac disease as a child; however this was not disclosed during her pregnancy. Discussion: We postulate that the drop in systemic vascular resistance in pregnancy, combined with onset of labour and possible haemorrhage resulted in a reversal of flow through the ASD, resulting in right to left shunt with hypoxia and circulatory shock. Thiswas worsened by positive pressure ventilation following induction. This case highlights the critical role of translation services to optimise communication with patients who do not speak English as their first language.
