N2  - Objective(s): Several reproductive events have important maternal cardiometabolic health impacts beyond pregnancy. These cardiometabolic associated reproductive events (CARE) (i.e., hypertensive disorders of pregnancy [HDP], gestational diabetes, preterm delivery, abruption, birthweight < 2500 grams) identify females at increased risk of premature cardiometabolic morbidity (hypertension, dysglycemia, obesity, cardiovascular diseases, etc.). Objective was to describe the incidence in CARE over 2010–2020 in Alberta, as a foundational step in developing a provincial cardiometabolic health promotion pathway. Methods: A population-based retrospective cohort was studied by linking provincial administrative databases. The Discharge Abstract Database (DAD) was used to identify all singleton delivery hospitalizations in Alberta through fiscal years 2010/11 and 2019/20. Those with pre-pregnancy cardiovascular diseases, diabetes, or kidney disease were excluded. CARE outcomes were classified using IDC9/10 codes. CARE incidences were summarized by year and were compared over time using Mann-Kendall tests. P values < 0.05 were considered statistically significant. Results: 491 882 deliveries (314 222 females) were included. The incidence of CARE increased by 27.4% from 19.0% in 2010/11 to 24.2% in 2019/20 (P < 0.05). Specifically, HDP increased from 6.1% to 7.7%, gestational diabetes from 5.7% to 10.0%, and birth weight < 2500 grams from 4.9% to 5.6% (P < 0.05). Any preterm delivery and abruption did not change significantly. Conclusion(s): All CARE increased in Alberta by 27.4% over a ten-year period. These findings identify: 1) major gaps in implementation of evidence-based interventions to prevent CARE (e.g., physical activity, low dose aspirin); and 2) an increasing population with CARE at risk of premature cardiometabolic diseases to reach with health promotion interventions.
