N2  - Introduction: Obesity (OB) is a pandemic that negatively impacts quality of life, rates of chronic diseases and mortality, also impairing fertility and worsening gestational outcomes. Bariatric surgery (BS) is the most effective treatment for OB and is growing in popularity, particularly sleeve gastrectomy (SL) and Roux-en-Y gastric bypass (GB). Half of the women undergoing BS are in childbearing age. Challenges involving pregnancy and contraception in BS patients are frequently not properly addressed. Conflicting evidence and paucity of studies contributes to lack of assertive information in these issues. Methods: A search was performed in Pubmed database using the following strategy: “((contraception) OR (contraceptive methods) OR (contraceptive agents) OR (contraceptive devices)) AND ((bariatric surgery) OR (gastric bypass) OR (sleeve gastrectomy))”, and 130 articles were selected. A filter was applied to include only reviews, systematic reviews, randomized controlled trials and meta-analysis in the last 10 years, retrieving 25 articles. Based on their titles, 17 were considered relevant. One was excluded because the full text was not accessible online. Results: Most studies (11) were narrative reviews, 1 was a systematic review, 3 were guidelines and 1 was an expert opinion review article. Analysis showed that bariatric surgery (BS) can improve fertility, increasing conception rates in 33-100%, leading to an increased risk of unplanned pregnancies. BS reduces the frequency of gestational diabetes mellitus, preeclampsia and macrosomia, but studies report an increased risk of small to gestational neonates: GB (Odds-ratio OR 2,16) and SG (OR 1,14). Since nutritional deficiencies are more frequent during the rapid weight loss phase after BS, a time to conception shorter than 12 months was linked to increased frequency of inadequate gestational weight gain (75%) and reduced longterm weight loss. Guidelines recommend avoiding conception for 12 to 24 months after BS, but up to 1.6% women conceive in the first 18 months. There is no consensus about efficacy of oral contraceptives (OC) in post-BS patients, so they should not be the first line choice, due to potential failure risk and increased risk of venous thromboembolism. Guidelines recommend using long-acting reversible contraceptive strategies (LARCs) such as progestin intrauterine devices (IUD), since in addition to being highly effective, they have an impact on reducing menstrual blood loss. Subcutaneous implants and bilateral salpingectomy concurrent with BS might be viable alternatives. Contraception counseling is performed to 30-85,7% of post-BS patients, but 30% report no contraception and 33% report using OC after disabsorptive BS. These results emphasize the need to improve contraceptive counseling in BS patients and suggest referral to a gynecologist as a standard multidisciplinary approach.
