N2  - Background Individuals with gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) have an increased risk of obstetric-associated complications and longterm risks of diabetes and cardiovascular (CV) disease. However, data on 30-day readmission peripartum CV complications remain limited. Methods We used data from the Nationwide Readmission Database (2015-2020). ICD-9 or -10 codes were used to identify delivery hospitalizations and GDM diagnoses. Results A total of 9,200,108 hospitalizations for deliveries were identified, of which 8.1% of individuals had GDM (n=743,558). A total of 20,394 (2.7%) individuals with index hospitalization diagnoses of GDM were readmitted within 30 days of discharge. Readmission rates for preeclampsia (32.2% vs. 25.1%) and peripartum cardiomyopathy (PPCM) (2.7% vs. 2.3%) were significantly higher for GDM compared with individuals without GDM. After adjustment for age, mode of admission, income, insurance, chronic hypertension, heart failure, obesity, dyslipidemia, smoking, preterm birth, stillborn, and Cesarean delivery on index admission, GDM remained independently associated with 30-day readmission for preeclampsia (Odds ratio (OR) 1.38, (1.34-1.42) and PPCM (OR 1.23, (1.12-1.35). Conclusion Individuals with GDM face a heightened risk of 30-day CV readmission with preeclampsia and PPCM. This high-risk population necessitates meticulous postpartum care strategies to proactively mitigate the occurrence of these readmissions. [Formula presented]
