N2  - Background Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy(HDP) are associated with future cardiovascular disease; mechanisms are not well defined. We examined the association between ASCVD biomarkers and HDP 5-10 years after childbirth. Methods Secondary analysis of the NICHD MFMU Network Gestational Diabetes (GDM) Trial Follow-Up study. Participants were recruited and biospecimens obtained 5-10 years after the original trial of a dietary intervention for mild GDM. Patients were included in this analysis if a biospecimen was available and HDP status was known. We compared patients who experienced HDP to normotensive controls (NC). Outcomes included ASCVD serum biomarkers Apolipoprotein B(ApoB), high-sensitivity C-reactive protein(hs-CRP), and creatinine. Generalized linear models were used to compare concentrations between groups. Results Of 740 participants, 78 had HDP. Mean duration to follow up was 7.1±1.3 years for both groups. Unadjusted means of each biomarker were not different between groups. After adjusting for obesity, serum creatinine was elevated in HDP (0.78 95%CI (0.74, 0.84)) vs NC (0.72 95%CI (0.70, 0.74)), p= 0.02. Adjusted means for Apo-B between HDP and NC were 67.6, (95%CI (62.8, 72.4)) and 64.1 (95%CI (62.5, 65.8)), (p=0.18), and for hs-CRP were 2.92 (95%CI (2.22, 3.63)) and 2.79 (95%CI (2.54, 3.04)), (p=0.72) (Figure). Conclusion In patients with HDP compared to normotensive controls, serum creatinine, but not ApoB or hs-CRP, was modestly elevated within 10 years of childbirth. [Formula presented]
