N2  - Background Women with angina and no obstructive coronary artery disease (ANOCA) often have coronary vascular dysfunction (epicardial or microvascular) and an increased risk of cardiovascular events. While Adverse Pregnancy Outcomes (APOs) are emerging as strong risk factors for heart disease in women, the relationship between APOs and ANOCA is not well described. Methods Symptomatic women with ANOCA (defined as <50% stenosis) enrolled in the Angina Study between 2022-2023 at Emory were included (n=25). 96% had coronary function testing (CFT) to diagnose endothelium and non-endothelium dependent vascular dysfunction. Baseline demographics and past medical history, including self-reported APOs history were collected. APOs were defined as gestational hypertension, preeclampsia/eclampsia, gestational diabetes, preterm birth, and low birth weight. Descriptive statistics were performed between those with and without APOs. Results Mean age was 60±11 years, with 68% hypertension, 20% diabetes, and 52% hyperlipidemia. All subjects with CFT had coronary endothelial dysfunction based on abnormal acetylcholine response. Fifteen out of 25 subjects (60%) reported APOs: 32% had gestational hypertension, 24% had eclampsia/preeclampsia, and 28% had gestational diabetes, compared to U.S. rates of 13%, 5%, and 8%, respectively. A history of preterm birth was also remarkably prevalent at 40% and 16% had low birth weight pregnancies. Coronary flow reserve (CFR) was lower in the APOs vs. Non-APOs group (2.1±0.9 vs. 2.9±1.3, p=0.14). Conclusion History of APOs is prevalent in women with ANOCA who have coronary endothelial dysfunction diagnosed by CFT. Further investigation of common mechanistic pathways that contribute to APOs and ANOCA in women is needed.
