N2  - Background: GDM, a transitory form of diabetes first recognized during pregnancy, is a known harbinger of future T2DM, hypertension, and cardiac disease (1). To prevent the future onset of T2DM in mothers and offspring, preventive postpartum care and follow-up are necessary. However, the area remains grossly underprioritized. Aim: The objective of this study is to examine long-term metabolic changes including T2DM in a cohort of Moroccan women with a known history of GDM. Method: A prospective cohort study was conducted in Morocco, from November 16, 2020, to December 31, 2021. A total of 355 women participated in the study. Results: Although our study population is relatively young (mean age 31.1 years), having GDM in the index pregnancy was 3.06 (OR = 3.06; 95% IC 1.25–7.52) times more likely to develop T2D within 4 years compared to women with no GDM after controlling for other confounding variables. Women with obesity (BMI ≥ 30 kg/ m2)were 4.15 times more likely to develop T2DM than women with normal weight (OR = 4.15; 95% IC 1.31–13.17). Complying with the recommended healthy diet was a protective factor, decreasing markedly the risk of developing diabetes within 4 years (OR 0.37; 95% CI 0.15–0.89). GDM and obesity were significant predictors of the development of T2DM after controlling for family history of diabetes mellitus (DM), GDM in previous pregnancies, age, parity, and physical activity. Conclusion: Given the emerging burden of gestational diabetes and the steady increase in diabetes-related morbidity and mortality rates (2), there is a pressing need to transform the current MCH system to address a burgeoning metabolic challenge for this high-risk population. Initiating interventions during the postpartum has the potential to confer long-term maternal-child benefits (3)
