N2  - SESSION TITLE: Allergy and Airway Case Report Posters SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: The anti-thymic stromal lymphopoietin (TSLP) monoclonal antibody tezepelumab is a novel biologic therapy targeting upstream regulation of the Th2 pathway and is currently FDA approved for the add-on treatment of patients with severe asthma. To our knowledge, this is the first case of a patient started on tezepelumab during pregnancy. CASE PRESENTATION: A 36 year-old female with gestational diabetes (GDM) and congenital absence of the left lung and associated congenital heart disease presented to our pulmonary clinic at 28 weeks pregnant. She had been diagnosed with asthma in childhood and was on baseline treatment with a combination of theophylline, inhaled high dose fluticasone/salmeterol, as well as nebulized budesonide and up to three times daily as needed nebulized albuterol. Despite this therapy, she required prednisone approximately four times per year for the last five years for asthma exacerbations. She had previously been treated with a trial of dupilumab with no significant effect on annualized exacerbation rate. Prior to establishing in our clinic she had been started on tezepelumab at 20 weeks into her pregnancy due to poorly controlled asthma. In the eight weeks after starting tezepelumab she did not have an exacerbation of her asthma and had significantly reduced nebulizer usage. The patient gave birth at 33 weeks, 2 days by emergency C-section as a result of concern for preterm labor and history of prior C-section and known placenta accreta. Intraoperatively, her cardiopulmonary mechanics were preserved but she had significant blood loss requiring multiple blood products. She gave birth to a preterm infant at 4 pounds and 4 ounces with good tone and no obvious congenital anomalies, but immature thermoregulation and feeding requiring dextrose containing IV fluids and with irregular respirations requiring non invasive ventilatory support and care in the NICU. DISCUSSION: Tezepelumab is an IgG molecule which theoretically can cross the placental barrier so maternal/fetal outcomes should be monitored. Women with long-standing asthma are at increased risk for complications during pregnancy, believed to be secondary to maternal hypoxia and inflammation. We hypothesize that biologics would offer a protective effect in asthma by decreasing inflammation, and thereby reducing maternal/fetal complications. There is insufficient data on biologics and their safety in pregnancy but as per a review by Shakuntulla et al. in 2022, several women stopped taking biologics during pregnancy due to the uncertainty of the effects of biologics on their fetuses. Given the high prevalence of asthma, more studies are necessary to evaluate the safety of biologics in pregnancy. CONCLUSIONS: This patient with a complicated medical history was started on tezepelumab for uncontrolled asthma during pregnancy and needed an emergent c-section most likely due to non-asthma related factors including GDM and placenta accreta. There is no evidence of complications due to tezepelumab use during pregnancy, with the caveat that the drug was also started when the patient was 20 weeks pregnant, well after organogenesis. More data is needed to better understand the effects of tezepelumab in pregnancy. Reference #1: Shakuntulla F, Chiarella SE. Safety of Biologics for Atopic Diseases During Pregnancy. J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract. 2022 Dec;10(12):3149-3155. DISCLOSURES: Advisory Committee Member relationship with regeneron/Sanofi Please note: 6/01/22-present Added 03/31/2023 by Lauren Eggert, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee No relevant relationships by Landy Luna diaz No relevant relationships by Aishwarya Yenepalli
