N2  - A male neonate born at 39 weeks’ gestation by vaginal delivery presented at the time of birth with a diﬀuse eruption of pink to red macules and thin papules on the face, trunk, and extremities, resembling a “blueberry muﬃn rash.” The pregnancy was complicated by gestational diabetes but the mother was otherwise healthy with no family history of autoimmune disease or immunodeficiencies. Labs were significant for thrombocytopenia with platelets 38. Extensive infectious work-up was negative, including CMV, parvovirus, and HSV 1&2 PCRs. The mother’s TORCH panel was also negative. A biopsy was performed that was overall nonspecific. There was a mild perivascular and perieccrine infiltrate of crushed inflammatory cells and no evidence of extramedullary hematopoiesis, leukemia cutis, or mastocytosis. He presented to outpatient dermatology follow-up at the age of 2 months, now with subtle annular plaques on the temples and pre-auricular cheeks as well as periorbital edema and erythema. The mother, who was completely asymptomatic, went for additional labs which showed positive anti-SSA, anti-SSB, and anti-RNP antibodies. The patient underwent additional testing and had a similar antibody profile. He was subsequently diagnosed with neonatal lupus. EKG and echocardiogram were performed which were both normal. He is now 4 months old and his platelet numbers have stabilized. He continues to improve with diligent sun protection alone. We present this case of neonatal lupus that presented at the time of birth with a “blueberry muﬃn-like” eruption to highlight an atypical congenital cutaneous manifestation that may lead to a delay in diagnosis.
