N2  - Introduction: The aim of this study is to characterize the epilepsy phenotype and outcomes in a large cohort of neonatal arterial ischemic stroke (NAIS) and presumed perinatal arterial ischemic stroke (PPAIS). Methods: This was a hospital based retrospective cohort study. Children aged less than 18 years of age with NAIS and PPAIS were included. Children with concurrent hypoxia, cerebral venous thrombosis or parenchymal haemorrhage and those who do not have a complete record set were excluded. Results: A total of 250 patients were enrolled. 136 (54.4%) had NAIS and 114 (45.6%) had PAIS. 150 (60%) were males and 100 (40%) were females. The mean age of last follow-up was 14 years. Most common antenatal risk factors were pregnancy induced hypertension (9%) and gestational diabetes (9%). 14% were born preterm and the rest were born term. The most common arterial territory involved was MCA (88.9%) and there was left preponderance (60.8%). The most common etiology for stroke was presumed placental embolism in 48% (120). Among the NAIS group, the most common presentation was seizures in 67%, with the most common type being asymmetric-clonic (75%). 28% of children had unprovoked seizures following NAIS later in life. Among the PPAIS group, seizures were the first presenting symptom in 13.1% (15). 49% of those in the PPAIS group had epilepsy. Focal epilepsy was the most common epilepsy syndrome following PPAIS. Infantile spasms (IS) were seen in 18 (7.2%) and epileptic encephalopathy with continuous spike-wake in slow wave sleep in 9 (3.6%). Drug-resistant epilepsy (DRE) was seen in 16 (6.4%). 9 underwent epilepsy surgery (6-hemispherectomy and 3-focal resections) with all achieving ILAE Class I outcome. Conclusion: This study shows that epilepsy in later childhood is seen in one-third of children with NAIS, with 7.2% developing IS and 6.4% developing DRE.
