N2  - Background: Ergometrine is rarely associated with coronary vasospasm and myocardial ischaemia in the setting of acute post-partum haemorrhage. This case illustrates a rare presentation of ST elevation myocardial infarction secondary to proven coronary vasospasm from ergometrine administration in post-partum haemorrhage. Case Presentation: A gravida 2, para 1 (G2 P1) 43-year-old female underwent caesarean section at 39 weeks gestation due to fetal macrosomia. Her background was significant for gestational diabetes and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) with an undetectable viral load treated with raltegravir, emtricitabine and tenofovir alafenamide. The procedure was complicated by uterine atony with one litre of blood loss for which the patient was treated with 40 units syntocinon and 250 micrograms intramuscular, followed by 250 micrograms intravenous, ergometrine. Following the ergometrine infusion, the patient developed severe central chest pain with anterior ST elevation and inferior ST depression captured on electrocardiogram. Transthoracic echocardiogram demonstrated moderate to severe segmental impairment in LAD territory. Urgent invasive coronary angiogram illustrated an 80% proximal LAD obstruction which improved with glyceryl trinitrate administration to a residual 30% stenosis. Optical coherence tomography demonstrated fibrous plaque in the proximal LAD and confirmed a 30% stenosis. The findings were consistent with coronary vasospasm. Troponin T peaked at 4,533 ng/L. The patient was commenced on nifedipine, ramipril, aspirin and rosuvastatin. Follow-up echocardiography showed mild to moderate left ventricular impairment. Conclusion: The case above outlines the important but rare cause of coronary vasospasm in a patient with chest pain receiving ergometrine infusion.
