N2  - Importance: Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (HDP), gestational diabetes (GDM), and having a small for gestational age (SGA) baby increase a woman's risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD). Despite this, evidence for models of care that mitigate CVD risk is lacking. Objective: To assess the effectiveness of a Women's Heart Clinic (WHC) on blood pressure (BP) and lipid control. Method: A six-month prospective cohort study of women aged 30-55 years with a past pregnancy diagnosis of HDP, GDM or an SGA baby attended a multidisciplinary WHC in Melbourne, Australia. The co-primary endpoints were: 1) BP<140/90mmHg or <130/80mmHg if diabetes mellitus and 2) total cholesterol to high density lipoprotein-cholesterol ratio (TC:HDL-C)<4.5. Results: 156 women were recruited with a mean age of 41.0±4.2years, a mean of 3.9±2.9 years from last delivery with 23.1% past HDP, 60.3% GDM, 13.5% both HDP and GDM and, 3.2% with an SGA baby. The proportion meeting BP target increased (69.2% to 80.5%, p=0.004); no change in lipid targets (80.6% to 83.7%, p=0.182), and reductions in mean systolic BP(-6.9mmHg, 95% confidence interval (CI): -9.1,-4.7, p<0.001), body mass index (-0.6kg/m2, CI: -0.8,-0.3, p<0.001), LDL-cholesterol (0.1mmol/L, CI: 0-0.2, p=0.042) and total cholesterol (0.1mmol/L, CI: 0-0.2, p=0.042). Heart-healthy lifestyle improved: increased fish/olive oil (36.5% to 51.0%, p=0.012), decreased fast food (33.8% to 11.0%, p<0.001) consumption, improved exercise targets (84.0% to 92.9%, p=0.025). Conclusion: Women at high risk for CVD due to past pregnancy-related conditions experienced significant improvements in multiple cardiovascular risk factors, after attending a Women's Heart Clinic with potential improved long-term CVD outcomes.
