N2  - Background: Women with past pregnancy complications of gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (HDP) and/or having small for gestational age (SGA) babies have increased risk for cardiovascular disease (CVD). We investigated women's awareness of their CVD risk after a Women's Heart Clinic (WHC) and variables associated with higher CVD risk awareness. Methods: Women aged 30–55 years with past GDM, HDP and/or SGA babies were recruited into a 6-month prospective cohort study with a WHC intervention. A CVD Knowledge Survey was used to assess women's awareness of their CVD risk at baseline and 6-months following the WHC. McNemar's test was used to assess awareness of CVD risk before and after the WHC. Logistic regression was used to assess variables associated with improved understanding of CVD risk after the WHC. Results: 156 women (mean age 41.0±4.2 years) were recruited, with 23.1% HDP only, 60.3% GDM only, 13.5% both HDM/GDM, and 3.2% SGA baby. At baseline, 37.2% (95% CI: 29.6%-44.8%) of women had understood their CVD risk. CVD risk awareness improved significantly after the WHC (37.2% to 76.1%, p<0.001). Women who improved their CVD risk awareness were more likely to have private health insurance (OR 4.24, 95% CI 1.65-11.42; p=0.03), be overweight (body mass index>25) (OR 3.55 95% CI 1.52-8.80; p=0.004) and be diagnosed with non-gestational hypertension (OR 2.88, 94% CI 1.11-8.39; p=0.038). Conclusion: Only a third of women understood that past pregnancy diagnosis with HDP, GDM and/or SGA increased their CVD risk. CVD risk awareness significantly improved after attending a WHC.
