N2  - Background: Adverse pregnancy outcomes (APO) including gestational hypertension, preeclampsia, gestational diabetes and preterm delivery affects 5-20% of all pregnancies. Long-term consequences of APO include increased risk for developing CVD in midlife and 2-fold increase in the risk of a cardiovascular event within 10 years postpartum. Lifestyle behaviour change may mitigate risk. This study assessed CVD risk, knowledge/perceptions of risk, and lifestyle behaviours in females who have experienced APO. Methods: A nationwide online survey targeting premenopausal, biologically female, young adults (19-49y) collected sociodemographics, biometrics, medical history (including pregnancies), and CVD risk perceptions. Medical CVD risk was calculated from self-reported measures vis-a-vis the Comprehensive Health Improvement Program criteria. A lifestyle score was calculated as a composite of sleep, physical activity and other behaviours. Results: Of 1236 respondents 39.6% reported a previous APO. Compared with no-APO, the APO group showed significantly greater prevalence of being classified high risk for CVD (18 vs 10%), displayed lower knowledge of CVD risks (30±8 vs. 33±7), yet perceived themselves to be at greater risk of developing future CVD (24 vs 15%; p<0.001)). Groups did not differ by exercise status, BMI, nor sociodemographics. APO had a lower lifestyle score (4.4±1.7 vs. 4.7±1.8) with sleep (p=0.01) and stress (p=0.04) the distinguishing factors. Conclusions: A substantive proportion of our sample reported lived experience of an APO. The APO group have a higher perception of future CVD risk, but are less engaged in health-promoting behaviours that may mitigate their future risk. Effective, targeted health messaging for this high-risk population is warranted.
