N2  - Background: Emerging evidence has assigned an important role to sleep as a modulator of metabolic homeostasis. The impact of variations in sleep quality in cardiometabolic function encompasses a wide array of disorders spanning from obesity, insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome, and cardiovascular disease risk and mortality, in both adults and children. Yet little is known about the impact of sleep disturbances in overweight or obese children. Aim: The aim of this study was to characterize sleep in children with overweight or obesity and to examine the associations between child sleep and components of metabolic syndrome in overweight or obese children and adolescents. Methods: This cross-sectional study was conducted from June 2022 to August 2022. Overweight and obese children evaluated in Pediatrics consultations in our Center were included. The Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index questionnaire was applied to patients (or patient's parents/guardians). Sleep quality was classified as follows: 0-5=good sleep quality; 6-10=poor sleep quality; >10=sleep disorder. Additional data was collected from the clinical file. The data was treated by the software IBM SPSS Statistics 27 at a significance level of 5%. A multivariate analysis was performed with adjustment for gender, age, history of gestational diabetes, maternal and paternal education, weeks of gestation at birth and whether the child was breastfed. Results: Sixty-nine patients were included (BMI: 29.9±6.3Kg/m2; age: 12.4±3.3years), of which 44.9%% were female. This last group had higher global Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index score (11.5±7.3 vs. 10.9±6.8, p=0.013. Twenty-eight percent of children met criteria for poor sleep quality and 47.1% of sleep disorder. There was a positive correlation between both BMI (r=0.305; p=0.001) and Z-score of BMI (r=0.269, p= 0.025) and sleep score quality but this association was no longer observed after adjusting for confounders (β=0.401, p=0.078 and β=4.456, p=0.078, respectively). A higher level of maternal education and age was inversely associated with worse sleep quality (p=0.013 and 0.039 respectively). No relationship was found between sleep quality and lipid profile, hepatic steatosis, blood pressure or A1c. Conclusion: Poor sleep quality may be inversely related to BMI, however, the small size of the sample and the fact that normoweight children were not included may have meant that significant differences were not obtained between the groups. It seems that the confounding factors were more correlated with the sleep quality score than the metabolic parameters, since adjusting for them does not show a significant association between BMI and the sleep quality score. Further studies are needed to assess sleep quality among children with and without obesity, in a population with more patients and more uniform ages.
