N2  - Acute pancreatitis is a devastating complication in pregnancy with an incidence of 1 in 1000-10,000 and occurs most commonly in third trimester. It is associated with high maternal and fetal morbidity and mortality. We report a case of hypertriglyceridemia induced acute pancreatitis in a pregnant woman and successful outcome following plasmapheresis. A 26-year-old gravida 1, para 0 presented to the hospital with low grade fever, abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting at 31 weeks gestation. She was found to be tachycardic and febrile on exam with epigastric tenderness. Patient's history was pertinent for hypertriglyceridemia and pregnancy has been complicated by gestational diabetes. There was an initial concern for pre-term labor and received iv magnesium sulfate for neurophrophylaxis along with a dose of betamethasone to promote fetal lung maturity. Labs were remarkable for elevated white count 13.8 10A9/L, hyperglycemia with glucose level of 242 mg/dL, metabolic acidosis with HC03 of 14 mmol/L, high anion gap of 19 mmol/L, elevated lipase at 1,101 IU/L, ALP 155 units/L. RUQ ultrasound did not show gallstones. Due to severity of pain, a general surgery consultation was obtained. No surgical intervention was recommended. Lipid profile was obtained that revealed significantly elevated serum triglyceride (TG) level at 4,071 mg/dL suggestive of hypertriglyceridemia induced pancreatitis. She was treated with IV insulin along with IV fluids, low fat diet, therapeutic plasma exchange, Omega-3 fatty acids and fenofibrate with improvement in triglyceride levels to <500 upon discharge. Fetal status was reassuring throughout with normal growth, BPP, NST and patient delivered at term. Hypertriglyceridemia induced acute pancreatitis is a challenging complication of pregnancy as it can be complicated by obstetrical emergencies. Clinicians should be cognizant of this rare etiology as delaying diagnosis could Jeopardize fetal outcomes. Plasmapheresis is an effective therapeutic option for rapidly lowering TG in such cases like our patient. The indications of plasmapheresis include patient refractory to nutritional and pharmacological approaches, serum TG > 1000 mg/dL, serum lipase 3 x ULN, severe hypocalcemia, lactic acidosis, and worsening inflammation and organ dysfunction.
