N2  - Background Autoimmune connective tissue diseases (CTD) predominantly affect women during their reproductive years. Maternal complications are often considered risk factors for future cardiovascular disease. CTDs such as systemic lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, and systemic sclerosis has been associated with poor maternal and fetal complications. We aim to evaluate pregnancy outcomes in CTD cohort compared to a cohort without CTD. Methods We conducted a population-based, retrospective cohort study using the national inpatient data sample to evaluate pregnancy and fetal outcomes in women with CTD, compared to those without CTD, delivering between 2015 and 2018. Regression analysis was performed and adjusted for multiple patient characteristics to compare maternal and fetal outcomes. Results Of the total 864,654 births in our cohort, 995 were to women with CTD. Table 1 highlights differences between both cohorts. Women with CTD were more likely to develop preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, placental abruption, chorioamnionitis, need labor induction, preterm labor, fetal distress, and post-partum hemorrhage. These findings were not significant when we adjusted for other variables. Total cost and length of stay were higher in CTD patients but were not statistically significant. Conclusion Women with CTD experience higher rates of maternal complications likely related to other confounders. Preconception counseling should take these risks into consideration. [Formula presented]
