N2  - Background Data on ECMO in the peripartum period is limited. We present a case of a patient with cardiogenic shock immediately post delivery, likely due to TCM, with successful recovery on V-A ECMO. Case A 38-year-old South Asian G1P0 woman, with a history of hypothyroidism and gestational diabetes presented at 36 weeks gestation for induction of labor in the setting of cholestasis. Labor course was complicated by chorioamnionitis, treated with intravenous antibiotics. She underwent successful normal spontaneous vaginal delivery of a healthy baby boy, but within hours of delivery, developed progressive dyspnea with escalating oxygen requirements. Echocardiogram demonstrated globally reduced left ventricular systolic function with apical hyperkinesis and a left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF) of 15%. Labs were notable for a troponin I of 17.8 ng/mL (0-0.04 ng/mL), BNP 1,470 pg/mL (<100 pg/mL), lactate 2.8 mmol/L (<2 mmol/L), and white blood cell count 39.9 K/uL (4.5-11 K/uL). Invasive coronary angiography was normal. Right heart catheterization demonstrated elevated biventricular filling pressures and mildly reduced cardiac index on high dose inotropes, so an intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) was placed. Given progressive hemodynamic instability, she was placed on V-A ECMO. She was decannulated from ECMO after four days and IABP was removed four days later. Echocardiogram one week after presentation showed significant LVEF recovery of 44%. She was initiated on low dose beta blockade and discharged two weeks after initial presentation. Echocardiogram at two months showed complete recovery with LVEF 57%. The patient opted against breastfeeding. Subsequent genetic testing was negative for inherited cardiomyopathies. Decision-making In this case, the presence of apical hyperkinesis with severe basal segment hypokinesis, along with rapid recovery in her LVEF, is suggestive of a reverse TCM phenotype. The patient responded clinically to early mechanical circulatory support (MCS). Conclusion Cardiogenic shock, regardless of etiology, in the peripartum period requires prompt recognition and action, including early multidisciplinary cardio-obstetric management.
