N2  - Background Gestational diabetes (GDM) and gestational hypertension (GH) are risk indicators for cardiovascular disease (CVD) in women [1,2]. Current evidence forces ‘ever GDM/never GDM’ and ‘ever GH/never GH’ dichotomies when incorporating pregnancy histories into CVD risk assessment. Aim We are examining GDM and GH history across two singleton pregnancies in relationship to the development of myocardial infarction, stroke, and hospitalized angina. Method Retrospective cohort study in Quebec, Canada. Administrative data includes International Disease Classification-coded outpatient and inpatient diagnoses. Birth registry includes offspring sex, gestational age, birth weight, and maternal and paternal countries of birth and languages spoken. We are analyzing data from women with two consecutive singleton deliveries from April 1990 to December 2012, with follow-up data to April 2019. We are examining associations between all possible combinations of GDM and GH across two pregnancies (16 categories) using multivariable Cox regression models. The reference group is mothers without GDM or GH in either pregnancy. Confidentiality rules in Quebec require rounded values for frequency measures with rounding at the unit level only at time of submission for final publication. We present here approximate values for frequencies and overall trends observed. We will provide more specific values at the time of presentation. Results Among over 430,000 mothers, over 4,000 CVD events occurred. The highest hazards for CVD occurred in those with both GDM and GH in both pregnancies, with a hazard ratio of greater than 4. Various combinations of 3 diagnoses of GDM and/or GH across two pregnancies were associated with hazard ratios that ranged between 2.5 and 3.8. With two diagnoses of GDM and/or GH, there was an approximately a 2-fold increase in CVD hazards. With a single diagnosis of GDM or GH, the increase in hazards ranged between 40 and 60%. Conclusion The cumulative number of GDM and GH diagnoses across two singleton pregnancies can provide more precise estimates of CVD hazards than simple dichotomies of ever/never GDM or GH.
