N2  - Introduction: Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is a common maternal condition associated with a higher incidence of hypertensive disorders, lipid metabolic abnormalities and puerperal infection. In addition, GDM signifcantly increases the risks of excess fat accretion, obesity and glucose intolerance in ofspring. Although GDM is known to alter glucose metabolism, little is known about lipid metabolism. This study aims to test whether insulin resistance (i.e., reduced insulin receptor signaling), the key feature and main cause of GDM, alters maternal blood pressure and placental n-6 and n-3 long-chain polyunsaturated fatty acids (LCPUFA) metabolism and transport. Further, the molecula basis of these alterations was assessed by examining the placental expression of genes of fatty acid oxidation and transport. A novel rat model of GDM generated by administration of an insulin receptor antagonist to pregnant rats was used for this study. Methods: Pregnant Sprague Dawley rats were administered with S961, an insulin receptor antagonist (30 nmol/kg s.c. daily), or vehicle from gestational day (GD) 7 to 20. Daily maternal body weight, food, and water intake were measured. Blood pressure assessment and glucose tolerance test were done on GD 20. Placenta and fetal plasma were collected on GD 20 and processed for measurement of LCPUFA using LC-mass spectrometry. Assessment of expression of genes related to lipid metabolism and transport was done in the placenta using RT2 Profiler PCR arrays. Results: Blockade of insulin action with S961 in pregnant rats signifcantly increased maternal blood pressure and heart rate. S961 treatment did not alter maternal body weight gain and food and water intake but induced glucose intolerance with signifcant increases in fasting glucose and insulin levels. The placenta concentrations of n3 and n6 LCPUFA were signifcantly decreased by 6% and 13%, respectively, while their fetal plasma levels were signifcantly increased by 15% and 5% in rats with blockade of insulin action. Placental expression of genes related to fatty acid β-oxidation (medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase, Acyl-CoA Thioesterase 2, Acyl-CoA Synthetase Bubblegum Family Member 1, and Carnitine Palmitoyltransferase 1B) and transport (fatty acid binding protein 3 and Solute Carrier Family 27 Member 3) were signifcantly upregulated in S961 treated rats. Conclusion: In summary, this study indicates that insulin resistance during pregnancy induces hypertension and impairment of placental metabolism with increased β-oxidation and transplacental transport of n3 and n6 LCPUFA to the fetus. The increased transplacental transport of fatty acids to the fetus could contribute to fetal fat accretion and metabolic dysfunction in the ofspring.
