N2  - Introduction: Little is known on how Adverse Childhood Experiences (ACE) afect pregnancy and perinatal outcomes. This is particulaly crucial for pregnant patients with cardiac disease, in whom addressing stressors and decreasing heart strain is essential for improved outcomes. Therefore, we sought to characterize perinatal outcomes in patients with cardiac disease and ACEs. Methods: This was a prospective cohort of pregnant patients with cardiac disease who delivered at a single institution between 2012-2022. Perinatal data from their first recorded pregnancy were abstracted from medical records. Validated ACE questionnaires, which include categories in the realms of abuse, neglect and household dysfunction before the age of 18, were sent. ACE scores were compared to perinatal outcomes including preeclampsia, gestational diabetes (GDM), fetal growth restriction (FGR), intrauterine fetal death (IUFD), preterm delivery, APGAR score and birthweight. Logistic and linear regression models were used to quantify odds ratios for categorical and continuous outcomes respectively. Regressions were adjusted for body mass index (BMI), a potential confounder. A p value of <0.05 was statistically signifcant. Results: Out of 326 patients who met inclusion criteria, 82 completed the ACE questionnaire. Of those, 50% reported experiencing ACEs. Demographics, prior medical history and baseline cardiac disease severity by modifIed WHO were similar in patients with and without ACEs (Table 1). After adjusting for BMI, for each additional ACE, there was an increased odds of FGR (aOR: 1.36; CI: 1.01-1.86; P=0.04) (Table 2). Moreover, there was one IUFD in a patient who reported ACEs. Conclusion: In our study, 50% of cardiac patients experienced an ACE, less than the national average of two-thirds. Greater number of ACEs was associated with FGR. This study brings to light the need for further research on large populations to uncover the true impact of ACEs on pregnancies in cardiac patients. [Figure Presented].
