N2  - Introduction: Vitamin B12 defciency in early pregnancy is related to adverse lipid profile and metabolic disorders such as fetal growth restriction, low fetal weight and preterm birth, and obesity, dyslipidemia and gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) in the mothers. Previous studies suggest potential effects of B12 defciency on placental development and function which could lead to impaired fetal growth. However, the direct effects of B12 on trophoblast is unknown. This study aimed to explore the effect of defcient or sufcient B12 on trophoblast turnover and endocrine function. Methods: Villous explants (n=10) from uncomplicated pregnancies were treated with Control (B12-500nM) or low B12 (25pM) concentration and cultured for 7 days under 21% O2. Conditioned culture medium was collected at day 4 and day 7 when the placental explants were harvested for whole protein measurement or immunochemistry staining. Human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) secretion and human placental lactogen (hPL) secretion were measured in the conditioned culture medium by ELISA to assess syncytiotrophoblast differentiation, and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) release was determined to assess necrosis. Immunohistochemical staining of Ki67 and M30 was performed to measure the number of cells in cycle and apoptotic cells. Results: hCG secretion was comparable between B12 defcient and sufcient group at both time points day 4 and day 7. The secretion of hPL decreased signifcantly from day 4 to day 7 in both treatment groups (day 4 vs day 7: Control, median ± IQR, 89.23±43.38 vs 63.55±25.00, (P=0.002), low B12, median ± IQR, 117.9±84.67 vs 71.91±58.22, (P=0.027)), but there was no signifcant effect of B12 defciency or at day 4 or day 7. LDH release was higher in B12 defciency at day 4, but no alteration was found at day 7. The rate of cell numbers in cycle or apoptotic cells were comparable between B12 defcient or sufcient group. Conclusion: These data demonstrate that vitamin B12 levels do not have a signifcant impact on trophoblast proliferation, apoptosis or endocrine function. However, to understand the relationship between B12 defciency and impaired fetal growth, metabolic pathways afecting placental dysfunction and their underlying epigenetic mechanisms should be explored in future.
