N2  - Background: Hypergonadism and infertility are characteristic of Turner Syndrome (TS). Pregnancies in TS patients are considered high risk. Methods: Retrospective audit of medical records of 87 TS patients attending a single tertiary centre from 2010 to 2022. Karyotype, medical and reproductive data were collected. Antenatal care was compared to international TS recommendations 1. Results: Identified karyotypes were 45,X (31/87) and mosaic (56/87). Spontaneous menarche occurred in 6.7% (2/30) and 36.3% (20/55) of 45,X and mosaic TS respectively. Primary amenorrhoea affected 74.1% (63/85) of TS patients overall. 25 pregnancies occurred in 13 patients, resulting in 16 live births; 10/25 occurred spontaneously (only mosaic TS) and 15/25 required ART. No patients had contraindications to pregnancy. One woman had pre-existing hypertension and one developed T2DM interpregnancy. All women had an echocardiogram ≥2 years pre-pregnancy and 62.5% during pregnancy. All women had a 28-week OGTT and 50% had an early OGTT. Increase in Aortic Size Index was observed in two thirds of women with baseline and antenatal echocardiograms but remained ≥1.79 cm/m2 (normal <2.1 cm/m 2 ). Gestational diabetes and preeclampsia occurred in 22% and 14% of pregnancies respectively. Mean(SD) delivery gestation was 37 ± 3.3 completed weeks. Preterm delivery occurred in 22.2% pregnancies. Two thirds of primiparous patients delivered by Caesarean section. One third of neonates were small for gestational age. Conclusions: Mosaic karyotype is associated with spontaneous menarche and pregnancy. Deficiencies in antenatal cardiac and diabetes screening recommendations were observed. TS women have higher rates of perinatal complications compared with the background population. These findings highlight the need for increased awareness and multi-disciplinary antenatal care for TS women.
