N2  - Objective: Little is known about pregnancy complications and cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk among ethnic minority populations. In this study we examined the association between pregnancy complications and CVD risk factors in women from six ethnic groups, and the potential value of pregnancy complications as an eligibility criterion for CVD risk screening in addition to traditional risk factors. Study Design: We created a retrospective cohort by combining pregnancy data from the Dutch perinatal registry with baseline data on cardiovascular risk from the HELIUS study. We included 2,466 women who participated in the HELIUS study with at least one pregnancy between 2000 and baseline measurement (2011-2015). Poisson regression analyses were conducted, adjusted for confounding factors and traditional eligibility criteria for CVD risk screening. Results: History of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (HDP) and preterm birth (PTB) were associated with higher prevalence of chronic hypertension, chronic kidney disease and high metabolic risk across most groups (Figure 1). Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) was associated with increased type 2 diabetes mellitus risk (Figure 2). We found no evidence for interaction between ethnicity and pregnancy complications. However, absolute rates of pregnancy complications and cardiovascular risk factors were higher among Surinamese and Ghanaian women compared to Dutch origin women. The prediction models did not improve substantially after adding pregnancy complications to the traditional eligibility criteria for CVD risk screening. Conclusion: History of HDP, PTB and GDM is equally associated with increased CVD risk in parous women across ethnic groups, but absolute risks vary. This warrants extra attention for the cardiovascular risk profile among these groups before and after pregnancy. Addition of pregnancy complications to traditional eligibility criteria for CVD risk screening does not substantially improve the prediction of CVD risk factors if uptake of protocolised screening is high. However, in absence of such screening, pregnancy complications may serve as a red flag. [Formula presented] [Formula presented]
