N2  - Objective: As a result of improved childhood survival, more patients with congenital heart disease (CHD) are reaching reproductive age, leading to a growing high risk obstetric population. Though much knowledge has been gained in this cohort, less is known about their offspring compared to other cardiac populations. In this study, we sought to determine the fetal and neonatal outcomes from pregnancies complicated with maternal CHD in comparison to acquired heart disease. Study Design: We studied a retrospective cohort of patients with cardiac disease that delivered between 2012-2022 (N=384) at a single tertiary care institution. Neonatal outcomes were compared between pregnancies complicated by CHD (N=190) and acquired heart disease (N=194) using logistic and linear regressions. The primary outcome was neonatal death. Multivariate regression was used to adjust for possible preset confounders such as chronic hypertension (cHTN), and pre-gestational diabetes. A P value of < 0.05 indicated statistical significance. Results: Patients with CHD had lower BMI, were younger, were more likely to have had open heart surgery and less likely to have cHTN in our cohort (Table 1). Six patients opted for termination (4 in CHD group). Of the continued pregnancies, patients with CHD had lower birthweights than patients with acquired heart disease (Table 2). Notably, there were 5 neonatal deaths in the CHD group. These were associated with extreme prematurity (2) and/or congenital malformations (4), three of which were detected postnatally. Conclusion: In our study, pregnancies afflicted by CHD had lower birthweights despite similar rates of prematurity. This highlights the need for future studies to add focus to neonatal outcomes in pregnancies complicated by CHD as well as risk stratification between types of heart disease. Management should include preconception counseling, access to timely termination and prenatal assessments with a multidisciplinary cardio-obstetric team and a dedicated neonatal support for optimal care of this high-risk population. [Formula presented] [Formula presented]
