N2  - Background: To assess umbilical cord coiling index as a marker of perinatal outcome. Materials and Methods: One hundred fifty antenatal women who went into labour were included. Umbilical coiling index (UCI) calculated as total number of complete vascular coiling/ total length of cord (cm) at the time of delivery. Based on UCI, three groups as normocoiled (10th- 90th percentile of the mean UCI), hypocoiled (UCI < 10th percentile) and hypercoiled (90th percentile of the mean) in group I, II and III respectively was formed. Result: The mean length of coil was 51.6 cm. The mean number of coil was 12.3 and UCI was 0.29 cm. Vaginal delivery was seen in 40, 34 and 30 and caesarean in 10, 16 and 20 in group I, II and III respectively. Heart rate was normal in 42, 36 and 38 and abnormal in 8, 14 and 12 in group I, II and III respectively. Birth weight was normal in 43, 42 and 40 and low in 7, 8 and 10 in 8, 14 and 12 in group I, II and III respectively. APGAR score was normal in 41, 38 and 35 and low in 9, 12 and 15 in group I, II and III respectively. The difference was significant (P< 0.05). Complications was GDM seen in 5, 3 and 2 and PIH in 2, 4 and 1 in group I, II and III respectively. Abruption was seen in 10, 6 and 8 and Oligohydramnios was seen in 3, 2 and 3 in group I, II and III respectively. The difference was significant (P< 0.05). Conclusion: Abnormal umbilical coiling index was associated with several adverse antenatal and neonatal features.
