N2  - Interconception Care is Care provided to mothers between pregnancies to improve health outcomes for patients (and their infants). It is important to view postpartum care and preconception care as a continuum, and emphasizes the evaluation of a patients health through a life course perspective that acknowledges the influence of pregnancy on health trajectory. Women with adverse pregnancy outcomes (APO) such as hypertensive disordes of pregnancy, gestational diabetes, preterm delivery, small for gestational age, large for gestational age, placental abruption, and miscarriages, stillbirths, are at high risk for future development of chronic diseases or Atherosclerotic CVD. Some guidelines recommend that health care providers inform women affected by APO of the risks of future disease and instruct them to maintain a healthy lifestyle postpartum. Referral to an appropriate health provider is recommended, too. However, a clear follow-up system for these women has not yet been established. One reason for this is the lack of evidence for risk stratification and corresponding management during the interconception period, taking into account the next pregnancy. There might also be a lack of awareness among health care providers (especially primary care physicians or internists) about these women. Comprehensive care is needed for postpartum women including prevention and management of chronic disease, pregnancy history, family planning, preconception counseling, postpartum weight management, and postpartum mental health care. This presentation will present the risk of future development of chronic disease in women affected by hypertensive disorders of pregnancy from our cohort study, and will discuss the management of women during the interconception period.
