N2  - Purpose of Study: This study examines delivery complications in morbidly obese women to better prepare obstetric staff for adverse delivery outcomes. Obesity is now considered a global pandemic with a general increase in prevalence in adults, adolescents, children, and women of reproductive age. It is well established that obesity is associated with increased morbidity, including diabetes mellitus, hypertension, chronic kidney disease, heart disease, stroke, sleep apnea, and cancer. Obesity is associated with serious consequences for pregnant women and their children in all stages of pregnancy. Obese pregnant women are at greater risk of multiple adverse maternal-fetal outcomes than their normal body mass index (BMI) counterparts, including gestational diabetes mellitus, hypertensive disorders, birth trauma, fetal malformation, and emergent cesarean delivery. This study examines the relationship between BMI and cesarean section operative times, epidural times, and the amount of blood loss during delivery. Methods Used: Deliveries (n = 3066) that occurred in 2019 at Loma Linda University Medical Center were analyzed with respect to antepartum, labor, and delivery outcomes. Patients were divided into categories based on BMI; underweight and normal (BMI <24.9), overweight (BMI 25-25.9), class I obesity (BMI 30-34.9), class II obesity (BMI 35-39.5), and class III obesity (BMI >40). Chi-square tests were conducted and odds ratios were calculated to determine how outcomes varied by mother's BMI. Summary of Results: Cesarean section operative times of patients with Class I, II, and III obesity were each significantly longer than that of patients classified as overweight (p<.0001). Patients with class III obesity also had the greatest number of unscheduled cesarean sections. Epidural placement took significantly more time for Class I and II obesity groups compared to the overweight group (p<.0001). Compared to the overweight group, all classes of obesity had significantly greater estimated blood loss during vaginal and cesarean deliveries (p<.0001). All analyses controlled for age, gravidity, and ethnicity. Conclusions: Our analysis concluded that patients with class III obesity have longer cesarean section operative times, greater rates of unscheduled cesarean sections, longer epidural times, and greater amounts of blood loss compared to their lower BMI counterparts. Implications of these findings include pre-procedure planning on the part of obstetric and anesthesia teams to ensure patient safety and treatment efficiency. Areas of future research include investigating the impact of BMI on other obstetric outcomes, such as breastfeeding rates at delivery and long term.
