N2  - Background: Thyroid disorders are common chronic pathologies in women of reproductive age. Maternal, fetal, and neonatal complications associated with these disorders have been detected over the past and justify the need for therapeutic management, including levothyroxine. Objective: To identify the risks associated with thyroid disorders and levothyroxine gestational exposure, for the woman and her child. Methods: We conducted a literature review to synthesize current evidence on thyroid disorders and levothyroxine use during pregnancy, using PubMed. We retrieved and examined articles about these disorders and their management in pregnant women, using “thyroid disorders, pregnancy, levothyroxine, perinatal and pediatric outcomes” as keywords. We summarized thyroid diseases and their prevalence in pregnant women and described their associated risks on pregnancy. Benefits and adverse effects of levothyroxine use for hypothyroidism during pregnancy on maternal and pediatric outcomes evaluated in potentially relevant studies were reported. We identified the knowledge gaps regarding the use of levothyroxine during pregnancy. Results: Thyroid disease refers to a spectrum of endocrinology disorders. These disorders include subclinical hypothyroidism, which is the most prevalent among pregnant women (2-5%), overt hypothyroidism (0.2-1%), overt hyperthyroidism (0.2-0.7%) and subclinical hyperthyroidism (0.8-1.7%). Suboptimal treatment of hyperthyroidism was consistently associated with preeclampsia and prematurity. Furthermore, studies estimate a 60% risk of pregnancy loss in overt hypothyroidism and 30% in subclinical hypothyroidism. Management of hypothyroidism during pregnancy remains controversial: levothyroxine has been shown to significantly reduce the risk of pregnancy loss, preterm delivery, and gestational hypertension in some studies, but it increased the risk of gestational diabetes in others. The disparity in the results of these studies was partly explained by variabilities in diagnostic criteria, dose prescribed, and time of therapy initiation. Besides, no positive effect on fetal neurocognitive development, such as IQ improvement, was observed after levothyroxine initiation at the end of the first trimester, in hypothyroidism-diagnosed women: intervention timing might be too late to significantly impact neurodevelopment. Conclusion: Further studies evaluating the impact associated with levothyroxine on perinatal complications and neurocognitive function in children, considering the severity of hypothyroidism, dose adjustment, and therapy initiation moment, are still needed.
