N2  - INTRODUCTION: Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is defined as glucose intolerance with onset during pregnancy and resolving after birth. GDM is known to be associated with an increased risk of adverse outcomes during pregnancy and birth, and there is consistent evidence to suggest a strong association between GDM and progression to T2DM later in life. However, the risks of developing other health conditions and the healthcare usage and costs of caring for GDM women (beyond pregnancy) are not known. In this project we will quantify the long-term health risks of GDM and estimate the economic and healthcare costs attributed to GDM. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Using QResearch database, we will extract electronic primary care patient records linked with hospital episode statistics (HES), of women with at least one singleton delivery between 1 January 2000 and 7 August 2021. Exposure will be diagnosis of GDM in any pregnancy. Cases will be matched with up to four control records of women who have never received a diagnosis of GDM, matched on maternal age and delivery (+/-3 mo). Women will be followed-up from the delivery until an outcome of interest, death or transfer out of the practice that shares data. We will analyse the risk GDM poses for a range of clinical conditions e.g type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, depression, cancer. We will also extract information on the total number and type of consultations, prescriptions, diagnostic tests and treatments to estimate healthcare costs attributed to GDM. RESULTS: We have identified records of 49,477 women with GDM, and these were matched with 170,335 unique maternal records, making-up the control group. Women who received a diagnosis of GDM had higher pre-pregnancy BMI, and a greater proportion reported to be Asian, African or Chinese backgrounds compared with the control women. Analysis on clinical outcomes is currently in progress and preliminary results will be presented. CONCLUSION: The results will provide information which is important to women who are pregnant or planning pregnancy and their families, it will inform the development of clinical guidelines and help commissioners to judge the cost-effectiveness of potential new pathways of care which may help prevent and treat GDM more effectively.
