N2  - Background: There is increasing need for multidisciplinary cardio-obstetrics programs to care for pregnant women with high-risk cardiovascular disease; however, relatively few descriptions of these programs exist. Methods: We identified patients managed by our multidisciplinary high-risk cardio-obstetrics team from July 2017 to December 2020. We collected data on baseline characteristics, diagnostic testing, antepartum and intrapartum history, labor and delivery and post-partum outcomes. Results: A total of 172 women with 188 pregnancies were included. The median age was 31 years; of these, 27.5% were advanced maternal age (≥ 35 years old). The cardiac comorbidity was known prior to pregnancy in 175 pregnancies (93.1%). The most common cardiac conditions were congenital heart disease (30.3%), heart failure/cardiomyopathy (20.2%), arrhythmia (17%), valvular disease (11.1%), and hypertensive disorder (9%). Over half (55.3%) were on cardiac medications during pregnancy, most commonly beta blockers. Most pregnancies (94.1%) ended in live birth, with 77.7% delivered full-term (≥ 37 weeks gestational age), and nearly half were cesarean deliveries (48%). Maternal complications included postpartum hemorrhage (10.1%), pre-eclampsia (7.4%), gestational diabetes (5.9%), and ICU admission (3.2%). There were 20 (10.6%) readmissions at 12 months postpartum, 65% of which occurred within 30 days. The most common reasons for readmission were infection, high blood pressure, and heart failure. There were no maternal deaths. Conclusion: In a single-center experience, the presence of a cardio-obstetrics program resulted in low rates of adverse maternal outcomes in patients at high risk for cardiac complication.
