N2  - Background: The association between pre-gestational maternal diabetes (MD) and an elevated risk of congenital heart disease (CHD) is well-recognized. MD accounts for a significant and growing burden of fetal echo referrals, yet our recent province-wide study found that major CHD (mCHD), defined as requiring an operation in the first year of life, was rarely diagnosed in mothers referred solely for MD. The aim of this study was to refine criteria for fetal echo referral. We therefore determined the incidence of mCHD among those with MD in comparison to gestational diabetes and non-diabetic controls, and adjusted for the effect of covariates and stratified by levels of glycemic control. Methods: Using surgical and fetal cardiology records, we determined the incidence of mCHD among pregnancies in Alberta from 2008-2018. We linked data including presence of MD or gestational diabetes in all provincial births from the Alberta Perinatal Health Program registry with maximum hemoglobin A1c level (as an indicator of glycemic control) prior to 16 weeks gestation and other potential covariates. Results: There were 1405 cases of mCHD in 594,755 Alberta births in the study period (2.3/1000). mCHD was present in 48/7449 births with MD (6.4/1000). The risk ratio for mCHD associated with MD was 2.79 (95% CI 2.1, 3.72) (p<0.001). mCHD was present in 95/39,141 births with gestational diabetes (2.4/1000 births, risk ratio 1.03 (95% CI 0.84, 1.27) (p=0.73). Maximum A1c at <16 weeks gestation was higher in cases of MD with mCHD than MD without mCHD (mCHD 8.5% (IQR 6.9, 10.2); no mCHD 7.3% (IQR 5.9, 8.2), p=0.001). The stratified risks for mCHD associated with A1c ≤6.1%, 6.1% - 8.4% and ≥8.5% were 3.6/1000, 7.7/1000 and 20.9/1000 births respectively. The risk ratio for mCHD associated with MD and an A1c ≥8.5% was therefore 8.9 (95% CI 5.2, 15.3), and 9.9 (95% CI 5.6, 16.7) when adjusted for multiple gestations, child&apos;s gender, maternal pre-pregnancy weight and age. Conclusion: Pre-existing maternal diabetes is associated with a risk ratio for mCHD of 2.8, which increases to 9.9 in those with HbA1c of >8.5%. This data may allow refinement of referral indications for high-risk pregnancy screening.
