N2  - Background Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) increases the risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD) in the years after pregnancy. Proactively addressing CVD risks in women with a history of GDM can improve long term CV mortality and morbidity. Methods In a retrospective case-control study ending in 2013 we compared the risks of CV outcomes over a 15-year period in pregnant women with GDM who had a parity of one and had a live birth, to pregnant primiparous women who were not diagnosed with GDM and who had a live birth (controls). Women with a prior history of diabetes mellitus, myocardial infarction (MI), stroke or heart failure (HF) were excluded. The New Jersey Electronic Birth Certificate and Perinatal database and the Myocardial Infarction Data Acquisition System were used to conduct the analysis. Pregnant women 18 to 54 years old without a prior history of MI, HF, stroke, or hypertension were included. Results First time pregnant women diagnosed with GDM had a significantly higher risk of stroke and CVD death over this fifteen-year study period compared to controls as follows: 0.15% vs. 0.07%, p=0.002; and CV death 0.04% vs. 0.02%, p=0.013. Conclusion This study indicates that women with GDM in their first pregnancy have a significantly higher risk of adverse CV outcomes compared to women without GDM. Patient education and routine incorporation of CVD surveillance and care in this population are indicated to reduce future adverse CV events in this population. [Formula presented]
