N2  - Introduction and aims: Low to moderate intensity physical exercise is recommended in uncomplicated pregnancies and in women with Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM). Aim of the present study is to investigate the effect of physical exercise on maternal and fetal circulation in uncomplicated and GDM pregnancies. Methods: 21 women with uncomplicated pregnancies and 21 women with GDM between the 27th and 36th gestational weeks were included in the study. All women underwent 30 minutes of low to moderate intensity cycling at 60-70% of the estimated maximal heart rate. In all women the pulsatility index of the Middle Cerebral Artery (MCA), Umbilical Artery (UmA) and Uterine Arteries (UtA) were estimated before and immediately after the physical exercise. Results: The average maternal age in uncomplicated and GDM pregnancies were 31.2 ± 5.1 and 31.6 ± 5.4 respectively (p>0.05). The average gestational age in uncomplicated and GDM pregnancies were 32.1 ± 4.6 and 31.6 ± 5.1 respectively (p>0.05). The average BMI in uncomplicated and GDM pregnancies were 27.9 ± 5.3 and 28.4 ± 4.8 respectively (p>0.05). Before the physical exercise there was no statistically difference between the two groups regarding the pulsatility index of MCA, UmA and UtA (p>0.05). After the physical exercise, there was a statistically significant difference in the pulsatility index of the UtA between the uncomplicated 0.83 ± 0.2 and the GDM pregnancies 0.57 ± 0.1 and (p<0.05). After the physical exercise, there was no statistically significant difference in the pulsatility index of the MCA and UmA between the groups. Conclusions: The physical exercise led to decreased pulsatility index of UtA in the GDM pregnancies, compared to uncomplicated pregnancies.
