N2  - Background: Chromosome microarray technology has disclosed an increasing number of submicroscopic copy number variants for which interpretation of pathogenicity can be difficult. One useful approach is comparison of phenotypic findings among individuals with similar genotypes, both within families and in unrelated individuals. Delineation of pathogenicity can pose a difficult challenge when information on a given submicroscopic copy number variant is scarce, or conflicting. Certain cytogenetic locations appear prone to copy number variation, considered to be mediated by flanking highly homologous duplicated regions. This context generates a predisposition to non-allelic homologous recombination with potential to result in either genomic gains or losses. Chromosome 2q13 is one such location, with reported 1.7 Mb copy number variants including both gains and losses. Previous reports of a recurrent 2q13 microdeletion syndrome have yielded variable and nonspecific phenotypic data. Features reported include autism spectrum disorder, developmental delay, congenital heart disease, dysmorphic craniofacial features, and hypotonia. We describe two siblings with 2q13 microdeletion and varied clinical phenotypes, review the literature (15 papers, 54 subjects, nine heterozygote parents), and collate existing data on this condition. Case presentation: Case 1: A male infant was born at 36 weeks gestation to a 34 year old mother via cesarean-section for maternal hypertension. The birth weight was 2296 grams (2.5th percentile) and length 44.5 cm (2nd percentile). There was limited prenatal care during the pregnancy, which was complicated by maternal hepatitis C and syphilis, as well as intrauterine exposure to heroin, methadone, and methamphetamine. At birth, the infant required NICU admission for withdrawal symptoms. While admitted, he had a positive lumbar puncture for syphilis, which was treated with antibiotics. An echocardiogram performed to evaluate a heart murmur at two months of age showed a small atrial septal defect and mild pulmonary valve stenosis. At 7 months of age, he was noted to have developmental delay, nystagmus, pigmentary retinopathy, dysmorphic craniofacial features (frontal bossing, ocular hypertelorism) and hypotonia, prompting referral for genetics evaluation. Chromosomal microarray was obtained and showed a deletion of 2q13 (111398336_113101219 x1). Case 2 (Older sister of case 1): A female infant was born at 29 weeks via cesarean-section to a then 30 year old mother. Pregnancy was complicated by gestational diabetes. An echocardiogram to evaluate a congenital heart murmur showed patent foramen ovale, atrial septal defect, and small aorto-pulmonary collateral vessel. Dysmorphic craniofacial features were observed including ocular hypertelorism, bulbous nasal tip. Additional phenotypic features include developmental delay and antalgic gait. Chromosomal microarray showed the same 2q13 deletion as her brother. We reviewed the medical literature and identified 15 papers describing 63 examples of 2q13 deletion, including 54 pediatric subjects and nine parents. The most common features were facial dysmorphism, cardiac defects, and neurocognitive delays. Conclusion: It is apparent that some regions of the human genome are prone to recurrent copy number variations. Possession of accurate phenotypic data is important to aid with management and genetic counseling when such copy number variants are found. Detailed phenotypic descriptions of chromosome 2q13 microdeletion are relatively rarely reported. Here, we describe an example of intrafamilial variability and review the available literature on this disorder. Variable penetrance and/or expressivity appears to characterize this microdeletion syndrome, the molecular bases for which will require further research.
