N2  - Objective: Villitis of unknown etiology (VUE) is an inflammatory placental condition characterized by maternal CD8+ T lymphocyte infiltration into the fetal villi. It has been linked with adverse fetal outcomes and recurrence in subsequent pregnancies, making it a clinically significant pathology. As predicting onset of VUE prior to delivery would allow for the development of treatments or preventative strategies, our study sought to determine maternal risk factors and placental pathologies associated with VUE. Study Design: Using a retrospective case-control study design, placental pathology reports from 2010 to 2018 were used to identify 356 cases of documented VUE, matched 1:2 to 664 placentae without VUE by maternal age, parity, and gestational age at delivery. For both cases and controls, maternal demographics and additional diagnosed pathologies were recorded from the electronic medical record. Comparisons between groups were reported as means with standard deviation and significant differences were estimated using fisher’s exact test or chi-square test as appropriate. Results: Women with preexisting chronic hypertension (odds ratio [OR] 1.7) and a BMI >30 kg/m2 (OR 1.2) were more likely to develop VUE. During pregnancy, VUE is associated with a diagnosis of preeclampsia (OR 1.7) and gestational diabetes (OR 1.3). When accompanying pathologies were compared, VUE was more common in placentae with a single umbilical artery (OR 2.5), avascular villi (OR 11.0), deciduitis (OR 42.2), development of maternal vasculopathy (OR 4.9), and chorionic vasculitis (OR 3.4). Conclusion: Obese and hypertensive women are at greatest risk for developing VUE during pregnancy. Pathologic evaluation of the placenta following delivery is therefore important and is especially useful for preconception counseling. Obese women with history of VUE may benefit from antenatal surveillance in subsequent pregnancies.
