N2  - Objective: To investigate Trikafta (elexacaftor/tezacaftor/ivacaftor and ivacaftor) effects on perinatal outcomes. Study Design: Five pregnant patients with cystic fibrosis were managed with the combination CFTR modulator, Trikafta, and were enrolled in this clinical case series. Pre-pregnancy cystic fibrosis status, pregnancy and postpartum course, and neonatal outcomes were evaluated in detail. Results: All five pregnancies were conceived spontaneously. One of the five patients had previously required assisted reproductive techniques. Patients were started on Trikafta 1-12 months prior to their pregnancy. Four patients chose to continue Trikafta throughout the majority of the pregnancy, while one chose to discontinue Trikafta in the first trimester but resumed with worsening symptoms. All patients experienced improvement in their pulmonary symptoms and had decreased exacerbations on Trikafta. Only one patient experienced active exacerbation in pregnancy. One patient had hemoptysis before starting Trikafta, and experienced resolution in pregnancy. Two patients developed a hypertensive disorder of pregnancy. Four pregnancies delivered at term, with an iatrogenic late preterm delivery for the fifth patient. Four of the five patients required cesarean section delivery, and three of these were performed due to category II fetal heart rate tracing remote from delivery. Both patients with pre-gestational diabetes had neonates with birth weights < 50%ile and the other three had birth weights < 25%ile. One neonate had a postnatal diagnosis of muscular VSD; no other anomalies were identified. Three neonates required NICU admission, of which two were for transient tachypnea of the newborn. Conclusion: A large multi-center prospective study is needed to assess the full impact of Trikafta use in pregnancy. At this time, providers should continue to weigh the risks and benefits of its use when considering both the maternal and fetal health. [Formula presented]
