N2  - OBJECTIVE: PCOS is known to raise the risk of pregnancy complications, including gestational diabetes (GDM) and hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (HDP) as compared to controls. These increased risks are likely related to the intrinsic insulin resistance in PCOS, and the magnitude of insulin resistance is primarily related to obesity. Lean women with PCOS are minimally at risk for cardiovascular disease due to the mild nature of their insulin resistance. Therefore, we have studied the risk of pregnancy complication in women with PCOS and BMIR30 kg/m2 as compared to women with PCOS and BMI<30 using a national database. MATERIALS AND METHODS: This is a retrospective study using the Health Care Cost and Utilization Project-Nationwide Inpatient Sample (HCUP-NIS) database from 2004 to 2014, inclusive. Using multivariate logistic regression analysis, women with PCOS who had a BMIR30 kg/m2 were compared to women with BMI<30 kg/m2 regarding pregnancy, delivery, and neonatal outcomes, while controlling for confounding effects. There were over 9 million unique pregnancies in the database, 14855 of whom had PCOS. 3286 had a BMIR30 kg/m2 and 11569 had a BMI<30kg/m2. Per the Tri-Council Policy statement (2018), IRB approval was not required, given data was anonymous and publicly available. RESULTS: Obese women with PCOS were more likely covered by Medicaid, more commonly Black or Hispanic and not Asian, with lower household incomes, were older, used illicit drugs, had chronic hypertension, smoked, had pre-gestational diabetes (P<0.001 in all cases) and less often used IVF (P<0.05). Rates of thyroid disease did not differ (p=0.42). When controlling for rates of these statistically different factors, women with PCOS and BMI>30 were at increased risk for: HDP (aOR: 1.89, 95%CI: 1.59-2.25), gestational hypertension (aOR: 1.56, 95%CI:1.22-1.98), preeclampsia (aOR:2.17, 95%CI:1.78-2.80), GDM (aOR: 1.75, 95%CI:1.47-2.47), chorioamnionitis (aOR: 1.55, 95% CI:1.07-2.24, p=0.02) & caesarean section (aOR: 1.41, 95%CI:1.21- 1.65), Risks were decreased for: placenta previa (aOR: 0.38, 95% CI:0.15-0.999) & spontaneous vaginal delivery (aOR: 0.75, 95% CI:0.64-0.88). (p<0.0001 in all cases above, unless noted). Rates of eclampsia, super imposed preeclampsia, PPROM, abruption, operative vaginal delivery, post partum haemorrhage, wound complications, blood transfusion, maternal infection, thromboembolic phenomena, small for gestational age infants, intra uterine fetal demise and congenital anomalies did not differ between groups. CONCLUSIONS: Obesity in PCOS is primarily associated with increased risk of HDP, GDM and operative deliveries when compared to women with PCOS and BMI<30 kg/m2. IMPACT STATEMENT: The insulin resistance of obesity combined with that of PCOS further augment the risks of pregnancy complications traditionally associated with defects in insulin mediated glucose uptake, but not other complications.
