N2  - Background and Aims: Statins are contraindicated during pregnancy and lactation; however, data on statin use in pregnancy is limited, and the risks associated with statin exposure in human pregnancies remain unclear. We aimed 1) to examine patterns of statin use prior to, during, and after pregnancy, and 2) to examine the association between exposure to statins during pregnancy and health outcomes in mother and offspring. Methods: We collected data on statin use in pregnancy and outcomes for all pregnant women in Norway in 2005-2018 from national registries, including the Medical Birth Registry of Norway and the Norwegian Prescription Database. Registries were linked using the woman’s personal ID number. Drug utilization measures included statin prescription fills, co-prescribing patterns, predictors of discontinuation, and statin use in recurrent pregnancies. Drug safety measures included statin exposure associated with pre-specified perinatal and maternal outcomes, including birth weight, gestational age at birth, major congenital malformations, preeclampsia, and gestational diabetes mellitus. Results: Over 800,000 pregnancies were included in the analysis. Few women dispensed a statin during the first trimester of pregnancy (0.045 %). The two main statins used were simvastatin and atorvastatin, and we found a clear towards the latter the past decade. Women that filled a prescription for a statin in first trimester of their first pregnancy were more likely to do so in subsequent pregnancies. Conclusions: As statins are increasingly being used by women of childbearing age, it is essential to surveille patterns of statin prescriptions across patient’s reproductive life, and to determine the safety of these drugs in pregnancy.
