N2  - Introduction: We describe an unusual presentation of acute kidney injury (AKI) during labour in a woman with no previous renal pathology or obvious precipitating causes. Case Report: A 20-year-old primigravida weighing 140 kg (BMI 51 kg/m2) was admitted to maternity unit at 39+5 weeks’ gestation in spontaneous labour. She had diet-controlled gestational diabetes and her baby was large for gestational age. She had history of recurrent urinary tract infections during pregnancy, but was otherwise well. Aspirin 75 mg had been stopped 3 days before admission. Initial observations were normal and fetal heart rate was normal. Labour progressed slowly and the woman was considered for oxytocin augmentation. As per guidelines for sodium monitoring before oxytocin infusion, blood biochemistry was taken.1 Urea, creatinine and electrolytes, were all within normal range. The oxytocin infusion was delayed until the anaesthetist was able to site epidural a few hours later. Poor urinary output despite a positive fluid balance was noted and the woman had not passed urine output for a number of hours. She was assessed regarding fluid status, evidence of infection or preeclampsia. She was haemodynamically stable, capillary refill was [Formula presented] sec, temperature 37.9 °C. The fetal heart rate remained normal. An IV fluid bolus was prescribed and serum biochemistry repeated. Creatinine rose from 54 to 124 [Formula presented]ol/L in 6 h. Urea increased to 5.7 from 3.1 mmol/L. A further fluid bolus was given and her catheter flushed to ensure patency. Ultrasound revealed an empty bladder. Creatinine rose further to 184 [Formula presented]ol/L and urea to 6.7 mmol/L. Her lactate was normal. Examination revealed cervical dilatation of 8 cm with the baby in an OP position with caput and moulding. Malposition of baby was possibly causing bilateral ureteric obstruction resulting in anuria and AKI. Senior obstetricians were, however, initially reluctant to perform emergency caesarean section as the fetal heart rate was normal and because of the woman’s high BMI. The renal team performed a bedside ultrasound scan of the kidneys which gave the impression of bilateral prominent calyces. The decision was made for emergency caesarean section. Creatinine was now 205 [Formula presented]ol/L, and estimated GFR 29, 12 h after a normal baseline. Caesarean section was performed under spinal anaesthesia and delivery of baby was uneventful. No obvious renal tract abnormalities were noted. By the end of the operation 200 mL of urine was present in urinary catheter. The patient continued a normal diuresis with renal indices returning to baseline within 48 h. Discussion: Anuria and AKI are key to prompt diagnosis of post-renal obstructive uropathy. Acute renal failure due obstructive uropathy by the gravid uterus is a rare cause in pregnancy, and management with amniotomy or urinary-by pass techniques such as nephrostomy or stents have been reported.2 However, this may be unsuitable during labour. Early diagnosis and delivery by caesarean section may halt worsening renal function and morbidity.
