N2  - Objective The North East has the highest prevalence of Obesity in England, with County Durham the second highest local authority in rates of obesity related hospital admissions. This audit assesses the management of Obesity in Pregnancy in Darlington Memorial Hospital (DMH) which provides maternity services to the population of Darlington, the largest district in County Durham. Methods This was a retrospective audit which used a standardised proforma to collect data on 40 women with a Body Mass Index (BMI) of over 35 who had delivered at DMH between August 2018 and August 2019. Results BMI ranged from 35.9-65.3. 47.5% of women had a BMI of more than 40 at booking. All women were reviewed at a consultant-led clinic. 95% of patients had risks of obesity discussed at booking. All women were assessed for risk of venous thromboembolism (VTE) antenatally and postnatally with prophylaxis appropriately prescribed. 90% had a Glucose Tolerance Test (GTT) at 24 weeks. 19 of 21 patients with a BMI of over 40 had a booking GTT. All with a BMI of over 40 had an anaesthetic review. All had appropriate intrapartum management including active management of the third stage of labour. 18% of patients were not re-weighed antenatally. The most frequent complication was post-partum haemorrhage, followed by failed induction, fetal growth restriction and gestational diabetes. The most common mode of delivery was Caesarean Section (55%) followed by normal vaginal delivery (42.5%) and instrumental delivery (2.5%). Conclusions Reweighing of patients and discussion of risks require improvement. Staff education, review of the Trust Obesity Proforma and re-audit are imperative in improving care.
