N2  - Objective To bring awareness of the need to contribute to the limited amount of information regarding maternal post COVID-19 complications and highlight a case of supraventricular tachycardia in pregnancy post COVID-19 infection. Case report A 31-year-old female G2P1 at 27 weeks gestation presented to hospital with a 2-day history of headache unrelieved with analgesics. Her antenatal course was complicated by gestational diabetes, positive RPR test and recent diagnosis of Severe COVID-19 infection with Type 2 respiratory failure at 24 weeks gestation of which she received an appropriate regime of antibiotics, steroids, and chronic medication. On presentation, patient noted experiencing an episode of vomiting with subsequent palpitations and was found to have tenderness about the frontal and maxillary sinuses, an irregular heartbeat prompting an electrocardiogram that identified atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response. Shortly after, patient became diaphoretic and subsequent attachment of cardiac monitor identified supraventricular tachycardia (140-200s) with hemodynamic stability prompting a consult to Internal Medicine service. Patient was commenced on appropriate regime of a calcium channel and later a beta blocker. She also received deep venous thrombus prophylaxis. All blood investigations and fetal assessments remained in normal limits. Heart rate normalization was achieved on day 6 of admission and echocardiogram results were normal. Prior to discharge, patient was suspected to have a deep vein thrombus following complaints of pain to upper part of right leg but same was ruled out with Doppler study. Discharge medication was given along with advice to ensure delivery is facilitated in a tertiary level institution to minimize any possible negative outcome. Conclusion Maternal post COVID-19 complications is an area of particular interest in the field of obstetrics and gynecology with significant importance to those involved with guideline and protocol development. Cardiac manifestations of COVID-19 infection can have life-threatening ramifications if not identified early and subsequently treated. Those involved with obstetric patient management must have a high degree of suspicion for these complications as COVID-19 infection can add further strain to a cardiovascular system still undergoing dynamic changes. Further research on maternal post COVID-19 complications is encouraged, especially if more cases of supraventricular tachycardia are recognized.
