N2  - Tolosa-Hunt syndrome (THS) is a rare steroid-responsive etiology for painful ophthalmoplegia which is caused by idiopathic granulomatous inflammation in the carotid artery or cavernous sinus. The estimated annual incidence is one case per million per year. It is characterized by unilateral orbital pain associated with paresis of one or more of the cranial nerves, commonly leading to diplopia. We reported current case to highlight on the challenges of the diagnosis and treatment of a rare brain tumor in pregnancy. A 43-year-old Emirati female, G11P7A3, previous vaginal deliveries, presented at 38 weeks of pregnancy to emergency department with a 6 weeks history of chronic headaches, left sided facial numbness, ptosis and blurry of vision with recent MRI brain showing pituitary tumor with possible left cavernous sinus thrombosis. Her pregnancy was uneventful complicated by gestational diabetes (GDM) controlled by diet. Reported a similar attack which was treated with steroids in 2014 besides previous right bell's palsy with mild residual right facial weakness more than ten years ago associated with migraines spells. Clinical findings included left eye ptosis, partial left abducens nerve palsy, right facial weakness with a significant elevation of prolactin level, a slight elevation in erythrocytes sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein level. MRI brain revealed a pituitary tumor with an abnormal signal of soft tissue fullness within the cavernous sinus extending toward superior orbital fissure and narrowing of the left internal carotid with displacement of both optic nerves medially, with no evidence of cavernous sinus thrombosis. In view of that, there was highly suspicion of THS. Subsequently, patient underwent cesarean section delivery with bilateral tubal ligation under spinal anesthesia with good fetal outcome and commenced on a high intravenous dose of steroid in view of the diagnosis of THS upon a multidisciplinary team decision. At follow up visits, initially, improvement of presenting symptoms noticed but after 3 months, persistent diplopia, left sided facial numbness with fatigue and difficulties with concentration developed again. Further investigations were carried out and Octreoscan showed possible Cavernous Sinus Meningioma, which treated by radiotherapy for six weeks with corticosteroids. At three weeks post radiotherapy, patient neurological symptoms resolved completely. THS is a rare condition and its diagnosis depends on the presence of characteristic physical features and thorough clinical assessments with a variety of specialized radiologic tests to exclude other neurological conditions. Steroids are the mainstay of treatment, but several therapies have been investigated for steroid resistant THS like radiotherapy in our case.
