N2  - Background: Pregnancy is characterized by a series of metabolic adaptations to guarantee the proper energetic and nutritional demands of fetal development. A decrease in insulin sensitivity, alterations in glucose homeostasis, and hyperlipidemia are present in late gestation. Among other potential factors, the rising consumption of high caloric diets and sedentary lifestyles contribute to an increased risk for metabolic-related pregnancy complications. Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) is highly associated with maternal increased risk of type-2-diabetes and cardiovascular diseases development after pregnancy. We aimed to understand cardiac metabolic adaptations of pregnancy per se and to evaluate the alterations induced by GDM. Materials and Methods: Pregnant (P) and non-pregnant (NP) female Sprague-Dawley rats were fed a control diet (C) or a high-fat-high-sugar-diet (HFHS) and euthanized 19-weeks later. Cardiac and plasma tissue were collected for analysis. Glucose tolerance was evaluated before and at mid-pregnancy. Gestational weight gain, litter size and sex distribution, cardiac mitochondrial oxygen consumption rates, and membrane potential were assessed. The levels of plasma parameters, protein expression, and mRNA transcripts were evaluated. According to Gaussian distribution, data were analyzed using Mann-Whitney tests or t-student test (n ≥ 6), p ≤ 0.05 considered statistically significant. Results: P-HFHS showed greater glucose intolerance in the first hour after glucose administration (vs. P-C). HFHS-diet consumption resulted in excessive gestational weight gain (p < 0.0001), larger litter sizes (p ~ 0.03), and predisposed to an asymmetric sex distribution of the offspring, favoring male (p ~ 0.02). HFHS-diet and pregnancy per se contributed to decreased glucose levels, which were increased in P-HFHS (p ~ 0.002 vs. NP-HFHS, p ~ 0.004 vs. P-C). HFHS-diet was associated with increased Atherogenic Index of Plasma (AIP). HFHS-diet consumption reduced mitochondrial respiration efficiency, observed by decreased RCR values and ADP/O ratio for both groups, especially aggravated in P-HFHS (p ~ 0.008 and p ~ 0.02 vs. NP-C), using Complex-I substrates. The protein levels of inflammatory markers (TNFα and IL6) increased concomitantly with the aggravated condition of the experimental groups, and pregnancy contributed to increased levels of HIF1α transcript (p~0.04vs.NP-C) and decreased levels of PPARα transcript (p~0.04vs.NP-C). Conclusions: HFHS-diet consumption or pregnancy per se and P-HFHS acted as separate hits in plasma glucose levels, although AIP was particularly affected by the HFHS-diet. The cardiac mitochondrial function of the female rats was significantly affected by the HFHS-diet. Pregnancy per se affected inflammation, and HIF1α and PPARα transcript levels, with further aggravation when coupled to the consumption of a HFHS-diet.
