N2  - Background Women with a history of adverse pregnancy outcomes (APOs), such as hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (HDP), gestational diabetes (GDM), and preterm birth, are at increased risk of ischemic heart disease in later life. We aimed to determine the features of myocardial infarction (MI) among women with prior APOs compared with those with normal pregnancies. Methods We performed a retrospective analysis of women referred for left heart catheterization with an MI defined by (1) elevated troponin or (2) a diagnosis code of non-ST elevation MI or ST elevation MI (2010-2019). Those with an MI were then matched with our Magee Obstetric Maternal and Infant database, which has detailed abstracted pregnancy data for deliveries from 1988 to 2019. Those with hypertension or diabetes pre-dating the index pregnancy were excluded. Demographics, medical history, and MI presentation details were compared between women with MI with and without an APO history. Results We identified 341 women ages 18 to 65 with an MI and left heart catheterization, of whom 160 had a prior APO (HDP N=60, age 48±9 years; GDM N=23, age 52±6 years; and preterm birth N=44, age 50±8 years). Women with HDP and GDM history presented with their MI sooner after delivery (19±7 years for HDP and 18±7 years for GDM) compared with women without an APO history (21±7 years; p=0.03). Women with prior APO had a higher prevalence of current diabetes compared with those without an APO (37% for HDP, p=0.001; 53% for GDM, p<0.001; and 14% for normal pregnancies). Women with APO history had similar MI features including similar rates of ST elevation MI, obstructive and multi-vessel coronary artery disease, percutaneous coronary intervention, and cardiogenic shock compared with women without APO history. There were also similar peak troponin levels and left ventricular ejection fraction between groups. Notably, women with GDM history had higher left ventricular end diastolic pressure compared with women without APO history (23±9 vs 18±7 mmHg; p=0.04). Conclusion Women with APO history present with MI sooner after delivery, but otherwise have similar MI characteristics and MI severity compared with women without an APO history.
