N2  - Background Abnormal placental development and function occur in pregnancies with fetal congenital heart disease (CHD) but it is unclear whether maternal or fetal factors contribute. This study aimed to determine the frequency of maternal and fetal placental abnormalities in CHD. Methods A single-center retrospective cohort study was conducted from January 2013 - June 2019. Inclusion criteria were inborn infants ≥36 weeks gestation with a prenatal diagnosis of moderate-severe CHD. Exclusion criteria were multiple gestation pregnancy, unknown gestational age, or unavailable placental pathology data. Placental pathology reports were reviewed and abnormalities categorized as maternal vascular malperfusion (MVM) or fetal vascular malperfusion (FVM) per the 2016 Amsterdam Placental Workshop Group Consensus Statement. Demographic and clinical data were collected from existing clinical databases and retrospective chart review and logistic regression was performed to evaluate the association with MVM and FVM. To determine the association of CHD, infants were categorized as single ventricle with aortic obstruction, single ventricle with pulmonic obstruction, dextro-transposition of the great arteries (d-TGA), or two ventricle physiology. Results There were 212 pregnancies included for analysis. Rates of MVM and FVM were 48.6% and 31.1%, respectively. There was no difference in MVM by cardiac group. However infants with two ventricle physiology were more likely than those with d-TGA to have FVM [OR 4.22, 95% CL (1.383-18.80), p=0.0260]. After controlling for CHD group, gestational diabetes [OR 2.36, 95% CL (1.09-5.38), p=0.0335] and oligohydramnios [OR 9.22, 95% CL (1.57-175.37), p=0.0409] were associated with MVM. Pregnancies without maternal hypertension [OR 2.66, 95% CL (1.15-7.01), p=0.0318] and female infants [OR 1.88, 95% (1.03-3.45), p=0.0403] were more likely to have FVM. Conclusion Both MVM and FVM are common in pregnancies with fetal CHD. Maternal/pregnancy factors are associated with MVM whereas innate patient-specific factors (sex and diagnosis) are associated with FVM. Further research is needed to determine the impact of these abnormalities on infant outcome.
