N2  - Background While the number of successful pregnancies in heart transplant (HT) recipients is increasing, data regarding outcomes after delivery are limited. Methods The 2010-2015 Nationwide Inpatient Sample was queried to identify index hospitalizations for delivery in HT recipients. Adverse delivery events of HT patients were compared to those of women without HT or systolic heart failure (HF) undergoing delivery using multivariable logistic regression. Sample weights were used in all multivariate analyses to estimate measures of association generalizable to the U.S. population. Results A total of 21,922,631 delivery hospitalizations were analyzed; 94 hospitalizations were of HT recipients. Women post-HT were significantly younger (26 vs. 28 years, p <0.001), had greater comorbidity burden as evidenced by the Elixhauser Comorbidity Index (1.16 vs. 0.24, p<0.001), lower household incomes (p=0.03), and were more likely to deliver at large (89.5% vs. 58.3%, p=0.02) and teaching hospitals (100% vs 59.4%, p<0.001). There were no significant differences in race, primary insurance payer, or hospital region between groups. Mean length of hospital stay was significantly longer in women post-HT compared to controls (4.53 vs. 2.64 days, p<0.001). After adjustment for age, race/ethnicity, pre-existing hypertension, and Elixhauser Comorbidity Index, history of HT was significantly associated with increased incidence of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (HDP) (OR 6.22, p=0.004), fetal malformations (OR 2.86, p=0.04), and induction at time of delivery (OR 2.99, p=0.02). There were no differences in the incidence of gestational diabetes, fetal growth restriction, rate of c-section, postpartum hemorrhage, pre-term delivery, or renal failure. There were no in-hospital deaths during the study period. Conclusion The number of pregnant women delivering post-HT remains low. Women post-HT experience increased maternal and fetal risks during the delivery period compared with women without HT or history of systolic HF, including increased risk of HDP and fetal malformations. More studies are needed to improve obstetric care and maternal-fetal outcomes for the HT population.
