N2  - Introduction: Currently obesity is an important socio-economic problem in all countries. Mother's obesity contributes to the development of complications of pregnancy and childbirth, as well as a risk factor for congenital malformations in the fetus. Object: To reveal the features of the perinatal period in children born to obese mothers. Methods: The study identified the most frequent complications of pregnancy in obese women, such as: preeclampsia (10.7% and 1.8%, p<0.05), threat of interruption (17.2% and 3.6%, p<0.05), intrauterine growth retardation (8.6% and 2.6%, p<0.05). A burdened obstetric and gynecological anamnesis was recorded in 22.7% of obese patients, in patients with a normal body mass index-13.4% (p<0.05). Obese women were more likely to have concomitant extragenital pathology: diseases of the cardiovascular system (16.4% and 6.2%, p<0.05), urinary system (17.2% and 10.7% p<0.05), gastrointestinal tract (12.5% and 3.6%, p<0.05), endocrine system (22.7% and 12.5%, p<0.05). However, statistically significant differences in the frequency of occurrence of gestational diabetes mellitus were not found in 16.4% in the group of obese mothers and 11.7% in the control group (p> 0.05). The manifestations of urogenital infection were observed equally often in both groups (7.8% and 5.4%, p> 0.05), also, no statistically significant differences were found concerning diseases of the respiratory system (27.3% and 21.4%, p> 0.05), respiratory viral infections during pregnancy (25% and 26.8%, p> 0.05), however, the use of antibiotics was more common in the group of obese mothers (7.8% and 2.7%, p<0.05). In obese women, cesarean section was performed more often than in the control group (40.6% and 15.2%, p> 0.05). Conclusion: Thus, the influence of overweight mothers is a risk factor for unfavorable perinatal outcomes, and has a negative impact on the development and health indicators of newborn. Further research is needed on the relationship between maternal obesity and the effect on the fetus. In the group of mothers with obesity, the birth of a large fetus was more often observed 21.1% in relation to the group of mothers with a normal body mass index-4.5% (p<0.05). In children born to obese mothers, in the early neonatal period, hyperbilirubinemia was most often observed (30.5% and 16.1%, p<0.05), hemolytic disease of the newborn (4.7% and 1.8%, p<0.05), asphyxia (12.5% and 1.8%, p<0.05) and genital crisis (7.0% and 1.7%, p<0.05). Children born to obese mothers more often required intensive care (11.7% and 1.8%, p<0.05), including more often required mechanical ventilation (3.9% and 1.8%, p<0.05).
