N2  - Genetic disorders of pancreatic beta cell secretion leading to congenital or familial hyperinsulinism (HI) are the most common cause of severe and persistent hypoglycemia in infants and children. The most common genetic etiologies in children with congenital HI are inactivating missense mutations in the genes that encode for the two subunits of the pancreatic beta cell potassium ATP channel (KATP), ABCC8 and KCNJ11. While these disorders are well described in the literature, until 2014, the association between congenital HI and rhabdomyolysis had not been reported (Albaqumi et al. 2014). Herein, we report a patient with a heterozygous paternally inherited variant of uncertain significance in KCNJ11: c.617G>A (p.Arg206His). Our proband was an 11 day old LGA male born at 38 weeks 5 days gestation to nonconsanguineous parents (birth weight 4080 g, 91st % tile). There was no history of maternal or gestational diabetes. He underwent genetic testing for persistent hypoglycemia resulting in seizures that required frequent D10 boluses and continuous feeds initially in the NICU. Endocrinology was consulted. Total insulin level drawn while patient was hypoglycemic was elevated at 9.4 μU/mL. A hyperinsulinism panel (23 genes) with del/dup analysiswas obtained. The probandwas responsive to diazoxide and maintained stable blood sugars on discharge with scheduled feeds. Boodhansingh et al. 2019 described 6 newnovel missense variants in (KATP) in 11 probands with HI which added to the 62 monoallelic mutations in ABCC8 and KCNJ11 already known. One of those novel variants described was a maternally inherited c.617G>A (p.Arg206His) in KCNJ11 that was responsive to diazoxide. Novel mutations were confirmed to be dominant based on in vitro expression studies and in vivo testing. That patient's mother did not experience significant hypoglycemia symptoms. Our proband's father is a 28 year old Caucasian male with a history of vague myalgias, arthralgias, headaches, and intermittent fatigue and dizziness during his adolescence. He recalled that eating would improve his symptoms. At age 18, he was hospitalized for nausea, fatigue and emesis after a period of vigorous exercise when he was found to have severe rhabdomyolysis with creatinine kinase (CK) greater than 150,000 that lead to anuria and acute renal failure requiring 2 weeks of hemodialysis. The etiology of that episode of rhabdomyolysis at the time was attributed to his lifestyle as a collegiate athlete (vigorous exercise) combined with dehydration. He was not found to be hypoglycemic during that admission nor on subsequent follow up labs. Upon chart review, the patient's father had normalization of BUN and creatinine by 4 years status post admission, and annual kidney function evaluation was discontinued. Our proband's diagnosis and the paternal genetic results prompted reinvolvement of nephrology and endocrinology for the patient's father. At that time he had mild hypophosphatemia (5.4), normal BUN and creatinine, but mildly elevated CK (250). His C-peptide, total insulin levels and TSH were normal (not drawn at a time when he was definitively hypoglycemic). He has had no additional hospitalizations or required dialysis after his acute renal failure at age 18 although he continues to have hypophosphatemia and persistently mildly elevated CK. Currently he is advised to abstain from strenuous exercise, carbohydrate rich meals, and to check blood sugars daily and monitor clinically for symptoms of hypoglycemia. This report expands the phenotype of those with KCNJ11-related congenital HI and reports the second individual with rhabdomyolysis with a KCNJ-11 HI. We propose monitoring for physical signs and symptoms of rhabdomyolysis as part of healthcare surveillance in patients with this condition. Multidisciplinary involvement of genetics, nephrology, and endocrinology is recommended to facilitate appropriate genetic testing and counseling and prognostic guidance. More case reports and case series are needed to better understand the association between HI and rhabdomyolysis such that effective surveillance and treatment strategies can be established.
