N2  - Variants identified in patients with comorbidities and environmental exposures can complicate their interpretation. This case describes amale neonate with multiple congenital anomalies, antenatal exposuresand a variant of uncertain significance in WNT5A.Antenatally, the pregnancy was complicated by maternal hypertension,type 2 diabetes with hemoglobin A1c of 11 prior to pregnancy,pneumonia, pulmonary embolism and cardiomyopathy. Imaging at 21weeks gestation identified complex cardiac defects, clenched fists,bilateral absence of the tibia and fibula, abnormal posturing of the feetand shortened femurs, shortening of the upper extremities, suspectedsacral agenesis, micrognathia, fetal growth restriction and a singleumbilical artery. Amniocentesis was performed and both thekaryotype and chromosomal microarray (CMA) showed normal maleresults, 46,XY.arr(XY)x1,(1–22)x2.Our patient was born at 34w2d to a 29-year-old G7P6 mother, bycesarean section due to preeclampsia and breech presentation. Herequired intubation in the NICU. His physical exam and postnatalimaging showed severe micrognathia, smooth philtrum, systolicejection murmur, severe rhizomelic and mezomelic shortening withbilateral clubbed feet,11 ribs on the right,10 ribs on the left, abnormalsacrum, camptodactyly of the upper extremities and bilateralcryptorchidism. Neonatal echocardiogram showed truncus arteriosuswith biventricular hypertrophy, small to moderate atrial shunt, largemuscular inlet ventricular septal defect and branch pulmonaryarteries not well defined with concern for discontinuation.While suspicious of diabetic embryopathy, our patient’s severephenotype prompted genetic evaluation with rapid duo-exome,postnatal CMA and FISH analysis for the 22q11 region. The duoexomeidentified a novel, non-maternal, heterozygous WNT5A(NM_003392.4):c.41del (p.L14Wfs*14). Variants in WNT5A are associatedwith autosomal dominant Robinow Syndrome (RS); however,while our patient’s phenotype had some similarities to RS, his clinicalcourse was more severe than would be expected. There is no familyhistory of RS and the father of the child is reportedly of average heightand good health but was not available for molecular testing.Classification of this variant hinges on the strength and applicability ofPVS1 criteria and PM6/PS2 (PMID: 25741868). Evidence providessupport for an interaction effect of this variant and the antenatalenvironment but does not meet the threshold for likely pathogenic.This variant is predicted to result in nonsense mediated decay;however, loss-of-function is not a known disease mechanism and it isunclear if this clinical presentation could be explained by haploinsufficiencyof WNT5A. Reported variants in WNT5A with a clinicaldiagnosis of autosomal dominant Robinow Syndrome are missense(24716670, 19918918). Population data suggests constraint consistentwith absence of truncating variants 5’ of the theoretical NMDboundary (c.634), absence of canonical splice variants in gnomADand a pLI score of 0.99 (30192042, 28552198). However, there are a fewpublished cases of truncating WNT5Avariants. A patient homozygousfor c.561del presented with similar complex cardiac anomalies to ourpatient as well as thoracic abnormalities, genitourinary and skeletalfindings, however his confirmed heterozygous parents were healthy(29575631, 25898814). In another case with an inherited heterozygousc.325C>T variant, the patient had isolated left-sided renalagenesis (29220675). Additional evidence for loss-of-function isfound in published zebrafish and Wnt5a−/− mouse models(100021340, 17515859, 25412793, 29276006, 32046118). The phenotypesinclude complex cardiac anomalies of the outflow tracts,abnormal lung lobulation and histology, duplex kidney, skeletalanomalies of the limbs, thorax and caudal regression and absentgenital tubercle. Heterozygous mice were apparently normal.Additionally, several lines of evidence support a role for WNTsignalingin the pathogenesis of diabetic embryopathy includingreduced expression of Wnt5a in diabetes exposed embryos(18937363, 25813538, 25540130).Ultimately, our patient suffered pneumothorax leading to cardiacarrest and demise. The postmortem evaluation uncovered additionalanomalies that were similar to published Wnt5a−/− mouse models. Itrevealed right pulmonary artery stenosis, atretic mitral and bicuspidvalves, abnormal branching pattern of the bronchi entering a singlelobe on each side, duplex right kidney with two ureters andpelvicalyceal cyst in left lower kidney.We hypothesize the patient’s phenotype was due to insufficientexpression of WNT5A resulting from heterozygosity for a null variant and exacerbated by maternal diabetes, leading to severely abnormalearly development
