N2  - INTRODUCTION: Clostridioides difficile infection (CDI) in pregnant women has an increased incidence and severity. The risk factors for CDI in pregnancy include age over 35 years, multiple gestations, smoking, Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis, long-term antibiotic use, pneumonia, pyelonephritis, cesarean or perineal wound infection. Immune changes associated with pregnancy may also contribute. In pregnancy, the T helper 1 (Th1) response is downregulated and T helper 2 response is upregulated so that the maternal immune system does not reject the fetus. Clostridium difficileinduced colitis is mediated by a Th1 immune response. It is possible that the immunoregulation associated with pregnancy increases the risk of CDI by downregulating the Th1 response. METHODS: A 21-year-old, G1P0 female who had cesarean section (C-section) at 35 weeks and 4 days gestation presented six days later with upper abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. She had been diagnosed with gestational diabetes and preeclampsia and received 2g cefazolin prior to C-section. She had given birth to a healthy boy weighing 9 lb 7 oz. Leukocytosis (13.7 X10^9/L) and acute kidney injury were present. Computed tomography (CT) of abdomen and pelvis showed marked gaseous and fluid distention of the stomach, small bowel loops, and colon with minimal ascites. She developed diarrhea and stool Clostridioides difficile was positive. She was started on oral vancomycin and intravenous metronidazole. She developed multi-system organ failure with hypotension requiring intubation. Antibiotics were changed to meropenem and oral vancomycin. She further decompensated and required vasopressors. She then went into cardiac arrest and could not be resuscitated. Autopsy revealed patchy pseudomembranes on the mucosa of the distal small intestine and large intestine. There were focal areas of exudate with accompanying pus on the serosal aspects of the ascending colon, transverse colon, and bowel mesentery. RESULTS: A high index of suspicion of CDI is necessary for diagnosis in the peripartum period given significant increase in maternal fatality related to complications. Despite being young with no risk factors for CDI and having received only one dose of prophylactic antibiotic, our patient developed fulminant colitis leading to death.
