N2  - Background and Aims: Presumed perinatal ischemic stroke (PPIS) defines children presenting outside a normal perinatal period with chronic, focal infarction on neuroimaging. We describe the characteristics of PPIS attended in a Childhood Stroke Clinic in a third level hospital and correlated the findings from the neurosonological examination with the MRA. Validating ultrasound as a tool to rule out vascular pathology in PPIS would reduce costs and avoid the need for anesthesia. Methods: We retrospectively collected the cases PPIS attended at the Virgen Macarena Hospital in the period 01/01/2005-01/12/2020. The risk factors, clinical and radiological findings were analyzed. The results from supra-aortic trunk and transcranial doppler (SAT/TCD) conducted by trained neurologists were compared to MRA. Results: We report 20 cases of PPIS. The mean age at diagnosis was 28 months. 40% of the mothers suffered previous abortions. The most prevalent risk factor was gestational diabetes mellitus (60%). The majority were arterial strokes (73%) in the territory of the left middle cerebral artery (60%). MRA did not provide any relevant additional information from what we obtained with SAT/DTC. A case with elevated factor VIII and a case with protein S deficiency were detected through thrombophilia tests. All patients suffered some type of sequelae and no recurrence stroke occurred in the cohort. Conclusions: We report our experience in one of the first Childhood Stroke Clinics that helped modifying management of some patients during this study. In addition, we have obtained good correlation rates between SAT/DTC and MRA, which could change the diagnostic paradigm in pediatric vascular pathology.
