N2  - INTRODUCTION Intrauterine ductus arteriosus (DA) closure is rare, particularly under 32 weeks of gestational age (wGA) because of the decreased sensitivity of ductal tissue to factors that promote constriction at earlier GA, and often results in significant morbidity and mortality [1]. Its incidence is unknown. Premature DA closure leads to increased blood volume through pulmonary arteries causing pulmonary hypertension and thus right ventricular hypertrophy/dilation, tricuspid regurgitation, fetal hydrops until intrauterine or postnatal death [2-4]. Herein we report two cases, one with prenatal diagnosis of DA closure in extremely premature newborn and another one about term newborn in which diagnosis was made in the immediate postnatal period; no maternal medication intake was reported in both cases. CASE REPORTS Pt 1 was born at 27+6 wGA from cesarean section (CS) from biamniotic monochorionic twin pregnancy. At 18 wGA fetoscopic laser was performed for the suspect of twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome. At 25 wGA the mother was hospitalized for pPROM; fetal monitoring ultrasound was normal until 27 weeks when the suspicion of closed DA was raised. Fetal echocardiography at 27+5 wGA showed DA closure, hypertrophic and dilated right ventricle, tricuspid insufficiency and supra-systemic pulmonary pressure. Baby presented respiratory distress at birth and was intubated. He progressively developed severe pulmonary hypertension which required iNO (up to 25 ppm) and pulmonary antihypertensive drugs, such as milrinone and sildenafil. On the 13 day of life, Bosentan was started and iNO was continued while on NCPAP and non-invasive ventilation for a total of 50 days. The Respiratory Distress Syndrome was challenging as he required 5 doses of surfactant and he later developed bronchopulmonary dysplasia. The child was discharged on Bosentan therapy, which was continued for 4 months. Echocardiography after bosentan discontinuation was completely normalized. Pt 2 was born at 38+0 wGA from urgent CS for alteration of cardiotocographic trace. Diet-treated maternal gestational diabetes has been reported. At birth baby required ventilation for 2 min; APGAR score at 5 min was normal. Non-invasive respiratory support was needed for the development of respiratory distress to maintain saturations. Chest x-ray showed enlarged cardiac shadow and echocardiogram documented biventricular hypertrophy, dilated right atrium, aneurysmal duct and mild pulmonary hypertension. On clinical examination she had hepatosplenomegaly; blood tests revealed thrombocytopenia that required transfusion and increased liver and cardiac enzymes, due to probable right heart failure secondary to fetal ductal constriction. She required oxygen until day 10 with improvement of clinical conditions. Post-discharge echocardiography still showed mild ventricular hypertrophy. CONCLUSIONS Clinical Manifestations of Fetal DA Closure Range From Mild To Severe Symptoms Until respiratory failure. This event can be diagnosed antenatally with fetal echocardiography and pulsed wave [2] and a multidisciplinary approach is required to define better modalities and timing of delivery [4]. The most frequent neonatal complication is persistent pulmonary hypertension which may require intensive treatment, especially in preterm newborns with comorbidity. These two cases suggest to consider fetal DA closure in case of right overload and tricuspid insufficiency in the absence of structural heart disease but also in every newborn with unexplained pulmonary hypertension.
