N2  - INTRODUCTION Profound and concomitant cardiovascular hemodynamic changes, necessary to support fetoplacental development and its increasing supply demands, occur during a physiological pregnancy. The result of these changes is a volume overload that leads to several compensatory transient heart adaptations. Pregnancies complicated by hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (HDP), particularly those complicated also by fetal growth restriction (FGR), are characterized by a cardiovascular maladaptation. Moreover, endothelial dysfunction may persist after delivery causing an increased risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD) and heart failure (HF). Despite the importance of the problem, very little is known about the link between HDP and CVD. The aim of this study is to explore the relationship between maternal hemodynamic parameters and morpho-functional cardiac changes in pregnancy complicated by HDP. METHODS This is a prospective multicenter observational cohort study. Women who have experienced HDP during their pregnancy were recruited for the purpose of this study and were divided into two groups: (1) women with appropriate for gestational age fetuses (HDP-AGA) and (2) women with growth restricted fetuses (HDP-FGR). Echocardiography data on cardiac function during pregnancy were available, together with a sera sample. A group of normotensive women with uneventful pregnancies was recruited as control. Primary cultures of neonatal rat cardiomyocytes were used as a model to investigate the role of the sera in regulating their proliferation. EdU (5-ethynyl-2'-deoxyuridine) was administered to mark DNA synthesis in proliferating cells. After 3 days of in vitro culture, EdU incorporation was analysed upon immunofluorescence staining using specific antibodies by high content microscopy. A possible protective effect against apoptosis exerted by the selected sera was evaluated, as well, by Caspase activation. RESULTS Overall, 39 women (12 HDP-AGA, 16 HDP-FGR and 11 controls) were included for the analysis. Pre-pregnancy body mass index (BMI) in the presence of gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) was significantly associated with a decrease in total vascular resistance (TVR). In particular, TVR decreases by 22.5 units in women with GDM. Hashimoto's thyroiditis was also associated with a significant decrease in TVR and SVRI (about 343 and 188 units, respectively). Regarding TVR, in the HDP-AGA group, a unitary increase in CM proliferation corresponded to a significant increase in TVR equal to 170.8 units and a unit increase in apoptosis was associated with a decrease in TVR of 0.037 units. Regarding SVRI, a unitary increase in CM proliferation corresponded to a significant increase in SVRI only in the HDP-FGR group, equal to 194.4 units. A significant reduction effect in apoptosis was observed both in the controls and HDP-FGR group: by a unitary increase in apoptosis, there was a decrease in SVRI (0.03 units in the normotensive group and 0.06 units in HDP-FGR). No other significant associations were found. CONCLUSIONS Our results show a relationship between maternal haemodynamic parameters and morpho-functional changes in cardiac cells. Furthermore, there is an association between TVR and apoptosis in line with the oxidative stress typical of placental insufficiency.
