N2  - Objectives Diabetes during pregnancy is associated with metabolic alterations in the offspring leading to future risk of cardiometabolic disease. Circulating levels of adhesion molecules are predictive of future cardiovascular disease in adults, and have been shown to be associated with glycemic control. Diabetes exposure also alters miRNA expression in endothelial cells which also play a crucial role in vascular function. We hypothesized that exposure to diabetes in utero would result in higher concentrations of circulating adhesion molecules and these would be positively associated with exosome-borne miRNA. Methods Umbilical cord serum was collected from healthy, term offspring of women with type 2 or gestational DM (DM group) and normoglycemic controls undergoing elective cesarean section. Endothelial markers were measured by ELISA. Exosomes were precipitated from the serum and total RNA was extracted using commercially available kits. Reverse transcription and qPCR were done using equal volumes of total RNA and cDNA, respectively. Between-group differences were assessed using T-test or Mann Whitney U test, as appropriate, and p<0.05 was considered significant. Results As expected, C-peptide was 59% higher (p=0.0082), insulin 118% higher (p=0.0207) and non-esterified fatty acids (NEFA) 63% higher (p<0.001) in the cord blood from diabetes-exposed infants compared to controls. No between-group difference was found in ICAM, VCAM, E-selectin, soluble endoglin (sENG), or NO. Overall, sENG was significantly correlated with VCAM (r2=0.06, p=0.029), ICAM (r2=0.06, p=0.033), NEFA (r2=0.07, p=0.017), and all miRNAs measured in circulation. ICAM was also associated with E-selectin (r2=0.22, p<0.001) and NEFA (r2=0.07, p=0.018). Conclusions Circulating adhesion molecules have been predictive of future cardiovascular disease in adults and shown to be associated with glycemic control, but less is known about their utility in infants and children. In the present study, exposure to diabetes in utero resulted in higher NEFA and insulin in cord serum demonstrating metabolic derangement, but no differences in circulating adhesion molecules were observed. While the diabetic milieu of pregnancy does not appear to alter these circulating factors, Endoglin, a marker of angiogenesis, was associated with the adhesion markers as well as with circulating exosome-borne miRNAs suggesting that all may be co-secreted products of endothelial origin.
