N2  - Introduction: Every year, >20,000 Australian women develop gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), placing them at risk of long-term cardiovascular disease. We hypothesised that lipid dysregulation would be evident in early gestation in women who later develop GDM. Identifying these changes could lead to early detection of GDM and its sequelae. Methods: Using Liquid-Chromatography Electrospray Ionization Tandem Mass Spectroscopy we examined lipid profiles (>700 species) in early pregnancy (12-15 weeks' gestation) plasma samples from women at low-risk (n = 217) or high-risk (n = 84) for GDM (HREC:/51952/MonH-2019-169657). The two cohorts were then compared using a fixed effect meta-analysis. Results: Seven hundred and thirty-four unique lipid signatures were quantified within each of the 301 plasma samples. Regression analyses (adjusting for maternal age and BMI) with 2-hour glucose values obtained during an oral glucose tolerance test at 26-28 weeks' identified 168 significant correlations between lipid concentration in the first trimester and insulin resistance in the second trimester (P > 0.0.05, Benjamini Hochberg adjusted). Notable associations included increases in diacylglycerols and triacylglycerols with increasing glucose values and decreases to saturated and monounsaturated ether lipids, alkylphosphatidylcholine and alkenylphosphatidylcholine species. These associations were present in both the low-and high-risk cohorts. Conclusions: Preliminary results suggest a relationship between maternal lipids in the first trimester, insulin resistance in the second trimester and the development of GDM. As these relationships were present in both the low-and high-risk cohorts potential screening of glycerolipids and ether lipids in the first trimester may be a useful tool for early prediction of GDM for all women.
