N2  - Background: Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is associated with higher risk for developing diabetes (DM). Breastfeeding (BF) is protective for the development of type 2 diabetes after gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM). BF may mediate GDM risk in a subsequent pregnancy. Methods: Multisite cohort study in Western Sydney of women with one previous GDM pregnancy. Health and breastfeeding information were surveyed. Outcome measures: diagnosis of GDM (ADIPS 1998 criteria) and glucose tolerance test (GTT) results in a subsequent pregnancy. Results: Of the 211 women eligible, 73% (n = 153) were diagnosed with GDM in their second pregnancy. Demographics were similar between GDM groups except for higher BMI (p = 0.03). A lower mean GTT was found over fasting, 1 hr and 2 hr results for both BF > 6 months (p = 0.03) and high intensity BF (p = 0.03). In adjusted analysis high intensity BF had lower 2 hr GTT result (p = 0.03). We found >6 months BF was associated with an 18% reduction in the risk of GDM (RR 0.82, CI 0.68-0.97). After adjusting for confounders the relationship was attenuated (RR 0.89, CI 0.76-1.03). Conclusions: This is the first study we are aware of to demonstrate that a greater intensity of BF can improve glycaemia in pregnant women who have a previous GDM affected pregnancy. BF may reduce incidence of GDM and subsequently future DM and cardiovascular disease risk.
