N2  - Background: Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (HDP) are associated with at least double the cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk within 10 years of an affected pregnancy. In a nationally representative Australian population, this study aimed to estimate relative and absolute risk of CVD after HDP, including interaction with other pregnancy complications and 'traditional' CVD risk factors. Methods: Retrospective cohort of women with singleton pregnancies in New South Wales Jan 2002-Dec 2016, linking population health administrative datasets including for maternity and hospital admissions. The exposure was new-onset HDP (preeclampsia/ eclampsia and gestational hypertension) and outcome hospitalisation or death due to cardiac disease or stroke. Covariates included demographics and other pregnancy complications such as gestational diabetes (GDM), small-for-gestational-age and preterm birth. Results: Of the 528 106 women, 10.3% experienced HDP in their first pregnancy. Ten-year estimated absolute CVD risk was 5.5 per 1000 women after any HDP versus 2.1/1000 if no HDP, and 15.9/1000 after early-onset HDP. CVD event rate (adjusted model) was 2.5 times higher after late-onset HDP and 4.9 times higher after early-onset HDP. Combinations of pregnancy risk further increased future CVD likelihood: after GDM plus lateonset or early-onset HDP respectively, women had a 3.8-fold risk and 7.4-fold risk of CVD, while those with pre-term birth plus late-onset or early-onset HDP had a relative risk of 5.2 and 10.1 respectively. Maternal smoking added to risk. Conclusions: HDP conferred a clear increase in risk for premature CVD. Synergistic increases in risk were noted with both other pregnancy complications and traditional cardiovascular risk factors.
