N2  - Objective: Indonesia ranks 6th of the countries in the world with highest number of people having diabetes mellitus (DM), and 70 % of them are not aware of it. To prevent health and socio-economic consequences of DM, it is very important to detect in its early development phase, especially in high risk population. We intended to obtain prevalence of prediabetes and type 2 DM in office workers with risk factors, in capital city Jakarta. Methods: We collected data on men and women, age 35 to 55 years, actively working in the office, body mass index (BMI) >23.0 kg/m2. Subjects had 1 (or more) of history of DM in the family, or gestational diabetes or gave birth to baby weighing >4 kg, physical inactivity, hypertension, and or cardiovascular disease. Subjects must not have history of DM, elevated blood glucose, or consumption of medication which affect blood glucose. Hypertension determined through history or physical examination. Exercise and sedentary lifestyle from questionnaire. Fat mass examined using SECA mBCA514. Dependent vari1ces ar1 asting plasma glucose (FPG) and hemoglobin A (HbAc). We are using Stata16 to analyze data by logistic regression. Results: Completed data comprised of 105 total subjects, 37 (35.24%) male, 68 (64.76%) female. Mean age 40.15 (SD 6.7) years. Mean BMI 28.9 (SD 4.64) kg/m2. Waist circumference in male 101.48 (SD 11.17) cm, 94.59% had circumference >90 cm. Waist circumference in female 91.06 (SD 8.57) cm, 91.18% had circumference >80 cm. Fat mas percentage 39.20 (SD 7.53) %. Family history of DM found in 43.81%. Hypertension in 16.19%. Under exercise in 93.33%. Sedentary lifestyle in 80.95 %. FPG 89.62 (SD 24.15) mg/dL, maximum 306 mg/dL. HbA1c 5.57 (SD 1.02) %, maximum 13.1%. Five (4.76%) subjects had FPG 100-125 mg/dL. Two (1.9%) subjects had FPG >126 mg/dL. HbA1c 5.7-6.4% found in 29 (27.62%) subjects. HbA1c >6.5% found in 4 (3.81%). Two (1.9%) subjects had FPG >126 mg/dL and HbA1c >6.5%. Three (2.86%) subjects had both FPG 100 - 125 mg/dL and HbA1c 5.7 - 6.4%. For DM based only on HbA1c, odds ratio (OR) of age = 1.102 (95% CI 1.03-1.17), OR of hypertension = 3.0 (95% CI 1.03-8.67), OR of waist circumference = 1.05(95%CI 1.009-1.097), each with p value <0.05. OR of family history, under exercise, BMI, and fat mass percent were not significant. No risk factors were significantly correlated with DM based on FPG alone. Discussion/Conclusion: Among 105 “healthy” workers, we found3.8% DM and 27.6% prediabetes utilizing HbA1c criteria. Using FPG level, the prevalence of DM was only half of that, and the number of prediabetes was much less. Only 1.9% of subjects had concordant criteria of both FPG and HbA1c for diagnosis of DM. HbA1c might be more sensitive but less specific than FPG, in this population. FPG usually diagnose less people with prediabetes or DM, compared to 2 hours post load glucose. Hypertension, waist circumference and age factors were significantly correlated with diabetes. Unexpectedly, sedentary lifestyle, under exercise, and BMI didn't have correlation. These could be due to relatively young age, active workers, and small numbers of subjects. This study may raise awareness of diabetes and pre-diabetes in asymptomatic office worker population.
