N2  - Introduction: Pregnancy in renal transplant recipients poses many challenges. There is paucity of data on the immunosuppressive protocols during pregnancy, maternal and foetal outcomes and long- term impact on graft survival. This study aimed at analysing pregnancy outcomes in renal transplant recipients. Methods: It was an observational, retrospective, cohort study including all women renal transplant recipients in the reproductive age group of 15-45 years, between years 1999 and 2019. Pregnancy outcomes in the form of live births, spontaneous abortions, medical termination of pregnancy (MTP) and intra-uterine death (IUD) were determined. Factors favouring successful pregnancy outcome and effect of pregnancy on graft function were analysed. Results: Out of a total of 1097 renal transplants, 201 women were in the child bearing age group. There were 14 pregnancies identified during a mean duration of follow up of 9.75 ± 4.34 years post-transplant. The mean dialysis vintage of this cohort was 5.57 ± 2.2 months. At the time of conception, all the women were on prednisolone, 12 patients (85.7%) were on azathioprine and four (28.5%) had changed from mycophenolate mofetil to azathioprine, 3.5 months before conception. The mean age at conception was 26.53 ± 2.64 years and the median time interval between transplant and conception was 5 [IQR: 3.12, 7.5] years. Maternal outcome as follows: Pre-ecclampsia was detected in 2 participants (14.2%), anaemia in 4 patients (28.5%) and none of the recipients had gestational diabetes. Post-partum complications - graft pyelonephritis, post-partum depression and varicella with breast abscess were noted in 1 participant each. Mean duration of breastfeeding was 7.25 ± 2.37 months and median time of resumption of menstrual cycles was 4 [IQR: 2.25, 5.75] months. Graft outcome: Acute graft rejection occurred in 1 patient and graft dysfunction in 5 (35.7%) during pregnancy or post-partum period. Difference between the pre-conception mean serum creatinine (1.18 ± 0.56 mg/dL) and post-delivery mean serum creatinine (1.27± 0.48 mg/dL) was statistically significant (p = 0.001). Pregnancy outcome: Nine women had live births (64.2%), out of whom 4 had caesarean section and the rest 5 of them had miscarriages (35.8%) with 2 spontaneous abortions, 2 MTP and 1 IUD. Foetal outcome as follows: Premature birth (<37 weeks of gestation) was seen in 7 newborns (77.7%) and the mean gestational age at birth was 35.22 ± 1.85 weeks. Six neonates (66.6%) were of low birth weight (< 2.5 kg) and the mean birth weight was 2.2 ± 0.32 kg. Perinatal survival rate was 100%. Univariate analysis revealed pre-conception serum creatinine and the change in serum creatinine level during pregnancy to be significant determinants of pregnancy outcome. After a median follow-up of 10 years, mean serum creatinine of good pregnancy outcome group was 1.26 ± 0.6 mg/dL and the median serum creatinine of poor pregnancy outcome group was 1.7 [IQR: 1.2, 1.7] mg/dL. Prevalence of hypertension and proteinuria were 35.7% and 42.8% respectively. [Formula presented] [Formula presented] [Formula presented] Conclusions: The live birth rate was 64.2% in our cohort of renal transplant recipient mothers. Risk factor for poor pregnancy outcome was baseline serum creatinine ≥ 1.4mg/dL in our study.
