N2  - Objective: Using data obtained through maternal questionnaire, medical records, and neuroimaging assessment, we aimed to develop a working classification of the most common causal pathways seen in children with cerebral palsy (CP). Design: Cross-sectional study. Method: Participants were 222 biological mothers of children with prenatally or perinatally acquired CP who were born in the Australian state of Victoria between 1999 and 2008 (mean age 15 years, 0 months [SD 2 years, 8 months]; 132 males; GMFCS: 78 level I, 56 level II, 25 level III, 32 level IV, 31 level V). Mothers were invited to participate through the Victorian Cerebral Palsy Register. Those enrolled completed an online or paper-based questionnaire and consented to the collection of birth and neuroimaging data pertaining to their child from obstetric and paediatric hospital records. Using chi-square analysis, antecedent variables were assessed for their strength of association with neuroimaging patterns classified according to the MRI Classification System (MRICS). Results: No antenatal variable (pregnancy-induced hypertension/ preeclampsia, antepartum haemorrhage, gestational diabetes, fetal growth restriction, prescribed or illicit drug use, other relevant maternal conditions or pregnancy complications), either separately or in combination, was strongly associated with imaging classification (all p>0.1). On the other hand, birth gestation (<35 vs 35+ weeks; p<0.001) and intrapartum variables (sentinel event, indicator of fetal distress, delivery method, resuscitation, 5-minute Apgar score, time to establish regular respirations), either separately or in combination, were preferentially associated with specific neuroimaging patterns (all p<0.002). Neonatal wellbeing (care requirement, presence of neonatal seizures; both p<0.001) and dysmorphic features (p=0.001) also discriminated between neuroimaging patterns. Based on the associations found on univariable analysis, seven pathways related to prenatally or perinatally acquired CP were identified: 1) Structural brain maldevelopment, 2) known or presumed genetic/syndromic cause without evidence of structural brain maldevelopment, 3) neonatal immaturity and predominant white matter injury, 4) predominant white matter injury in term or late preterm infants, 5) neonatal encephalopathy near term and predominant grey matter injury consistent with peripartum hypoxia-ischaemia, 6) perinatal arterial ischemic stroke, 7) other known pathways, for example, kernicterus. Conclusion: Based on frequently observed imaging patterns in CP, our new classification of causal pathways will provide a common terminology for clinicians, researchers, and families. It will also enable more in-depth analysis of pathways and risk factors within subgroups that are more aetiologically homogeneous and will facilitate identification and monitoring of preventive strategies.
