N2  - Objective: Given the paucity of data on management of isolated maternal fever in labor, ACOG recommends consideration of antibiotics, allowing clinical discretion. We sought to assess outcomes in women with a single isolated maternal fever treated with antibiotics vs. expectant management. Study Design: This is a retrospective cohort study of laboring women with a singleton gestation at term and a single temperature between 38.0° and 38.9°C at a tertiary teaching hospital from January 1, 2015 to December 31, 2018. We used a validated, prospectively created database to identify women. Exclusion criteria were: leukocytosis > 15,000/mm3, fetal tachycardia, malodorous amniotic fluid, suspected alternate source of infection, rheumatic or renal disease, non-gestational diabetes, preterm labor, abruption, vaginal bleeding, HIV, malpresentation, and fetal anomalies. The primary outcome was postpartum fever. Secondary outcomes were treatment for postpartum endometritis, uterine atony, postpartum hemorrhage, NICU admission, 5 minute Apgar < 7, and neonatal antibiotic administration. Results were compared using descriptive statistics. Results: 359 women were identified; 85 received antibiotics and 274 did not. Baseline characteristics were similar between groups. Postpartum fever rate was insignificantly lower in the antibiotic group (10.59% vs. 18.98%, p = 0.072). Significantly fewer women in the antibiotic group were treated for postpartum endometritis (3.53% vs. 11.31%, p = 0.034). NICU admission and neonatal antibiotic administration rates were twice as high in the antibiotic group (41.18% vs. 17.88%, p = < 0.001 and 36.47% vs. 12.41%, p = < 0.001). 5 minute Apgar < 7 was more common in the antibiotic group (8.25% vs. 2.19%, p = 0.016). Conclusion: While there was a lower rate of treatment for endometritis in women receiving antibiotics for a single isolated maternal fever, there was a higher rate of adverse neonatal outcomes. Further research is needed to evaluate neonatal risk or other clinical factors leading to antibiotic treatment of certain women perceived to be at higher infectious or fetal risk than others. [Formula presented] [Formula presented]
