N2  - Objective: The impact of pre-gestational diabetes on maternal health outcomes in the postpartum period is unclear. Our objective was to evaluate the risk of postpartum readmission and describe the severe maternal morbidity (SMM) associated with pre-gestational diabetes during postpartum readmission. Study Design: This was a cross-sectional analysis using the National Inpatient Sample (2007-2013). Subjects were women aged 10 to 60 years with delivery and postpartum admissions. SMM was defined per the CDC criteria; we evaluated composite SMM and each morbidity indicator separately. We compared postpartum readmission and SMM rates among women with and without pre-gestational diabetes using Chi square and logistic regression. We controlled for maternal comorbidities using a previously validated comorbidity index with the exclusion of pre-gestational diabetes itself. Results: The postpartum readmission rate without pre-gestational diabetes was 2.1% (5,605,850 deliveries and 115,094 postpartum readmissions). The rate of postpartum readmission was significantly higher in women with pre-gestational diabetes at 7.3% (52,097 deliveries and 3,791 postpartum readmissions), p < 0.05. The rate of SMM was significantly greater in women with pre-gestational diabetes, 27.0% versus 17.9% (p< 0.05). In women with pre-gestational diabetes, the unadjusted OR for SMM during readmission was 1.71 (95% CI 1.56 – 1.83). After adjusting for comorbidity index, the OR was 1.09 (95% CI 1.003 – 1.17). When each morbidity indicator was analyzed individually, women with pre-gestational diabetes had increased odds of acute myocardial infarction and aneurysm, acute renal failure, ARDS, disorders of cardiac arrest, pulmonary edema and heart failure, sepsis, shock, temporary tracheostomy and ventilation (Figure 1; p < 0.05). Conclusion: Women with pre-gestational diabetes were more likely to be readmitted postpartum and suffer from increased morbidity when a readmission occurs. These results indicate that women with pre-gestational diabetes warrant specialized counseling and follow up in the postpartum period. [Formula presented]
