N2  - A 1-day-old girl, born at 38 weeks' gestation to unrelated parents, via uncomplicated vaginal delivery, was referred with a scar-like chest-wall defect noted after birth. She had a history of maternal gestational diabetes treated with metformin and normal antenatal anomaly scans. Examination revealed a 10- cm supraumbilical midline raphe extending to the sternum with two small open areas at each end, no haemangiomas and a palpable sternum. Systemic clinical examination and ophthalmology screen were normal. Due to concerns regarding potential congenital syndromes, she was extensively investigated. Chest X-ray and echocardiogram showed no sternal separation and a structurally and functionally normal heart. Ultrasound scans of the chest wall, head and abdomen were normal. Magnetic resonance imaging with angiogram of the head showed no posterior fossa or vascular anomalies. Her skin defects were managed conservatively and healed well. Supraumbilical raphe is a ventral nonunion defect occurring due to abnormal fusion of the layers of the skin. It usually occurs as part of congenital syndromes involving ventral-wall defects such as PHACES syndrome (posterior fossa malformations, haemangiomas, arterial anomalies, aorta coarctation, cardiac defects, eye abnormalities, supraumbilical raphe or sternal cleft), sternal malformation-vascular dysplasia syndrome and pentalogy of Cantrell (ectopia cordis, intracardiac defects, sternal cleft, omphalocele, pericardial defect). Isolated congenital sternal malformation can occur but it usually involves a sternal cleft with normal overlying skin and soft tissues. We present a rare case of isolated supraumbilical raphe without haemangiomas or extracutaneous anomalies and discuss the need for ongoing follow-up to monitor for any developing syndromic features.
