N2  - In recent years, great attention has been devoted to the genetic mechanisms able to affect the individual susceptibility to common diseases such as cardiovascular diseases, diabetes and cancer, grouped together under the definition of “Chronic Non Communicable Diseases” (NCS). Although lifestyle is considered to play a crucial role in such susceptibility, several gene variants involved in the metabolism of food have been demonstrated to affect the individual risk to develop NCD. The study of these variants (“nutrigenetics”), could represent a very powerful tool in the prevention of NCD, as evidenced by data collected in pregnant women suggesting that the presence of a single SNPs within the TCF7L2 gene is able to increase the risk of gestational diabetes with a 2.5 OR. However, in addition to nutrigenetics, very recently further studies have focused on the concept of “nutrigenomics”, that is the modification in gene expression mediated by diet. It has been demonstrated that early nutritional experiences, and even pre-natal or pre-conceptional parental diet, can affect not only the health of the embryo, but also the long-life health after birth, inducing and increased risk of NCD.
