N2  - Introduction: Fetal genetic material crosses the placenta. When intact fetal cells are incorporated into the woman’s body, cellular fetal microchimerism (cFMC) arises. Circulating cFMC is augmented in preeclampsia, a hypertensive pregnancy complication characterized by placental dysfunction. Other diseases associated with placental dysfunction, including pre-gestational and gestational diabetes mellitus (DM), may also impact cFMC acquistion. Following pregnancy, cFMC can persist for decades in maternal blood and tissues. Fetal cell gene expression and maternal-fetal immunological interaction may impact maternal health. Longterm, cFMC is associated with enhanced wound healing, and may ameliorate or aggravate autoimmunity, cancer, and potentially cardiovascular disease. Objective/hypothesis: We hypothesize that preeclampsia and placental dysfunction in general are associated with augmented cFMC in maternal circulation 5-8 years postpartum. Methods: Women previously recruited to the Oslo Pregnancy Biobank were included in our postpartum pilot study. Specifically, we included 29 women 5–8 years post index pregnancy (preeclampsia: n = 9, DM ± preeclampsia: n = 9, uncomplicated pregnancy: n = 11). DNA was isolated from the buffy coat of peripheral blood drawn postpartum and from fetal samples acquired during index pregnancy delivery. Fetus-specific polymorphisms (absent in the mother) were identified by high resolution sequencing of the human leukocyte antigen loci DRB1, DQA1, and DQB1. cFMC was detected in maternal blood using validated polymorphism-specific quantitative polymerase chain reaction assays. Results: We demonstrated cFMC in 3/29 (10.3%) postpartum samples. Of those, one was from a woman who had preeclampsia during the index pregnancy, and two from women who had uncomplicated pregnancies. Discussion: Our findings show that cFMC is detectable in maternal blood 5-8 years postpartum. We did not find an association between preeclampsia or DM and persistent circulating cFMC. One potential explanation is sequestration of cFMC in maternal tissues. Our pilot study cohort is, however, too small to reject our hypothesis. A study in a larger postpartum cohort is underway.
