N2  - Background and Aims: Impaired fasting glucose (IFG) in adolescents is often associated with obesity, family history of diabetes, and maternal/gestational diabetes. We aimed to assess possible risk factors for IFG in Greek nonobese adolescents, without known family history of diabetes/gestational diabetes. Methods: We examined 100 adolescents (50 boys/50 girls, 15.9±0.3 years), 40 hospitalized for minor problems. BMI was 21.5±1.6Kg/m2. IFG was defined by the Hellenic Diabetes Association guidelines (fasting glucose 100-125mg/dl). Demographic, clinical characteristics, smoking, and dietary habits were assessed. Results: IFG was diagnosed in 49 adolescents (median fasting glucose 115mg/dl vs. 81mg/dl in normoglycemic adolescents). IFG was positively associated with urban living (P=0.02), night-school (P=0.006), anxiety assessed by Hamilton anxiety scale (P<0.001), smoking (P=0.02), smoking severity (pack/years) (P<0.001), skipping breakfast (P=0.01), everyday consumption of white bread (P=0.03), consumption of red meat (>2 times/week) (P=0.03), fast-food (P<0.001), and sweets (P<0.001). IFG was negatively associated with rural living (P=0.02), living with elderly relatives (P<0.001), consumption of home-made food (P<0.001), white meat (P=0.01), yellow cheese (P=0.003), cornflakes (P<0.001), and milk (P=0.01). Multivariate regression analysis, after adjustment for age, and gender determined as significant and independent risk factors for IFG: urban living (beta=1.52, P=0.02), smoking (beta=1.98, P=0.03), skipping breakfast (beta=1.10, P=0.002), fast-food (beta=1.48, P<0.001), consumption of red meat (beta=1.72, P=0.01), white bread (beta=1.87, P=0.003), and sweets (beta=1.66, P=0.02), These factors explained 53% of IFG in the studied adolescents. Conclusions: Stress among hospitalized adolescents increases prevalence of IFG. Living conditions, smoking, and dietary habits potentially play a significant role in the development of dysglycemia in nonobese adolescents.
