N2  - Background and Aims: Physical inactivity is a major risk factor for prediabetes in overweight, and obese adolescents. We studied everyday activity and sedentary behavior as a potential risk factor for prediabetes in nonobese adolescents, without known family history of diabetes or gestational diabetes. Methods: We examined 49 adolescents with prediabetes and 51 adolescents without prediabetes (50 boys/50 girls, median age 15.9±0.3 years). BMI was 20.9±1.5Kg/m2 (girls) and 21.2±1.7Kg/m2 (boys). Prediabetes was diagnosed according to the guidelines of the Hellenic Diabetes Association (fasting glucose 100-125mg/dl). Questionnaires were designed and everyday activity, leisure time, and physical exercise were recorded, along with demographic and clinical characteristics. Results: Prediabetes was positively and significantly associated with study hours (3-5 hours/day) (P<0.001), tutorial hours (>10 hours/week) (P<0.001), television watching (3-5 hours/day) (P<0.001), and cellphone use (6-10 hours/day) (P=0.04). Prediabetes was negatively and significantly associated with hours of exercise training (3-5 hours/week) (P=0.003), walking (P=0.02), gym practice (P=0.02), track training (P<0.001), swimming (P=0.02), ballet dancing (P=0.02), listening to music (P=0.01), less television watching (1-2 hours/day) (P<0.001), sleeping hours (>8 hours/day) (P=0.01), and learning a musical instrument (P=0.001). Multivariate linear regression analysis demonstrated as significant and independent risk factors for prediabetes the hours of exercise training (beta=0.44, P=0.006), television watching (beta=1.73, P=0.005), study hours (beta=1.48, P=0.001), everyday cellphone use (beta=1.11, P=0.003), and sleeping hours (beta=0.16, P=0.05). These factors explained 51% of prediabetes in the studied adolescents. Conclusions: Physical inactivity, lack of sleep, extra study hours, and television watching, as well as cellphone addiction play an important role for development of prediabetes in nonobese adolescents.
