N2  - Background : Fontan circulation is the result of a series of palÂliative operations for patients with congenital heart disease, which a biventricular repair is not possible. Fontan circulation results in high central venous pressure, low cardiac output and hypoxia. Women with a Fontan circulation are known to have a high rate of miscarriage, preterm delivery and fetal growth reÂstriction (FGR). The purpose of the current study is to investiÂgate the placental pathology in patients with a Fontan circulaÂtion. Methods : Five pregnant women with Fontan circulations that delivered at our institution between 2009 and 2018 were retrospectively reviewed. The 5 placentas were analyzed histo-pathologically. Results : Maternal median Sp02was94 [range : 91-96] %, central venous pressure was 10 [9-11] mmHg. cardiac index was 2.75 [225-3.67] L/min/m2 and ejection fraction was 68 [44-72] % at pre-pregnancy. The median gestational age at delivery was 34 [30-37] weeks. Obstetric complications included gestational diabetes mellitus (n=l). preterm delivery (n=3) and FGR (n=3, all < 3rd percentile). Fetal/Placental weight ratio was small in all 5 cases of the study, which is seen commonly in cases of FGR. All placental specimens showed histological findAîngs consistent with placental hypoxia and maternal underper-fusion. There was no evidence of decidual vasculopathy, typiÂcally found in hypertensive disorders of pregnancy. ConcluÂsion : Placentas with Fontan circulation exhibited placental hyÂpoxia and maternal under perfusion on histopathological analyÂsis. despite favorable baseline hemodynamic characteristics. These changes may precipitate worse pregnancy outcomes inÂcluding FGR. Further study is needed to clarify the impact of Fontan circulation on pregnancy.
