N2  - Objective In the absence of gestational or pre-existing diabetes, the rates of significant perineal trauma or shoulder dystocia are only modestly increased with large-for-gestational age (LGA) babies. However, in the presence of gestational diabetes these risks are significantly more common. Our objective is to determine the prevalence of gestational diabetes in a group of women not previously diagnosed with diabetes, who were diagnosed as LGA on scan. Design/methods We prospectively looked at all women who were scanned for suspected LGA and were found to have an abdominal circumference (AC) or an estimated fetal weight (EFW) over the 95th centile. These women were asked to perform home blood glucose monitoring (HBGM) twice a day; with a fasting blood glucose target of less than 5.3 and a 1 hours post-main meal target of less than 7.8. Those with two blood glucose readings above target within one week were diagnosed as having gestational diabetes. We look at this in the context of BMI, ethnicity, and treatment required to control blood sugars. A prospective approach enabled us to reliably capture women who met the criteria for our study. In doing so we have been able to calculate a more reliable estimate of the risk of gestational diabetes in women among our population, who are found to be LGA. Results 87 of the 680 (12%) women referred in for suspected LGA were found to have an AC or EFW above the 95th centile. 56 of those 87 (64%) were found to have abnormal blood sugars and subsequently diagnosed with gestational diabetes. Conclusion Although only a small proportion of those suspected clinically to be large for gestational age are actually large, when an AC or EFW is found to be above the 95th centile, 64% will be found to have abnormal blood sugars. Given the Montgomery ruling, HBGM following ultrasound scan evidence of LGA is essential to allow appropriate counselling of women carrying an LGA fetus.
