N2  - Pulmonary edema occurs in only 0.08% of all pregnancies. Only a quarter of these cases are due to cardiac disease. The remaining cases occur due to tocolytic exposure, history of pre-eclampsia and fluid overload. We present a 33-year-old female who was G3P3003 (3 term births, 0 pre-term births, 0 abortions, 3 living children) status-post day 7 from a planned 39-week cesarean section who presented with acute hypoxemic respiratory failure. Her recent pregnancy was complicated by gestational diabetes. Her dyspnea began 2 days after her surgery and progressively worsened. She denied cough, fevers and chills. Physical exam was significant for tachypnea, tachycardia and lower extremity pitting edema. Electrocardiogram showed sinus tachycardia with no ischemic changes. Negative labs included troponin, sputum cultures, influenza nucleic acid amplification and coxsackie titers. Echocardiogram showed a normal systolic function with no diastolic or valvular abnormalities. Chest x-ray showed bilateral interstitial edema. Computed tomography angiography of the chest showed diffuse ground glass opacities with subpleural sparing suggestive of pulmonary edema with no evidence of pulmonary embolism. On further chart review, it was noted that the patient was given magnesium sulfate (MgSO4) due to concern for post-partum pre-eclampsia. She was started on diuretics and placed on non-invasive ventilation (NIV). After 1 day, she was weaned off NIV and then discharged home on room air on hospital day 3.Pulmonary edema in pregnancy is quite rare. Risk factors for pulmonary edema in pregnancy include the use of tocolytic therapy, hypertension, multiple pregnancies, positive fluid balance during delivery and postpartum cardiomyopathy. In our case, the likely etiology was a combination of MgSO4 therapy and history of multiple gestation. Other tocolytics such as nifedipine and terbutaline have also been associated with development of pulmonary edema. The risk of tololytic induced pulmonary edema is increased when more than one agent is given together. For example, one study found higher odds ratio of developing pulmonary edema when nifedipine was given together with MgSO4 than either agent alone. Other studies have found an association between higher doses of tocolytics and development of pulmonary edema. The mean length of time from MgSO4 initiation to the development of pulmonary edema is 2 days. The mechanism of MgSO4 induced pulmonary edema remains unknown. This case highlights the complications associated with tocolytics. Drug induced pulmonary edema must remain in the differential in a patient with normal cardiac function.
