N2  - Introduction: Fetal distress is the decrease or absence of oxygen that must be received by the fetus through the placenta. Chronic cases of suffering are due to some maternal pathology that causes a reduction in the oxygen concentration of the mother to the fetus: severe anemia, respiratory or cardiac pathologies, arterial hypertension, low placental irrigation or gestational diabetes. Even though these problems do not lead to obvious changes in fetal oxygenation during pregnancy, they can occur at the time of delivery, when there is a reduction in placental irrigation caused by uterine contractions. Objective: To report a case of chronic fetal distress in a pregnant woman with chronic hypertension and cardiopathy and review the literature on complications during pregnancy. Methods: The present study uses a bibliographical research reference, in order to seek information and report the case of chronic fetal distress in gestation and its maternal-fetal repercussions. Discussion and conclusions: NCS, 32 years, pregnant, 33 without, G1P0Ao, with chronic arterial hypertension and heart disease: mild aortic insufficiency, mitral and aortic valve calcification, due to reduced resistance of the middle cerebral artery. She performed a corticosteroid in the city of origin and ultrasonography: gestation of 33S, fetal weight 1800 g placenta with reduced extension, reduced average cerebral artery, umbilical artery and normal venous duct. She was referred for follow-up at high risk prenatal care. Realized USG:34s1d, weight 2190 g, venous duct with positive A wave, cerebral vascular resistance with discrete reduction, normal placental vascular resistance. Returns to care at the obstetric center with contractions and outflow of greenish liquid. At the examination: AFU: 31 cm, BCF 171,visualized amniotic fluid with meconium. Indicated cesarean delivery, with single fetus extraction, small for gestational age, placenta with insertion of velerosa cord, malformed, with accessory lobe and umbilical artery only. Centralization of cerebral flow is one of the first mechanisms of fetal adaptation to chronic suffering. In the follow-up by Doppler velocimetry when abnormalities are identified in the umbilical arteries or diagnosis of absent or reverse flow, the action must be immediate so as not to cause fetal compromises.
