N2  - Gestational diabetes (GDM) poses many challenges in pregnant women. However, ketoacidosis is usually only seen in type 1 diabetic patients. We present a 28-year-old primigravida with gestational diabetes who developed ketoacidosis. An ultrasound at 36/40 showed polyhydramnios and large fetal abdominal circumference. Blood sugar monitoring was diagnostic of GDM, and induction of labour was offered at 38/40. She was admitted for prostaglandin induction and was encouraged to hydrate orally as her urine showed 3+ of ketones. Initial blood glucose was unremarkable. Mother and fetus were mildly tachycardic. Following episodes of vomiting, IV fluids were administered. Urinary ketones did not improve. Oxytocin was started following artificial rupture of membranes. Maternal and fetal tachycardia persisted and serum glucose rose. A variable rate insulin infusion was required. Arterial blood gas analysis demonstrated metabolic acidosis with high ketones (8.0 mmol/l). A diagnosis of ketoacidosis was made. Labour progressed slowly and following increasing concern for maternal health she was delivered by emergency caesarean section. Postoperatively, the patient spent 48 hours in the high dependency unit being treated for diabetic ketoacidosis. The baby was treated for hypoglycaemia in NICU and discharged 24 hours later. Postnatally, all serum glucose and antibody tests were normal. She remains well.
