N2  - Objective A case discussion of a woman who experienced sudden deterioration a few hours after an uneventful caesarean section. She needed to be in ITU and survived the crisis eventually after an incidental revelation. It was a near miss. Case description A 23-year-old para 1 with a BMI of 42, DCDA twin pregnancy and diet-controlled gestational diabetes. Background history of complex social issues, chaotic lifestyle, and smoking. An emergency caesarean section was performed at 34 weeks for preterm labour with leading twin in breech presentation. The caesarean section was uncomplicated. The woman became rapidly unwell 12 hours postoperatively with vomiting and epigastric pain and then became drowsy and less responsive. Observations of note were persistent hypothermia and elevated BP. Clinical signs were generalised oedema and bilateral basal crepitations. Inflammatory markers and lactate levels were raised. Chest X-ray and ECG were normal. Despite multidisciplinary input and appropriate treatment for sepsis and PET, the woman became progressively unwell. She developed pleuritic chest pain, drowsiness, hypothermia, tachypnoea with a MEOWS of 9 and developed AKI and metabolic acidosis. A CTPA and CT abdomen and pelvis was performed. Meanwhile, inflammatory markers progressively worsened with WBC of 50,000 and CRP of 245. She was transferred to ITU and intubated and ventilated as she developed ARDS. Conclusion The case highlights the importance of lateral thinking. We will arrive at the diagnosis with subtle clues along the way. Disclaimer: No prizes for guessing the right answer!
