N2  - Background: Pediatric/fetal autopsies can bring closure to grieving parents and concerned clinicians. There are cases where the autopsy itself does not reveal any abnormalities. Our study shows that the placental examination is vital to the fetal autopsy and even more so in an institution with a great number of high-risk pregnancies. It can hold the key to the cause of death even in cases of previously undetermined causes. Design: A pathology database search was done to find all fetal autopsies with gestational age 20 weeks or greater, performed between August 2003-August 2018 at our institution.Undetermined cases in which the placenta was not available for examination were excluded from the review. The gestational age, cause of death, chief disease and placenta findings were recorded. The causes of death were classified according to gestational age in the following categories: fetal, congenital, infection, placental, cord accident, maternal factors, twin and undetermined. The gestational ages were divided as 20-28 weeks, 29-36 weeks and 37-42 weeks.Placental causes include infection, abruption, fetal and maternal vasculopathy and other pathologies native to the placenta.Maternal factors included gestational diabetes, pre-eclampsia/eclampsia or maternal hypertension or other chronic diseases. Results: A total of 676 fetal autopsies were reviewed;406 male and 270 female.There were 335 total cases in the 20-28 weeks group, 214 in the 29-36 week group and 127 in the 37-42 week group.One hundred thirty-six female and 199 males were in the 20-28 week group, 78 females and 136 males were in the 29-36 week group and 56 females and 71 males were in the 37-42 week group. The top three causes of death in the 20-28 week group were related to placenta, fetal and umbilical cord at 40%, 16% and 12% respectively. In the 29-36 week group, the three main causes of death were placenta, undetermined and congenital at 34%, 20% and 16% respectively. The 37-42 week group also showed the three main causes being placenta, undetermined and congenital at 44%, 25% and 15% respectively. In almost half of the cases where the cause of death remained undetermined, the placenta revealed soft signs of intrauterine problems. Conclusions: The placenta was involved in 40% of all cases across all gestational ages. Placental examination is indispensable in all fetal deaths. It can even be helpful in undetermined cases by providing some idea of what may have happened in-utero.
