N2  - Objective: Fetal ECG ST-depression and ST-elevation are associated with fetal cardiac hypoxia. Diabetes is known to affect the fetal heart. The objective of this study was to evaluate whether these ST-changes occur more frequently in fetuses of mothers with pre-gestational diabetes mellitus (Pre-DM) or gestational DM (GDM) as compared to those without (No DM). Study Design: We performed a secondary analysis of a large, multi-centered randomized-controlled trial conducted at 26 US hospitals. Laboring women with singleton pregnancies at 36 weeks gestation or greater had fetal ECG scalp electrodes (Neoventa S31) placed and were randomly assigned to masked or unmasked ST- segment readings. In our analysis, we included subjects with available ECG-waveform analysis and excluded those with major fetal anomalies. Our primary outcome was the frequency of fetal ECG tracings with ST-changes (ST- depression or ST-elevation without ST-depression) compared according to whether women had pre-DM, GDM and No DM. Separate analyses were conducted for the first stage and the second stage of labor. Univariable analyses were performed to compare maternal characteristics across groups. All significant covariates (P<0.05) and duration of ECG monitoring were included in multivariable logistic regression models. Results: Our study included 5,436 eligible women in the first stage of labor and 4,427 who then progressed to the second. Maternal characteristics, several of which differed between groups, are displayed in Table 1. In the adjusted model, ST-depression occurred significantly more frequently in the fetal ECG tracings in the first stage of labor (aOR 2.14, CI 1.08-4.23) in Pre-DM compared to no DM. ST-depression occurred more frequently in GDM compared to no DM, however this difference did not achieve statistical significance (aOR 1.50, CI 1.00-2.26). In the second stage of labor, ST-elevation occurred more frequently in Pre-DM (aOR 1.91, CI 1.06-3.43) but not GDM (aOR 1.12, CI 0.81- 1.56), and ST-depression occurred more frequently in GDM (aOR 2.18, CI 1.08-4.39) as compared to No DM (Table 2). Conclusion: ST-changes in fetal ECG, a marker of fetal cardiac hypoxia, occur more frequently in fetuses of diabetic mothers. The effects of diabetes on the fetal heart are not limited to structural abnormalities. [Figure presented] [Figure presented]
