N2  - Objective: We aimed to investigate cesarean delivery (CD) rate in pregnancies prenatally diagnosed with CHD, factors associated with CD, and adverse postnatal outcomes compared to those delivered vaginally. Study Design: This is a retrospective case-control study of 336 pregnancies diagnosed with CHD between 2011 and 2016. Groups consisted of those delivered vaginally (control), and those via CD (study). Data included maternal, fetal characteristics, and postnatal outcomes (2 years survival).A p value of <0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results: Complete data was obtained from 280 pregnancies of which 49 % (n=139) delivered vaginally, and 51% (n=141) delivered via CD with primary rate being 60%. Most common five indications for CD were: repeat 39% (n=55), abnormal fetal heart tracing 18.4% (n=26), malpresentation 15.6% (n=22), maternal request 11.3% (n=16), and abnormal labor progression 9.2% (n=13). Significant risk factors for CD were: advanced maternal age (P=0.002), obesity (P=0.03), multiple gestation (P=0.02), gestational diabetes (P= 0.002), fetal growth restriction (P= 0.02), and abnormal umbilical artery Doppler (P=0.006). Our data showed that pregnancies with fetal hypoplastic left heart, and hypoplastic right ventricle were significantly associated with obstetrical complications leading to CD (P= 0.007). These pregnancies also noted to have significant: low birth weight (<2500 gm) (P=0.00009), required more postnatal support (P=0.00002), and significantly longer hospital stay (p<0.0000001) compared to vaginal delivery group. Conclusion: Cesarean delivery rate in pregnancies with fetal CHD noted to be as high as 51% (primary CD rate 60%), most of which due obstetrical complications which resulted in significantly complicated and longer postnatal hospital stay compared to those delivered vaginally. [Figure presented]
