N2  - Background Gestational diabetes (GDM) is prevalent and is associated with adverse maternal and foetal outcomes. The authors report the results of a three-year follow-up study of pregnant women with GDM. Methods A retrospective study was performed based on medical records of women with gestational diabetes followed in a Portuguese hospital centre with childbirth from 2015 to 2017. Results 501 pregnant women with GDM were included. Women's ages ranged from 16 to 44 years (mean 32.75). Most were in the 30 to 35 year age group and nearly a third were over 35. In 2017, there was a considerable reduction of overweighed or obese women (51.3%), however more than half still had a body mass index above normal values. The third trimester glycated haemoglobin value was stable throughout these three years, with a mean of 5.15% (±0.40). Average gestational age at childbirth was 38.58 weeks (±1.42), and 92.3% of women carried their pregnancy to term. Average new-born's weight was 3,192.88g (±490.55). The proportion of new-borns weighing more than 4,000kg at birth averaged 4.2%, ranging from 1.8% in 2015 to 4.9% in 2016. The percentage of low birth weight (less than 2,500 g) has been more stable, averaging 8.0%. More than 80% of the women included in our study where reclassified. At the reclassification appointment, more than 90% presented a normal glucose tolerance test. There was a progressive increase in fasting glucose abnormality diagnoses (0.64%, 1.76% and 2.08% in 2015, 2016 and 2017, respectively), whereas diminished glucose tolerance declined over the period (3.87%, 2.35% and 2.08% in 2015, 2016 and 2017, respectively). 2017 saw the largest proportion of diabetes diagnoses in reclassification appointments (2.58%). Conclusion The number of women who delay pregnancy has been growing over the years, as is shown by our study in which almost third of the sample were over 35 years of age. There is a surprising number of young women overweighed or obese, accentuating the increased cardiovascular risk associated with GDM. Nonetheless, pregnancy outcomes were quite satisfactory, as more than 90% of these women carried their pregnancy to term, and the average rate of macrosomia was less than 5%. Post-partum reclassification of the state of tolerance to carbohydrates is essential, as a history of GDM is a risk factor for GDM in a subsequent pregnancy, as well type-2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease.
