N2  - Diabetes mellitus is common and complicates 2–5% of pregnancies in the UK, with an increasing prevalence of pre-existing diabetes mellitus in pregnancy. Hyperglycaemia directly correlates with adverse maternal and fetal outcomes, including congenital anomaly, miscarriage, pre-eclampsia, pre-term delivery, macrosomia, stillbirth and neonatal hypoglycaemia. Optimal glycaemic control before conception and during the antenatal period is paramount to reduce these risks. Pre-pregnancy preparation should focus on glycaemic control, medication review and end-organ disease assessment. All diabetic women of childbearing age not planning a pregnancy should be offered contraception. Antenatal care should focus on education from a multidisciplinary team. Women should understand the changes that occur in pregnancy and the need for more intense monitoring and tighter glycaemic control. Increased surveillance focuses on end-organ disease (retinal, renal), the development of pre-eclampsia and the presence of fetal complications. The postpartum period is valuable for reiterating the benefits of contraception and planning of future pregnancies. This article focuses on the management of pre-existing diabetes mellitus from pre-conception to the postpartum period.
