N2  - Objective: Women who deliver a preterm or term small-for-gestational age (SGA) infant may be at increased risk for cardiovascular disease, perhaps related to higher blood pressure. While there is evidence that women with a preterm and SGA birth have modestly higher blood pressure (BP) prior to pregnancy and through early adulthood (age 18-40), there is limited evidence on whether these differences persist during the menopausal transition. The purpose of this analysis is to evaluate the impact of selfreported history of preterm and term SGA birth on maternal BP and incident hypertension during the menopausal transition. Design: A longitudinal analysis was conducted with 1772 women (45% white, 29% black, 6% Hispanic, 20% Chinese) from SWAN. Women were included in this analysis if they completed a detailed reproductive history questionnaire at the 13th SWAN visit and had BP data at baseline and at least one other visit. BP and anti-hypertensive use were assessed annually, over a 7-year follow-up. Linear mixed effect models were used to estimate progression of BP in two ways; across 1) chronological years (baseline to 7 years) and 2) years since the final menstrual period (range -16 to +8 years). Generalized estimating equations were used to examine the association between history of preterm and term SGA birth with BP categories (normal, elevated, stage 1 and stage 2 hypertension). In a subsample of women without reported hypertension or anti-hypertensive use at baseline, Cox proportional-hazards regression estimated incident hypertension. Models were adjusted for age at baseline, site, race/ethnicity, financial strain, body mass index, smoking status, physical activty, medications (lipid-lowering, anti-diabetic), parity, and age at first birth. Linear mixed models also adjusted for anti-hypertensive use. Sensitivity analyses were performed excluding women with self-reported hypertension or diabetes during pregnancy. Results: At the baseline SWAN visit, women were on average 47 years old. One hundred ninety-seven women (11%) reported ever having a preterm birth and 164 (9%) reported a prior term SGA birth. Compared to women with all terms births, the odds of elevated blood pressure/hypertension were 1.47 (95% CI: 1.07, 2.01) and 1.48 (95% CI: 1.02, 2.15) times higher for women with a preterm and term-SGA birth, respectively. Systolic BP was significantly higher at the final menstrual period in women with term SGA births (p<0.05), and decreased after the final menstrual period (β=-0.62, p=0.04). History of preterm birth was associated with incident hypertension (HR: 1.46, 95% CI: 1.27, 1.67) over a 7-year follow-up even after excluding women with reported hypertension or diabetes during pregnancy (HR: 1.49, 95% CI: 1.26, 1.76). Term SGA birth was not significantly associated with incident hypertension. Conclusion: This study suggests that preterm birth may increase the risk of incident and prevalent hypertension that occurs with chronological aging. While prior term SGA birth is associated with greater risk of prevalent hypertension in midlife, it is also related to decreasing systolic BP after the final menstrual period. Additional analyses are necessary to examine if rate of change differs across stages of reproductive aging. These findings are important as elevations in blood pressure in these groups of women may be linked to future cardiovascular disease.
