N2  - Increased maternal demand of steroid hormone in normal pregnancy may cause a dyslipidemic changes in maternal lipid profile. Due to association of lipid profile with atherosclerosis and CVD, it is carefully monitored in the non-pregnant populations. But it is not measured in routine obstetrical practices in our country, even no reference range is defined for lipid parameters during normal pregnancy to avoid perinatal complication and long-term effects on maternal health. The aim of the study is to evaluate serum lipid profile in 1st and 3rd trimester of pregnancy in tertiary care hospital. Pregnant women of 1st and 3rd trimester was divided into group 1 and 2 respectively (n = 30) in each group. Non-pregnant women were taken as controls (n = 40). Fasting serum samples were taken, and serum lipid profiles was estimated through kit available commercially. Data was collected and analyzed through Epi- info7. The mean level of serum triglyceride was significantly higher and serum HDL was significantly lower in 1st trimester and 3rd trimester when compared with controls. The mean level of serum total cholesterol and serum LDL were significantly higher in 3rd trimester compared with control while there was no significant difference in mean level of serum total cholesterol and LDL in 1st trimester when compared with control. Dyslipidemia in pregnancy are associated with gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), preeclampsia, preterm birth and other adverse outcomes. Therefore, pregnant women should be screened for lipid profile and dyslipidemia should be identified.
