N2  - Introduction: Gestational diabetes affects approximately 9% of pregnancies in the US. Infants of diabetic mothers (IDM) are at increased risk of postnatal complications including congenital heart disease, hypoglycemia, macrosomia, respiratory disorders, and perinatal mortality. IDM are also at risk of developing septal hypertrophy and diastolic dysfunction. Neonates are known to be a high risk group of cardiac surgical patients. Given these risk factors, we investigated whether postoperative risks of neonatal cardiac surgery are increased in IDM. Hypothesis: We hypothesize that IDM will be at greater risk of post-operative complications following neonatal heart surgery as compared to infants of non-diabetic mothers (non-IDM). Methods: We performed a retrospective chart review from 2012 to 2018. Full term neonates undergoing cardiac bypass procedures were identified via surgical case logs. IDM were identified based on maternal history. Exclusion criteria included history of non-cardiac surgery prior to the cardiac surgery, and transfer to another institution post-operatively. Due to the overall low mortality, expired patients were also excluded from the final analysis. Length of stay (LOS), intubation days, post-operative complications in the IDM vs non-IDM groups were compared. Results: 163 neonatal cardiac surgical patients met inclusion criteria (29 IDM, 134 non-IDM). The groups were similar in single vs biventricular surgeries, and surgical weight. IDM had longer total and post-operative LOS (p=0.038 and 0.036, respectively) and longer intubation time which approached significance (p = 0.09). IDM also had increased risk of tube feeds at discharge (RR 1.82, p = 0.028). There was no statistically significant difference among other variables. Conclusion: IDM have longer LOS and increased risk of being discharged on tube feedings following neonatal heart surgery. Further investigation is needed to examine the factors contributing to these risks.
