N2  - Introduction: Increasing prevalence of maternal obesity and gestational diabetes (GDM) is associated with offspring early weight gain and affects long-term cardiovascular risk. Fetal programming has been hypothesized to be the underlying mechanism. Hypothesis: We aimed to investigate the influence of maternal adiposity and GDM on child body composition and left ventricle mass (LVM) at 6 years of age. Methods: The observational follow-up study included 201 mother-child pairs, a subcohort from the Finnish Gestational Diabetes Prevention Study. Mothers and children were recruited at a mean of 6.1 (+/-0.5) years postpartum aiming for balanced GDM occurrence. Assessment included child echocardiography, child and maternal anthropometrics, body composition by bioelectrical impedance and blood pressure. Results: Maternal lean body mass, body fat mass and body fat percentage were mirrored in offspring. Maternal pre-pregnancy body mass index (BMI 30.5 +/-5.6 kg/m2) correlated with child body fat percentage (r=0.2 p=0.01) and was reflected in increased child BMI (mean Z-score 0.45 +/-0.93 kg/m2). LVM correlated with child and maternal anthropometrics, lean body mass, fat mass, child age, sex, and systolic blood pressure (Table 1). LVM, LVM index (g/m2.16) and LVM z-score were not associated with pre-pregnancy BMI, GDM exposure, or child body fat percentage. In a stepwise linear regression model LVM was independently predicted only by child lean body mass (R2=0.437). LVM index and lean body mass were higher (p<0.001; p=0.002), but body fat percentage lower (p<0.001), in boys compared with girls. Conclusion: Child LVM at six years of age is determined predominantly by child lean body mass, which is influenced by maternal lean body mass. Maternal pre-gestational adiposity is reflected in child adiposity, increasing future cardiovascular risk, but no association between LVM and child adiposity or evidence of fetal programming related to GDM was found in early childhood.
