N2  - Introduction: Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is the most common complication of pregnancy. Children exposed to GDM are at an increased risk for the development of cardiometabolic diseases later in life, though the mechanisms responsible for this observation are unknown. Hypothesis: We hypothesize that fetal exposure to GDM induces cardiomyocyte mitochondrial dysfunction, and concomitant left ventricular (LV) dysfunction with age. Methods: GDM was induced in female rats with a high fat (45% kcal) and sucrose diet prior to mating, throughout pregnancy and lactation. Lean control females received a low fat (10% kcal) diet. Fetal rat ventricular cardiomyocytes (FRVC) were isolated from e20.5 offspring for metabolomic analysis, mitochondrial respiration and calcium transport. Serum metabolites and cardiac transcriptome profiles from 3-month old offspring were measured by liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry and RNASeq, respectively. LV morphology and functionwas assessed throughout the life course of the offspring (e18 to 12-months of age) with transthoracic ultrasound. Results: Offspring exposed to GDM exhibited increased LV posterior wall thickness across their life course (fetal to 12-months of age; p<0.05) and impaired LV filling beginning at 6-months of age (p<0.05), independent of elevated blood pressure. Consistent with the development of diastolic dysfunction in vivo, alterations in calcium flux and sarcoplasmic reticulum-dependent calcium re-uptake (1.5-fold and 1.6-fold greater, respectively) were observed in FRVC isolated from GDM offspring (p<0.05). RNASeq analysis revealed that offspring exposed to GDM exhibit altered expression of calcium handling genes (e.g. ATP2B2 and ATP2A3) at 3-months of age. Basal and maximal mitochondrial oxygen consumption was reduced for glucose (35% & 68%) and fatty acid (49% & 52%) substrates in FRVC isolated from GDM offspring (p<0.05). Indicative of altered mitochondrial metabolism, metabolomic analysis revealed that beta-hydroxybutyrate levels are elevated (2.4-fold, p<0.05) and several citric acid cycle intermediates are reduced in the serum of 3-month old GDM offspring. Conclusions: GDM induced early-life mitochondrial dysfunction, altered contractility and calcium transporter gene expression in the hearts of the rat offspring, in concert with LV hypertrophy and diastolic dysfunction. Our findings identify several mechanisms that link early-life GDM exposure to the development of cardiovascular disease later in life.
