N2  - Objectives: To evaluate the risks, benefits and how to follow up on pregnant women with hyperthyroidism, providing a better outcome to the maternal-fetal binomial. Method: A literature review was carried out in the Pubmed database, using the descriptors “pregnancy” AND “hyperthyreoidism” in the period 2015-2018. A total of 171 articles were selected and after exclusion criteria which was bibliographic revisions, were reached a total of 18 papers related to the theme. Results: Among the complications of pregnancy, hyperthyroidism is present in 0.4-1.0% of pregnancies, being caused mainly by Graves' Disease and Transitory Thyrotoxicosis of Pregnancy. Studies reveal that the disease is related to a high risk of pre-cardiac arrhythmias, gestational diabetes mellitus and hepatic necrosis. For the fetus, there is evidence of intrauterine growth restriction, stillbirth, abortion, and congenital anomalies such as urinary and cervicofacial malformations. Currently, antithyroid drugs are recommended, although they are also related to complications. Conclusions: Early diagnosis and follow-up during prenatal care with the correct medication treatment of pregnant women with hyperthyroidism have a positive effect on the gestational outcome, bringing benefits to the maternal-fetal binomial.
