N2  - Objectives: To report a clinical case of ovarian adenocarcinoma in a successful intrauterine pregnancy. Method: Review of medical records after discharge from the patient and collection of information after the patient's free and informed consent. Results: M.S.N., 41 years, G3 P1 A1), 36 weeks 5 days, gestational diabetes controlled with diet, uterine height 38 cm; uterine dynamics absent; fetal heart rate 144b pm; Vaginal touch: 4 cm, cephalic presentation. Umbilical hernia in the mother containing peritoneal fat and colon; Multilocalized cystic expansive formation with volume of 3337 cm3. Resolution of the gestation by c-section. The histopathological revealed adenocarcinoma on left ovary measuring 27.0×14.0×6.0 cm and weight 1.135 g presenting malignant neoplasia with extensive areas of necrosis without lymphatic and blood or perineural vascular invasion. Conclusions: The pathophysiological factors involved in carcinogenesis of ovarian tissue are: age, parity, breastfeeding, infertility factors, use of ovulation inducing drugs, ethnicity, administration of oral hormonal contraceptives, environmental factors, surgical factors and previous history of pathologies such as endometriosis. In order to investigate malignant epithelial ovarian neoplasia, the serum markers such as CA-125 and ultrasound (transvaginal and pelvic), are recommended for women with a family history of ovarian cancer.
