N2  - Objectives: Optimal management of PROM at term is still evolving. Immediate induction is associated with decreased infectious maternal morbidity and neonatal ICU admission without increasing the LSCS rates.However, immediate induction of using IV oxytocin is associated with restriction of patient's ambulation. Oral misoprostol is inexpensive, easily available, stable at room temperature therefore, easy to store and administer.Hence, this study is being done to compare 50 μg of oral misoprostol with titrated IV oxytocin.The primary outcome is the duration between induction and delivery, vaginal delivery achieved within 24 hrs and the LSCS rate between the 2 groups. We also compared maternal and neonatal morbidity in both groups. Method: Material and methods: Pregnant women at 37 to 41 weeks of gestation with vertex presentation , with definite history of leaking per vagina or confirmed after per speculum examination and reassuring fetal heart rate with no antenatal complications such as preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, previous LSCS and chorioamnionitis were invited to participate in the trial and randomized after informed consent. All maternal and fetal outcome were collected. The sample size of 200 (100 in each group oral misoprostol and IV oxytocin group) was calculated to detect a mean difference of 2 hours in induction to delivery time between two groups. Results: The baseline characteristics were similar in both groups. The mean duration of time interval between induction to delivery was 12.1 hours in oral misoprostol and 11.3 hours in oxytocin group (P value 0.04, 95% Confidence Interval 0.8 [-1.6,3.2]). There was no difference in vaginal deliveries that occurred within 24 hours and caesarean sections in both groups. Total duration of oxytocin infusion was significantly less in the misoprostol group (5.4 hours verses 8.1 hours , p=0.006). Conclusions: Tachysystole was seen in 15.1% of women in the misoprostol group and 6.1% of women in the oxytocin group. There was no difference in the other maternal and neonatal complications. The induction to delivery interval was one hour shorter in the oxytocin group however, total duration of oxytocin infusion was significantly less in the misoprostol group. Tachysystole was more common in the misoprostol group despite withholding the drug when the woman had any contraction. Finding the optimal dose of misoprostol is an area for further research.
