N2  - Objectives: Evaluate the progression of the cardiac function and cardiomyopathy in children of well-controlled diabetic mothers, from fetal period until 28 days of life, through data obtained by doppler echocardiography. Method: 96 echocardiographic exams of 48 children were analyzed, one during the fetal period and the other at the neonatal period of infants of mothers with gestational diabetes mellitus in good clinical control. The fetal and neonatal cardiac variables analyzed were: ventricle septum and posterior wall thickness by M mode, shortening fraction of the left ventricle (systolic function), diastolic function by the E/A relation of the mitral valve and by the TEI index of the left ventricle. Results: During the fetal period exams, the gestacional age was of 32+-3 weeks, the incidence of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy was of 35%, the shortening fraction was normal in all of the fetuses, diastolic disfunction was found in 39,5% of the cases and alteration of the TEI index of the left ventricle, in 64% of the fetuses. On neonatal period the mean age of children was of 18±10 days old, the incidence of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy was 6% (p<0.001), abnormal shortening fraction in 7% of the newborns, alteration of the left ventricle diastolic function in 47% and of the left ventricle TEI index in 57% of the newborns. Conclusions: Sons of well-controlled diabetic mothers during gestational period, even with the reduction of the hypertrophic cardiomyopathy since intrauterine life, keep the abnormal cardiac function parameters.
