N2  - There is an increasing body of evidence that certain complications of pregnancy (preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, spontaneous pre-term birth and small for gestational age baby) are metabolic markers of increased cardiovascular risk for premature ischaemic heart disease in women. Collaboration between Cardiology and Obstetric Units at Northern Adelaide Local Health Network has resulted in the COFFEE Clinic (Cardiovascular assessment after Obstetric complications. Follow-up For Education and Evaluation). This cardiovascular prevention nurse practitioner-led clinic is an Australian-first clinic to educate women who have experienced a complication in pregnancy, and their general practitioners about their need to closely monitor current and future cardiovascular risk. The clinic's effectiveness to increase awareness, educate and encourage lifestyle changes that reduce cardiovascular risk (primordial prevention) as well as to ensure appropriate management of established or developing cardiovascular risk factors (primary prevention) will be assessed in a trial of intervention with counselling versus usual care over the next few years. Other innovative ways to educate and engage with young women to reduce their cardiovascular risk using social media and other internet-based tools are being explored currently. Recognising that this is an important patient cohort, women attending this clinic are being asked to consent to being a part of an ongoing registry for follow-up and a biobank for future research initiatives.
