N2  - Case This is an interesting case of a 35 year old Bengali woman with high risk for trisomy 18 of 1 in 50 (at term) on quadruple testing at 15 + 2 weeks. Amniocentesis was carried out at 16+1 weeks. QF-PCR report was normal for T21 and T13 but uninformative for T18. The microarray result and parental genetic testing confirmed a uniparental disomy (UPD) of chromosome 18 with both copies inherited from the mother. This is a rare finding with the estimated risk to the baby being around 4% due to possibility of low level T18 mosaicism or if the mother carries a recessive gene on chromosome 18. Anomaly scan and fetal echocardiogram were normal and serial growth scans were normal until 36 weeks with a slight decrease in growth velocity on the last scan. She developed gestational diabetes antenatally treated with metformin. She was induced at 37 + 3 and following 1 mg of Prostin® (Pfizer Limited, Sandwich, Kent), proceeded to have a successful vaginal delivery. A female infant was born with Apgars 9, 10, 10 weighing 3035 g. Baby appeared phenotypically normal and was discharged with the mother on day 1 of life and will be followed up with a geneticist at Great Ormond Street Hospital at 3 months of age. UPD occurs where both copies of a chromosome pair are inherited from one parent. Known conditions arising from this are Prader-Willi syndrome and Angelman syndrome (chromosome 15). Our review of the literature found no other reported cases of UPD of chromosome 18.
