N2  - Background Anti NMDA-receptor encephalitis is a rare, serious and potentially fatal condition that could affect young women and recently been associated with ovarian teratoma. Its clinical presentation is non-specific, thus diagnosis and treatment is often delayed. To date, there are only ten Anti NMDA Receptor Encephalitis cases in pregnancy reported worldwide. We present the first case reported in England of Anti NMDA Receptor Encephalitis in pregnancy. Case A 34 year old woman, para one, low risk with no past medical nor mental health problem. She presented with an acute psychotic episode at nine weeks of gestation. Initial investigations including bloods and CT brain were normal. She was sectioned under the Mental Health Act. Following this, she collapsed with a tonic-clonic seizure, went into respiratory failure and episode of bradycardia with loss of cardiac output requiring cardiopulmonary resuscitation. Anti NMDA receptor antibody was found both in her bloods and cerebrospinal fluid. An early pregnancy scan showed a small left ovarian teratoma which was confirmed by a pelvic MRI. Combination of IVIg, steroids, plasmapheresis and removal surgery had improved her condition. She recovered well and carried on with her pregnancy. She developed gestational diabetes as a result of prolonged steroid treatment. However, the remainder of the pregnancy was uneventful and she had normal delivery. The baby was well with no neurological deficit. Conclusion Anti NMDA receptor antibody encephalitis in pregnancy requires multidisciplinary team approach in order to timely diagnose and prompt treatment for best outcome of both mother and baby.
