N2  - Objectives To assess longitudinal changes in maternal haemodynamics amongst pregnant women diagnosed with gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) requiring either metformin or diet therapy in comparison to low-risk healthy controls, and to investigate the effects of metformin. Methods Fifty-six consecutive pregnant women attending their first appointment at the GDM clinic and 60 low-risk healthy pregnant women (control) attending their routine antenatal clinics were recruited and assigned to three groups: GDM metformin (GDM-M), GDM Diet (GDM-D) and Control. Non-invasive assessment of maternal haemodynamics, including arterial stiffness measurements (Arteriograph™ [TensioMed®, Budapest, Hungary]), were undertaken under controlled conditions within four gestational windows: antenatal (AN1) (26-28 weeks), AN2 (32-34 weeks) and AN3 (37-40 weeks), and postnatal (PN) (6- 8 weeks after delivery). Results Results showed evidence of a two-way interaction effect between groups (GDM-D, GDM-M and Control) and stages of gestation (AN1, AN2, AN3 and PN) for maternal haemodynamic parameters: brachial artery augmentation index (AIx) (P = 0.004), aortic AIx (P = 0.008), and central systolic BP (P = 0.001). However, differences in respect of aortic pulse wave velocity (P = 0.001) and heart rate (P < 0.001) were only significant for gestational stage, but not between groups. At AN2, metformin therapy had a potential beneficial effect on AIx, with a non-significant mean difference between GDM-M and control groups (P = 0.15). Conclusion AIx and central systolic BP measures of arterial stiffness are adversely affected by GDM in comparison to controls during pregnancy. The potential beneficial effects of metformin therapy seen at 32-34 weeks of gestation require further exploration in a future intervention trial.
