N2  - Background: Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC) is a disorder that carries an increased risk for sudden cardiac death. Routine genetic testing is often negative, and although whole exome sequencing improves diagnostic accuracy, it may not provide all answers. Case: A 43-year-old female kickboxer with history of sensorineural hearing loss and gestational diabetes presented with chest pain. ECG showed occasional premature ventricular contractions, and T wave inversions, Troponin I was elevated and echocardiography revealed biventricular functional abnormalities. She had normal coronary arteries on angiogram. A medical regimen was started and she was discharged from the hospital wearing a cardiac event monitor. At home she had a presyncopal event during a period of sustained ventricular tachycardia. She ultimately received an implantable cardioverter defibrillator for secondary prevention. Decision-making: Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging was performed to assess for an infltrative process, revealing global right ventricular hypokinesis and paradoxical interventricular septal motion with increasing suspicion for ARVC. Commercial panel testing for 13 common ARVC genes was performed. This initial panel testing was negative. As such, whole exome sequencing was performed and also failed to return likely causal variants. However, mitochondrial genome assessment revealed a known pathogenic mutation in MT-TL1 (m.3243A>G). MT-TL1 has been associated with dilated cardiomyopathy, but not previously with ARVC-like changes. Her children also tested positive for the MT-TL1 mitochondrial mutation, for which genetic counseling is underway. Conclusion: Devastating arrhythmias can be the frst manifestation of ARVC. Thorough genetic testing is prudent when suspicion is high. Here, we present the frst described case of an ARVC-like cardiomyopathy due to a mitochondrial gene mutation in MT-TL1.
