N2  - OBJECTIVE: Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM; at least two abnormal values on the 100-g oral glucose tolerance test [OGTT] during pregnancy) is associated with an increased risk of diabetes mellitus (DM) later in life. It is less clear if women with only one abnormal value are also at risk. We therefore evaluated whether one abnormal value on the 100-g OGTT also incurs a risk of DM. STUDY DESIGN: Women with an abnormal 50-g screen and a fasting glucose less than 95 mg/dL were enrolled in a mild GDM treatment trial (abnormal 3-hr 100-g OGTT) and a concurrent observational non-GDM cohort (normal 3-hr 100-g OGTT) and were followed up 5-10 years later. Women did not have a prior history of GDM or DM, chronic hypertension, or renal or cardiovascular disease at the time of enrollment. At follow-up, women had a 2-hr 75-g OGTTand provided blood for central laboratory glucose measurements. DM was defined as currently treated for DM or meeting the American Diabetes Association criteria for DM based on an abnormal OGTT. Relative risks and 95% confidence intervals were estimated from multivariable log binomial regression models. RESULTS: This analysis included 829 women (48% of eligible women from the original study) who were followed up 7±1 years after the index pregnancy and were not pregnant at the time of follow-up. Mean age at follow-up was 36±6 years. Overall, 6.4% had DM at follow-up. Compared with women with no elevated values on OGTT during pregnancy,mildGDMwas associated with a greater risk ofDM, however having had just one elevated value on the 100-g OGTT during pregnancy, and therefore not meeting a GDM diagnosis, was not associated with a greater risk ofDMby 5-10 years after the index pregnancy (Table). CONCLUSION: Women experiencing only one abnormal value on the 100-g OGTT during pregnancy do not appear to have the same risk of subsequent DM by 5-10 years after pregnancy as women with mild GDM. (Table Presented).
