N2  - OBJECTIVE: Abnormal fetal growth has been linked to adult obesity, insulin resistance, hypertension, and cardiovascular disease. Data regarding newborn size for gestational age and cardiometabolic health in childhood are conflicting. We therefore sought to evaluate the association between birthweight percentiles and cardiometabolic health in childhood. STUDY DESIGN: Secondary analysis of children born to women enrolled during pregnancy with mild gestational diabetes mellitus or lesser degrees of glucose intolerance. Pregnancy outcomes were collected during the original study. Size for gestational age was based on birthweight, gestational age at delivery, sex, and maternal race/ethnicity per Alexander to categorize as: small for gestational age (SGA; less than 10th percentile), appropriate for gestational age (AGA; 10th-90th percentile; reference group), and large for gestational age (LGA; greater than 90th percentile). At 5-10 years after birth, children were examined (weight, height, waist circumference, blood pressure) and fasting blood samples were obtained to determine high-density lipoprotein cholesterol, triglycerides, glucose, and homeostatic model assessment of insulin resistance. Log binomial or log Poisson regression models were used to estimate relative risks and 95% confidence intervals for abnormal cardiometabolic values. RESULTS: This analysis included 979 children (59 SGA, 815 AGA, 105 LGA) who were followed up at a mean age of 7±1 (SD) years. Compared with the AGA-born children, LGA- and SGAborn children were 2-3.5 times more likey to have elevated diastolic blood pressure. The LGA-born chidren were also more likely to have high BMI indicative of being overweight or obese (Table). The other cardiometabolic markers were similar between groups. CONCLUSION: Lower and higher birthweight percentiles were both associated with an increased risk of elevated diastolic blood pressure at 5-10 years of age. The higher birthweight category was also associated with a modest increased risk of being overweight or obese. However, no associations were observed for lipids, glucose metabolism or insuln resistance. (Table Presented).
