N2  - OBJECTIVE: To determine the effect of mild gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) on the children's proteome profile. STUDY DESIGN: This is a secondary analysis of a follow-up study of children of women enrolled in a multicenter trial of treatment versus no treatment of mild GDM. Plasma samples from male and female offspring from untreated GDM (n=12 and n=17, respectively) and non GDM (n=30 and n=22, respectively) at 5-10 years of age were assessed by LC/MS/MS. The raw data files were processed using Thermo ScientificTM Proteome DiscovererTM software against the IPI human 3.87 database (Mascot search engine). Peptides identified in at least 75% of samples in each group were included in the analysis. Data were expressed as spectra intensity. For the common peptides, ratio in spectra intensity in untreated group over non GDM group was converted to fold increase/decrease by Ingenuity Pathways Analysis (IPA). The final results were analyzed for associations with biological functions against the IPA database. Righttailed Fisher's exact test was used to calculate a P-value (P<.05). RESULTS: The IPA identified 96 peptides common to untreated and non GDM in males and 100 in females, most associated with developmental and hereditary disorders and immunological diseases. The top upregulated proteins were transthyretin (1.4 fold increase) in males and insulin like growth factor binding protein acid labile subunit (1.7 fold increase) in females. In untreated GDM males, we found 10 unique peptides related to cardiovascular disease (CVD), hematological disease and hereditary disorder. Eight peptides unique to non GDM males were associated with humoral immune and inflammatory responses and CVD (Figure A). In females from untreated GDM mothers, we found 9 unique peptides which were related to cancer, CVD and endocrine system disorders. In the females from non GDM mothers 4 unique peptides were related to cellular function and maintenance, developmental and hereditary disorders (Figure B). CONCLUSION: In utero exposure to insulin resistance and hyperglycemia programs the fetus for upregulation of proteins associated with metabolic pathways in childhood. This finding further highlights the adverse consequences of untreated mild GDM. The unique programmed peptides we identified could possibly guide future therapies to prevent altered developmental programming resulting from GDM.
