N2  - OBJECTIVE: To determine recent national trends in prevalence of concurrent cancer and pregnancy and ascertain associated medical and obstetric outcomes. STUDY DESIGN: We conducted a retrospective analysis of National Inpatient Sample (NIS), the largest all-payer inpatient health database in the United States (US). We identified delivery admissions from 2004-2013 with a concurrent diagnosis of cancer using ICD-9 codes. Demographic data were compared with Pearson's Chisquared or t-tests. Multivariable logistic regression was used to test the change in prevalence of concurrent cancer and pregnancy over time and to compare outcomes between deliveries with and without cancer. Trends in cancer survivorship were analyzed. All analyses were adjusted for NIS-provided population weights and strata. RESULTS: During the 10-year study period, data were available for 8,336,990 deliveries. The prevalence of concurrent cancer and pregnancy increased from 3.41/10,000 deliveries in 2004 to 4.33/ 10,000 in 2013 (Figure 1). This increasing trend was significant over time, including after adjustment for maternal age (aOR 1.03 [1.01-1.04]). Women with cancer were more likely to be white (60.5% vs 53%, p=0.0002), older (30.7 vs 28.0 years, p<0.0001), and have private insurance (58.8% vs 50.1%, p<0.0001). Cancer survivorship at delivery (Figure 2) also increased (aOR 1.07 [1.06-1.08]) with minimal overlap (0-1.6% of concurrent cases overlapped with survivorship cases yearly). Women with cancer were more likely to experience transfusion (OR 5.02 [3.83-6.58]) and one or more of the following medical conditions: death, ventilation, cardiac arrest, sepsis, or acute respiratory or renal failure during delivery (OR 10.8 [6.7-17.3]). Women with cancer more often had obstetric complications, including preterm labor (OR 2.7 [2.2-3.2]), gestational diabetes (OR 1.4 [1.1-1.8]), hypertensive disorders (OR 1.3 [1.02-1.6]), cesarean section (OR 1.5 [1.3-1.7]), and postpartum hemorrhage (OR 1.5 [1.1-2.0]). These associations remained significant after adjustment in a multivariable logistic regression model. CONCLUSION: The prevalence of concurrent cancer and pregnancy in the US increased from 2004-2013. This increase was not explained by maternal age, nor by cancer survivorship, which also increased over this interval. Pregnant women with cancer are more likely to experience medical or obstetric complications during their delivery admission. (Figure Presented).
