N2  - OBJECTIVE: To evaluate the association of 3 hours 100g Glucose ToleranceTest (GTT) results and the development of future diabetesmellitus. STUDY DESIGN: Retrospective cohort study of women between 18 to 45 years who underwent a 3 hours GTT during pregnancy and delivered in a tertiary affiliated medical center between the years 2007-2014. According to GTT values, patients were divided into three groups: (A) Normal GTT; (B) Impaired Gestational Glucose Tolerance (single pathological GTT value, IGT) and (C) Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (two or more pathological values, GDM). Future diagnosis of diabetes mellitus was recorded among the groups. RESULTS: 1) Overall, 5011 parturients who underwent a 3 hour GTT were detected and followed for a median period of 64 months (interquartile range of 32). 2) Compared with controls, women with GDM or IGT tended to be older, had a higher rate of ART treatment, were less likely to be primipara and had a higher rate of multiple pregnancy (table 1). When pregestational cardiovascular risk factors were compared, women with GDM and IGT had a higher rate of obesity and hyperlipidemia compared to controls, but rates of chronic hypertension, ischemic heart disease and cerebrovascular disease were not different. Future diabetes mellitus was diagnosed at a higher rate in women with GDM or IGT (7.0% for GDM, 2.7% for IGT compared with 0.6% for normal GTT, p<.001). Using a survival multivariable analysis (Fig. 1), GDM and IGT were significantly associated with a higher rate of future diabetes (adjusted HR 3.7 for IGT and 12.2 for GDM, P<0.001). In a sub-analysis of the IGT group, all single pathological values were found to be associated with a higher chronic DM rate, though abnormal fasting glucose had the highest rate (8.8% compared with 5.8% for GTT-60, 5.5% for GTT-120 and 6.6% for GTT-180). CONCLUSION: Our study shows that both IGT and GDM during pregnancy are independently associated with a significant higher rate of future diabetes mellitus, although their effect is influenced by other contributing factors. (Table Presented).
