N2  - OBJECTIVE: Our objectives were to examine the risk factors associated with chorioamnionitis among women undergoing an induction of labor with an unfavorable cervix and standardized labor protocol. STUDY DESIGN: We performed a planned secondary analysis of a randomized trial that compared time to delivery among four different induction methods. Women with a singleton gestation, intact membranes, and an unfavorable cervix (Bishop score of ≥6 and dilation ≥2) who were undergoing a term (≤37 weeks) induction were included. Induction and latent/active labor management were by standardized protocols for all participants, with close attention to the rate of cervical change and recommended interventions if labor was not progressing. Our primary outcome of chorioamnionitis was defined as maternal fever in the presence of maternal or fetal tachycardia or fundal tenderness. Chi-square was used to compare categorical variables. Mann Whitney U test was used to compare non-parametric data. Independent risk factors for chorioamnionitis were determined by multivariable logistic regression adjusting for confounders. RESULTS: There were 491 women included in the trial and the overall rate of chorioamnionitis was 12.4%. We evaluated differences in the risk of chorioamnionitis based on maternal demographics and induction/ labor characteristics (Table 1). After controlling for confounders including maternal age, nulliparity, pre-gestational diabetes, gestational age ≤40 weeks, and intra-uterine pressure catheter (IUPC) use, risk factors that were independently associated with the diagnosis of chorioamnionitis included: nulliparity (aOR 3.14 [1.51-6.55]), pre-gestational diabetes (aOR 4.81 [1.08-21.36]), and use of an IUPC (aOR 1.92 [1.07-3.45]). CONCLUSION: We identified independent risk factors for chorioamnionitis. This study is unique in that it is a prospective analysis with highly standardized labor management, which reduces the likelihood that our findings can be attributed to differences in labor management. (Table Presented).
