N2  - Introduction Persistent left superior vena cava (LSVC) is a rare occurrence in the general population and seldom causes symptoms. When identified in a child, LSVC is usually associated with complex congenital heart disease. We present a case of a child with apparently normal cardiac anatomy who had recurrent bronchiolitis, wheezing, and mild persistent hypoxemia. LSVC subsequently identified by chest CT. Case Report A 12 month-old boy presented to MSU Pediatric Pulmonology for evaluation of recurrent bronchiolitis and hypoxemia. He was born at term by spontaneous vaginal delivery to a 32 year-old G1P1001 mother after infertility management and a pregnancy complicated by maternal gestational diabetes. Birth weight was 2.211 kg. At age 2 months, a heart murmur was noted, and an echocardiogram demonstrated mild right peripheral pulmonary artery stenosis. At age 6 months, this child was hospitalized with cough, wheezing, without fever, and oxygen saturations of 89 - 92%. Rapid RSV antibody test was positive. At age 9 months he was admitted again to the hospital with fever and retractions. Oxygen saturation was 84% and respiratory rate was in the low 60's. With 10 liters of high flow nasal cannula 100% oxygen, saturations improved to 98%. Echocardiogram later that month showed resolution of the PAS without other abnormality. At age 11 months, he was hospitalized with hypoxemia and tachypnea without clear infectious source. Oxygen saturation was 82%. Pulmonary parenchyma was normal; LSVC was evident on chest CT. At cardiac catheterization, the LSVC directly drained to the left atrium. Catheter embolization of the LSVC was successful; pre and post procedure saturations were 89% and 98%. Discussion The incidence of a persistent LSVC in the general population is about 0.3%. In patients with congenital heart disease, the incidence is 3% to 5%. When LSVC is present, it drains to the right atrium via the coronary sinus in 92% of instances. It is rare for the LSVC to drain into the left atrium through an unroofed coronary sinus. In our patient, the LSVC directly entered the left atrium superiorly with resultant mixing of pulmonary venous blood with blood from the LSVC causing mild systemic arterial desaturation. The diagnosis of this most rare congenital defect derived from a roundabout pulmonary evaluation of hypoxemia. Since the connection of the LSVC to the left atrium was direct and the anatomy of the abnormality was posterior, transthoracic echocardiographic findings of this LSVC would be subtle, unusual, and unexpected.
