N2  - Background: Gestational diabetes mellitus is associated with increased risk of fetal and neonatal morbidity and mortality due to structural or functional heart abnormalities. Aim. To compare cardiac structure and function in 30 fetuses of gestational diabetic mothers between 22 and 34 weeks of gestation (Group 1) vs. 30 fetuses of nondiabetic mothers with the same gestational age (Group 2). Methods. Cardiac wall thickness were evaluated through fetal echocardiography (M-mode) on a mid-ventricular short-axis plane at end-diastole (inter-ventricular septum [IVS], left ventricular posterior wall [LVPW] and right ventricular anterior wall [RVAW]). Global systolic function was expressed as fractional shortening (FS) and ejection fraction (EF); systolic longitudinal function was calculated by TDI and expressed as S' wave velocity of medial and lateral mitral annulus (IVSs' and LVs') and lateral tricuspid annulus (RVs'). Diastolic function was assessed by PW Doppler derived E/A ratios of both atrioventricular valves, and also using TDI-derived e'/a' ratio and E/e' ratio of left and right heart. Global myocardial performance index (MPI of right and left heart) was calculated with both PW Doppler (rMPI and lMPI) and with TDI (rMPI' and lMPI'). RESULTS: There were no significant differences between groups in terms of systolic function (FS, FE, RVs', LVs', IVSs'); the significant results are shown in the table. CONCLUSIONS: Gestational Diabetes mellitus is likely to affect cardiac wall thickness and diastolic function even in the early phase of pregnancy. Some echocardiographic functional parameters, particularly left and right E/A ratio and MPI could be useful to improve fetal surveillance in diabetic pregnancy (Table presented).
