N2  - Introduction: Adverse pregnancy outcomes (APOs) are associated with increased risk for future adverse cardiovascular events. Abnormalities in vascular function may play a role. Whether a history of APOs and coronary microvascular dysfunction (CMD) are associated is unknown. We assessed history of APOs and CMD in the Women's Ischemia Syndrome Evaluation - Coronary Vascular Dysfunction (WISE-CVD) cohort of women with signs and symptoms of ischemia without obstructive coronary artery disease (CAD). We hypothesized that women with a history of APOs would later develop signs and symptoms of ischemia without obstructive CAD associated with CMD by either invasive or non-invasive testing. Methods: WISE-CVD subjects who underwent invasive coronary flow reserve (CFR) testing and/or cardiac magnetic resonance imaging with assessment of myocardial perfusion reserve index were evaluated (n=324). Relationships between history of any APO and CMD by invasive or non-invasive testing were assessed by multivariable logistic regression analyses. APOs included any of the following: Hypertension during pregnancy, pre-eclampsia, eclampsia, gestational diabetes, or intra-uterine growth restriction (low-birth-weight child). Results: Overall, 79/324 (24%) women reported at least one APO. Women with APOs were younger (52 years vs 56 years, p = 0.001) and more likely obese (BMI 32 kg/m vs 29 kg/m , p = 0.004) but otherwise had no additional differences in cardiac risk variables vs women without APOs. Among the subgroup who underwent invasive CFR testing (n=185), those with an APO had lower CFR (2.53 vs 2.76, p = 0.016) vs those without APO. There were no differences in myocardial perfusion findings between APO and non-APO groups. Conclusion: History of APOs in women with signs and symptoms of ischemia without obstructive CAD is associated with lower CFR indicative of CMD. These findings suggest that APO and CMD may be linked by a common pathway and support the need for additional data to address this knowledge gap.
