N2  - Background: Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is associated with short-term and long term complications for both mothers and their children. Normalizing blood sugar prevents some but not all complications of GDM. New therapeutic approaches are needed, but face difficulties in translating research concepts into clinically useful therapies while also ensuring that new medications are safe in pregnancy. We sought to use electronic health record (EHR)-based genetic associations to investigate whether drugs currently considered safe in pregnancy might be potential candidates to treat, or prevent complications of, GDM. Methods: We compiled a list of 139 systematically active drugs considered safe in pregnancy targeting the products of 288 genes. Using a University's BioVU DNA biobank associated with EHRs, we identified SNPs in the 288 genes associated with GDM and type 2 diabetes (DM2). Findings were correlated with gene expression in the pancreatic beta cells and the placenta in health and diabetes. Using data from the EHR, we examined the association between specific medications and markers of glycemic control including the 50 gram glucose tolerance test (GTT) and glycated hemoglobin (A1C). Multivariate analyses controlled for age, BMI, gender, race, and ethnicity. Results: We identified SNPs in 20 genes associated with GDM and 44 associated with DM2. Among this group were multiple G-protein coupled receptors and phosphodiesterases suggesting roles in insulin secretion. Variants in the MTNR1B were associated with GDM and DM2 and use of the MTNR1B agonist agomelatine has been shown to be associated with reduced A1C. Variants were identified in 5HTR3D and the HTR3-antagonist anti-nausea medications (e.g. ondansetron), were associated with a 3.7 mg/dL increase in GTT. Conversely, hyperemesis gravidarum was associated with a 7.2 mg/dL decrease in GTT. Multiple variants were identified in voltage dependent calcium channel subunits, targeted by nifedipine and other calcium channel blockers. Use of these drugs was associated with a 3.1 mg/dL decrease in GTT. Variants in muscarinic and nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, targeted by the Alzheimer medication galantamine were associated with GDM and DM2. Among Alzheimer disease patients, Galantamine was associated with a 1.1% increase in A1C. Conclusion: This innovative approach identified novel potential therapies aimed at GDM that are known to be safe in pregnancy. Future directions include study of candidate therapies in experimental models of glucose control and association with the negative complications of GDM in mother and fetus.
