N2  - Introduction: Foetal growth restriction subjects the newborn to the risk of hypoglycaemia as well as longer-term sequelae including, poor neurological outcomes, obesity and cardiovascular disease. There is evidence that measuring body fat percentage (BF%) at birth allows for early detection of the high risk neonate and as such is now considered the 'gold standard'. Leptin is a hormone produced by adipocytes, as well as foetal and placental tissues. Leptin is associated with energy homoeostasis. Measurement of cord leptin may also provide better evidence of poor neonatal nutrition than the current standard of assessment of birthweight. Aim: To assess the correlation between cord blood leptin levels and body fat percentage in 1000 consecutive deliveries at a single tertiary centre. Methods: All women giving birth at the tertiary centre were eligible for inclusion during the study period. This is a retrospective analysis of a prospectively collected cohort of all births between 4 July and 29 October 2016. Women were approached in the antenatal period, if possible, or in the postnatal period if they presented in established labour. PeaPod data was obtained within 48 h of birth, except on occasion of neonatal admission to the NICU when a time frame of 72 h after birth was accepted. Neonatal BF% was measured using air displacement plethysmography (PeaPod). Results: Data was obtained from 1002 deliveries. The cohort had a mean maternal age of 32.4 years, mean Body Mass Index (BMI) of 23.8, mean gestation at delivery 39.4 weeks and mean birthweight of 3370 g. There was a significant linear relationship between leptin and neonatal BF% p < 0.001, gestation p < 0.001, birthweight p < 0.001 and BMI p < 0.001. However, regression analysis showed no correlation between leptin and parity, maternal age, gestational diabetes or hypertensive disease. Discussion: It is suitable to use leptin as a surrogate for the PeaPod BF% measurement in circumstances where it is not appropriate to place the infant in the PeaPod or to give a more complete clinical picture. Measurement of cord leptin may also provide better evidence of poor neonatal nutrition than the current standard of assessment of birthweight.
