N2  - Description The following case documents a 27-year-old Caucasian primigravida who developed postpartum pulmonary oedema leading to a new diagnosis of mitral valve dysfunction secondary to undiagnosed rheumatic heart disease. She presented at 39 + 4 weeks with spontaneous rupture of membranes and a background of gestational diabetes but no other reported medical or travel history. Induction of labour was commenced and an epidural sited for labour analgesia. Her induction was unsuccessful and she underwent an uneventful category 2 caesarean section performed under epidural top up. In the postpartum period she became increasingly tachycardic and hypoxic leading to a working diagnosis of pulmonary embolism. While awaiting CT pulmonary angiography she became oliguric and further fluid was administered. She deteriorated significantly exhibiting signs of respiratory distress and clinically in fulminant pulmonary oedema, for which she was treated and transferred urgently to coronary care. Transthoracic echocardiogram revealed moderate mitral stenosis and moderate to severe mitral regurgitation with rheumatic appearances. She was discharged 5 days later following medical therapy and has remained clinically well although symptomatically diuretic dependent. On the advice of the cardiologists she is being worked up for a tissue prosthesis valve replacement as a risk vs. benefit to facilitate her having more children. On review of her medical notes she had suffered with episodes of fever and painful swollen knees as a child where rheumatic fever was considered as a differential but not diagnosed. Discussion Valvular pathology is rare and often the symptoms of normal pregnancy are similar to those of cardiac decompensation [1]. The normal haemodynamic changes during pregnancy, labour and delivery increase cardiac work and can lead to pulmonary oedema and cardiac failure in patients with mitral valve disease [1]. Pulmonary embolism was a leading cause of maternal death in the UK [2]. Heart disease and valvular pathology complicates < 1% of pregnancies, with rheumatic heart disease being the most common acquired cause in developing countries [3]. This atypical presentation in a predominantly asymptomatic pregnancy with no overt risk factors for rheumatic heart disease ultimately led to initial misdiagnosis and mismanagement. Both mother and baby had a good outcome despite there being an increase in morbidity and mortality in this cohort [1].
