N2  - Questions: The development of the fetal autonomous nervous system (ANS) can be investigated by heart rate variability (HRV) measured by biomagnetic sensors. We recently showed that fetal HRV is altered after a glucose load in mothers with gestational diabetes. However the effect of maternal metabolism and weight development of the mother on fetal HRV is largely unknown. Methods: A total of 184 fetal magnetoencephalographic datasets from healthy pregnant women (gestational age ranging from 27-38 weeks) were included. Fetal heart signals were extracted by standard procedure and HRV was calculated from beat-to-beat intervals. The effects of insulin sensitivity, fasting insulin level, maternal weight gain and BMI before pregnancy fetal HRV were tested through partial correlations, adjusting for gestational age, gender and parity. Findings: Maternal insulin sensitivity was negatively correlated to the normalized high frequency components (HFn) and was positively associated to the ratio of low frequency to high frequency components (LFn/HFn). Maternal fasting insulin showed a positive association with HFn and a negative association with normalized low frequency components (LFn). Maternal weight gain was negatively associated with fetal heart rate, whereas BMI before pregnancy was positively correlated to the HFn and was inversely correlated with the LFn/HFn. Conclusions: The observed changes in low and high frequency components, as well as heart rate in relation to glucose metabolism, maternal weight and pregnancy weight gain could reflect the changes in ANS modulation. These findings highlight the important role of maternal metabolism on the development of ANS that may contribute to fetal metabolic programming.
