N2  - Objective: The study provides a profile of various fetal central nervous system (CNS) malformations presenting at a tertiary care Fetal Center over a period of ten years. Background: Congenital malformations of the CNS are increasingly being recognized prenatally due to advances in fetal neuroimaging. Prenatal counseling aims at critical prognostication for the future neurodevelopmental outcomes. Design/Methods: The study reports CNS abnormalities detected in the prenatal period from 2006 through 2016. Prenatal neurologic consultations were reviewed for studying various CNS malformations, their association with perinatal risk factors, and the neonatal and neurodevelopmental outcomes. Results: Ventriculomegaly (27%) and cortical or complex brain malformations (26%) were the largest category comprising over half of the patient population. Other diagnoses included posterior fossa malformations (19%), complex/multiple CNS malformations (13%), neural tube defects (6%), corpus callosum agenesis (5%) and hemorrhage/prenatal stroke (5%). Results showed maternal age range of 15-30 years and paternal age 15-50 years, history of spontaneous abortion in 27% and gestational diabetes in 13%. Prenatal consultations included Neurosurgery (17%) and Genetics (50%), and genetic diagnosis was positive in 5%. Follow-up of the infants aged 0-11 years showed: epilepsy in 26%, developmental delay in 38-45%, therapy requirement in 54%, vision issues in 25 % and hearing impairment in 14% of the study population. The range varied based on type and severity of the CNS malformation. Fetal MRI was highly sensitive and in only 8% of the infants postnatal neuroimaging added new information. Conclusions: Neurodevelopmental outcomes ranged over a wide spectrum. Prenatal counseling includes cautious consideration of parental expectations, available long-term data on neurodevelopment and functional outcomes, limitations of neuroimaging and genetic testing, and critical decision making from both clinicians and families. This study aims to develop a standardized and comprehensive approach for fetal neurologic prognostication for neurodevelopmental outcomes, and for timely decision making in the prenatal and postnatal period.
