N2  - Introduction The St Vincent Declaration (1989) aim to improve the pregnancy outcome of pre-gestational diabetes to the general population within 5 years. In the UK about 5% of pregnant women are affected by diabetes. (NICE Guidelines 2015). Method This retrospective study reviewed maternal deaths due to diabetes reported to all the Confidential Enquiry into Maternal Deaths (CEMACH, MBRRACE) over past 25 years. Results There were 22 maternal deaths. Lessons Learnt - Top ten changes in management of diabetic women from the 1990's showed improvements in: (1) pre-conception counselling; (2) multidisciplinary obstetric/endocrinology antenatal clinics with specialist midwives and improvement in primary and secondary care communication; (3) fetal echocardiogram and third trimester serial growth scans; (4) first trimester folic acid 5 mg and aspirin 75 mg; (5) third trimester fetal surveillance; (6) patient-centred care (information leaflets); (7) patient empowerment (early booking and self-referral); (8) mother-baby bonding, discontinue routine SCBU admission for BM monitoring; (9) audits and (10) clinical governance, serious incidence reporting, RCA and external reviews (maternal and fetal deaths). The CEMACH National Diabetes Survey (2002) of 3808 diabetics showed they had poorer maternal and fetal pregnancy outcomes compared to the general population, with preterm labour (36% versus 7%), caesarean section (67% versus 22%), macrosomia (21% versus 11%) and shoulder dystocia (8% versus 3%); and increased stillbirth (fivefold), perinatal mortality (three-fold), and congenital abnormalities (two-fold). Conclusion The adverse fetal and maternal pregnancy outcomes of pre-gestational diabetes in the UK remains high. The National Diabetes in Pregnancy Audit results remains outstanding.
