N2  - Introduction One in five women that present at antenatal booking in the UK are obese. High BMI (≥35) in pregnancy is associated with complications such as pre-eclampsia, gestational diabetes mellitus and venous thromboembolism, resulting in postnatal cardiovascular risk. Pulse wave velocity (PWV) as a measure of arterial stiffness, is an independent predictor of cardiovascular morbidity and mortality in the non pregnant population. The main aim of this study was to investigate PWV in obese pregnant women. Methods This was a prospective case-control study. Tensiomed Arteriograph™ was used for record PWV among pregnant women (n = 27) with high BMI attending specialised clinic at a gestational age ≥28 weeks of gestation. The controls were women with normal BMI (18-25 kg/m2) who had uncomplicated pregnancies (n = 26). Results Women with BMI ≥35 were noted to have a significantly elevated mean PWV (8.963 m/s + 2.369) compared with healthy matched controls (7.738 m/s + 1.769; P = 0.0384). Central systolic blood pressure (CsBP) was different between the two groups, the mean being 116.0 mmHg (95% CI 110.862 to 121.175) in the High BMI group in comparison to 98.8 mmHg (95% CI 96.035 to 101.635) in the Normal BMI group (P < 0.0001). Mean age and parity didn't differ between the two groups. Conclusion Pregnant women with a high BMI tend to have significantly higher PWV reflecting a greater level of arterial stiffness than those with a Normal BMI, arguably resulting from increased CsBP. Further work is required to examine the relationship between PWV and pregnancy complications in obese women.
