N2  - INTRODUCTION: The management of term pre labor rupture of membranes (PROM) in women with a prior cesarean section (CS) is under debate. The infectious morbidity associated with prolonged interval from admission to delivery, is countered by the risk for CS scar rupture during active management. Therefore, in these women, we aimed to study: 1) maternal risk factors for prolonged interval; and 2) the effect of prolonged interval on infectious and other obstetric complications. METHODS: This population-based retrospective cohort study, included all women (n=819) who delivered in our medical center (1988-2013), with a prior CS and term PROM in the index pregnancy. Study population was divided into two groups; 1) those who delivered ≤24h from PROM (n=514) and 2) those who delivered >24h (n=305) from PROM. A negative binomial model was used for the identification of independent risk factors for prolonged interval. RESULTS: The rate of smokers (p=0.03) and chronic hypertension (HTN) was higher among women with a prolonged interval (p=0.009). The indications for the prior CS varied between the study groups. Cord prolapse, non-reassuring fetal heart rate and placental abruption were more prevalent in the short interval group, while the rate of first stage arrest was higher in the prolonged interval group. The rate of chorioamnionitis and first and second stage arrest, blood transfusion and wound infection were all higher in the prolonged interval group. Gestational diabetes (GDM) and chronic HTN were both independent risk factor for prolonged interval. Additionally, prolonged interval and first stage arrest were both independent risk factors for infectious maternal morbidity. CONCLUSIONS: Among women with a prior CS: 1) GDM and smoking increase the risk of prolonged interval from PROM to delivery; 2) previous CS due to dystocia is associated with prolonged interval and its related morbidities; 3) these findings warrant reconsideration of trial of labor in women with term PROM and a prior CS due to labor dystocia.
