N2  - Background Though the intrapartum and postpartum periods remain the highest risk for intracranial haemorrhages (ICH), the incidence is very low. A study conducted by Kittner et al., showed that the postpartum period carries the highest risk for stroke. Another study done in USA showed that incidence of ICH was 6.1 per 100 000 deliveries in 2007. It was found that although this phenomenon is rare, it is even more devastating when it occurs in a young peripartum woman. Case During 2016 Logan Hospital, Queensland encountered two cases of ICH postpartum. Both women were healthy and had unremarkable antenatal and intrapartum periods. Case A demonstrates a 32-year-old female who had a subarachnoid haemorrhage within a few hours of an uneventful vaginal birth. During pregnancy she developed a mild gestational thrombocytopenia there was no other significant medical history. Postpartum she complained of chest pain and headache associated with hypertension. She had a normal Glasgow Coma Scale and no neurological signs. Investigations revealed that she had developed pre-eclampsia and imaging identified an ICH. She recovered well with no long-term neurological sequelae. Case B is a 31-year-old lady of African ethnicity who developed an intracranial haemorrhage with subsequent ongoing neurological deficits. Her pregnancy was complicated by gestational diabetes requiring metformin. She had an uneventful vaginal delivery but developed significant headache, altered level of consciousness and raised blood pressure within hours of delivery. She was admitted to the intensive care unit and eventually transferred to a tertiary hospital for treatment and rehabilitation. No underlying pathology was detected in her case, making it an idiopathic ICH. Conclusion Based on these two cases it is understood that ICH can have different outcomes irrespective of their antepartum risk factors. Though these two patients have recovered reasonably well, ICH accounts for a substantial proportion of pregnancy-related mortality. Haemorrhagic stroke symptoms often develop suddenly, without warning. For these reasons, identifying these lifethreatening cases is absolutely crucial in our practice. Headache after a delivery might not be just a simple headache, but could represent a much more sinister pathology. There is a general tendency for delayed diagnosis of uncommon but serious conditions during pregnancy because of a reluctance to perform imaging studies and to dismiss common symptoms. Strict monitoring and observations during and post delivery should be undertaken so that these diagnoses can be picked up early and treated immediately to reduce disability and death.
