N2  - OBJECTIVE: Maternal morbidity and mortality is increasing across the Unites States and the obesity epidemic is believed to be a major contributor. Our objective was to determine whether maternal comorbidities and adverse maternal outcomes are increasing in concert with the obesity burden. STUDY DESIGN: A retrospective cohort study of pregnant women delivered at our institution between 1997 and 2014 was performed. Obesity was defined as a pre-pregnancy or first trimester body mass index (BMI) ≥ 30 kg/m2 and the BMI was further categorized as: 30.0-34.9, 35.0-39.9, 40.0-49.9, and ≥ 50.0. Specific pre-pregnancy chronic co-morbidities and pregnancy-related outcomes were compared between women with BMI < 30.0, those with BMI ≥ 30, and in each obesity category between 1997-2005 (T1) and 2006-2014 (T2). Chi-square test, Student t-test, and Mann-Whitney U test were used for the analysis. Level of significance: P<.05. RESULTS: Of 34,862 deliveries attended between 1997 and 2014, 25,637 (73.5%) had a BMI < 30.0 and 9,225 (26.5%) had a BMI ≥ 30. Maternal obesity increased 64.8% and deliveries with BMI ≥50 increased almost three fold between T1 and T2. Type II and gestational diabetes, chronic hypertension, venous thromboembolism (VTE), and primary cesarean all increased significantly in women with BMI ≥ 30 and almost in all obesity categories between T1 and T2 (Figures 1 and 2). Among obese women, those with BMI ≥ 50 had the highest increased rate of chronic hypertension. Preeclampsia rate increased in almost all obesity categories, but did not differ by time period study. Maternal mortality rate was not significantly different between T1 and T2 or related to the degree of obesity. CONCLUSION: Significant maternal pre-pregnancy co-morbidities and pregnancy complications were diagnosed more frequently during contemporaneous time periods in relation to obesity severity and of those, chronic hypertension had the most dramatic increase. Preventive measures prior to conception are urgently needed to ameliorate this major public health problem. (Figure Presented).
