N2  - OBJECTIVE: To determine the role of mild gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) treatment on fetal programming using the children's proteome profile. STUDY DESIGN: Secondary analysis of a follow-up study of children (ages 5-10) of women enrolled in a multicenter trial of treatment versus no treatment of mild GDM. Plasma samples from male and female offspring from untreated GDM (n=12 and n=17, respectively) and treated GDM (n=18 and n=12, respectively) were analyzed by LC/MS/MS. The raw data files were processed using Thermo Scientific™Proteome Discoverer™ software against the IPI human 3.87 database (Mascot search engine). Peptides identified in at least 75% of samples in each group were expressed as spectra intensity ratio in treated group over untreated group. The ratio was converted to fold increase/decrease by Ingenuity Pathways Analysis (IPA) and analyzed for associations with biological functions against the database. Right-tailed Fisher's exact test was used to calculate a Pvalue (P<.05). RESULTS: Total of 164 peptides in males and 166 in females were included in IPA analysis. Numbers of differentially regulated proteins are shown in the Table. In treated males diseases/disorders/molecular and functions analyses revealed the most significant association of downregulated proteins with a cluster of diseases related to lipid, cholesterol, glucose metabolism, while in treated females the developmental disorders were predominant. The highest number of downregulated proteins (20) in treated males was grouped within the network which included molecules specific for cardiovascular disease, lipid metabolism and molecular transport whereas in treated females 31 downregulated molecules belonged to hematological system development and function, organismal injury and abnormalities, and organismal functions network. Among upregulated proteins the top category in treated males was developmental disorders (15) and in treated females the infectious diseases (2). The top downregulated protein in treated males and females -transthyretin - belonged to the HDL group. CONCLUSION: Treatment of mild GDM was associated with changes in children's proteome profile. The observed gender differences could possibly guide future therapeutic interventions and managements to prevent altered developmental programming resulting from GDM.
