N2  - Aim: This article aims to review the maternal and fetal outcomes of all lupus pregnancies seen in University of Santo Tomas (UST) Hospital. The patients' general profile, co-morbidities and pregnancy course, as well as the clinical profile of the babies and their co-morbidities were also reviewed. Methods: Data were retrospectively reviewed from the SLE database of the UST Hospital Section of Rheumatology, Clinical Immunology and Osteoporosis. The medical records of these patients were used as references. Results: There were 97 patients with 175 pregnancies occurring during the course of the disease. The mean age in years at SLE diagnosis was 26.0 (+/- 5.19) while the mean age in years at pregnancy was 28.0 (+/- 5.19). Majority delivered via normal spontaneous delivery (34.86%) with 107 (88.43%) infants having normal birth weights. Pre-pregnancy co-morbidities included chronic hypertension (N = 22, 12.57%), lupus nephritis (N = 19, 10.86%), tuberculosis (N = 12, 6.86%), cerebrovascular accident (N = 2, 1.14%) and myoma uteri (N = 1, 0.57%). During pregnancy, maternal complications were pre-eclampsia (N = 13, 7.43%), infections (N = 11, 6.28%), gestational diabetes (N = 6, 3.43%), hyperthyroidism (N = 3, 1.71%) and one occurrence each of pericarditis, dilated cardiomyopathy, pulmonary embolism and endometriosis. Postpregnancy complications included one occurrence each of pericardial effusion, retinal rupture, dilated cardiomyopathy, postpartum depression, hypertensive retinopathy, Hashimoto thyroiditis and atrial septal defect. Among the pregnancy outcomes, term deliveries were recorded in 96 (54.86%) patients while 25 (14.28%) had pre-term deliveries. There were 54 (30.85%) nonviable pregnancies including intrauterine fetal demise (N = 5, 2.86%), miscarriages (N = 43, 24.57%) and blighted ovum (N = 6, 3.43%). Congenital diseases recorded after delivery included congenital heart block (N = 2), and one occurrence each of meningocoele, thyroid abnormality, G6PD, neonatal lupus, autism, physiologic jaundice and neonatal sepsis. Conclusion: Although successful outcomes occur in most SLE pregnancies, miscarriages, preterm deliveries, blighted ovum and IUFD remain a concern, requiring close monitoring and effective multi-specialty team approach.
