N2  - INTRODUCTION: The incidence of GDM is rapidly increasing worldwide, and GDM is known to result in both early perinatal complications and long-term effects such as cardiovascular disease in offspring. AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) is cardioprotective, especially in the treatment of ischemia/reperfusion (I/R). Therefore, we hypothesized that AMPK is involved in the cardiac disease of offspring exposed to in utero hyperglycemia, in particular, the response to I/R of male offspring. METHODS: 8 weeks-old female Sprague-Dawley rats were mated with age matched male. GDM animal model was established by administering streptozotocin (35 mg/kg) on E8.5 via i.p. injection. The blood glucose level, GTT, ITT of the male offspring were measured at 22-24 weeks of age, and then the offsprings were subjected to 20 min of global ischemia followed by 30 min of reperfusion by the use of Langendorff system. Cell signaling was determined by western blot. RESULTS: The average glucose level of diabetic animals during pregnancy was >16.7mmol/L (vs. control, N=4, P<0.05). The body weight of the GDM male offspring were significantly higher than control male offspring from the age of 8 weeks (N=16, P<0.05); the heart weights and heart weight/body weight ratio was also higher in the GDM group vs. control group (1.26±0.03g vs.1.48±0.06g, N=8, P<0.01 and 0.26±0.003 vs. 0.28±0.006% N=8, P<0.05, respectively). The phosphorylation of Akt, Erk(1/2), AMPK and (ACC) was markedly reduced in GDM male offspring rat hearts without I/R treatment vs. control group (36.8%, 39.1%, 53.7% and 31.6% respectively,N≥3, P<0.05). The post-I/R cardiac contractile function of GDM male offspring rats was significantly compromised vs. control group (63% vs. 94%, N=4, P<0.05). The phosphorylation of AMPK and ACC were elevated by I/R in both groups, but significantly lower in the GDM group compare to the control group (N=3, P<0.05). CONCLUSIONS: GDM male offspring rats have high risks of obesity and cardiac disease. GDM also has a transgenerational impact on cardiac I/R intolerance in male offspring, potentially due to alterations in the offspring cardiac AMPK pathway.
