N2  - Purpose: Postpartum women whose pregnancy conditions indicate elevated risk for cardiovascular disease and diabetes can decrease risk via postpartum weight loss, yet traditional face-toface interventions are often unsuccessful. Our objective was to develop and iteratively test a theory-based mobile health (mHealth) program designed to help overweight/obese postpartum women reduce risk for cardiometabolic disease. Methods: The mHealth Fit After Baby program delivers daily evidence-based content, facilitates tracking of weight, diet, and steps, and provides weekly coaching. We initially conducted one round of alpha-testing among women with young children, focused primarily on generating reaction to various features in the app. We then conducted two rounds of beta-testing in postpartum women with the target pregnancy conditions, focused on usability, navigability, and acceptability. We analyzed use and feedback to make iterative changes before conducting subsequent rounds of testing. Results: Five women completed alpha testing, and changes made in response to feedback included improving the process of getting started, improving navigation, and adding daily reminders to use the app. Four women completed round one and 13 women completed round two of beta-testing, with mean age 33 (SD 4.5) years; BMI 34 (SD 6.4) kg/m2, 94% White and 6% Asian. Participants had a recent pregnancy complicated by preeclampsia (41%), pre-term delivery (35%), gestational hypertension (29%), gestational diabetes (29%), and/or SGA delivery (12%). We demonstrated an increase in participant satisfaction and in the number of coaching interactions from round 1 to round 2. The primary themes from feedback were to: 1) improve navigation through a different home screen; 2) add structure to coaching interactions; and 3) increase content focused on challenges faced by new mothers and on diet (including recipes) and exercise (including workouts). Conclusion: Through a process of iterative development and testing we were able to improve the mHealth Fit After Baby program in preparation for a larger randomized trial.
