N2  - Offspring of diabetic pregnancies (ODP) are at risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD) at birth and throughout life, purportedly through fuel-mediated influences on the developing heart. Cellular bioenergetics play a key role in the pathogenesis of adult diabetic cardiomyopathy which is influenced by both hyperglycemia and hyperlipidemia. However, the long term effects of maternal diabetes and dyslipidemia on cardiac metabolism and mitochondrial function in the developing fetal heart are unknown. The objective of this study was to determine if a maternal high-fat diet during pregnancy exacerbates CVD disease in ODP through developmentally programmed alterations in cardiac metabolism and mitochondrial function. To do this, Sprague Dawley rats were fed a control or high-fat diet and administered placebo or streptozotocin to induce diabetes during pregnancy. Dams delivered offspring from four groups: controls, diabetes exposed, diet exposed and combination exposed. Litters were culled to equal size and pups were cross-fostered to normal dams. Cardiac function, cellular bioenergetics (mitochondrial stress test, glycolytic stress test and palmitate oxidation assay), lipid peroxidation, mitochondrial histology and copy number were determined at newborn, 3 weeks, 10 weeks and 6 months of age. Outcomes were interrogated for diet, diabetes and interaction effect using ANOVA with significance set at p≤0.05. At birth, diabetes exposed cardiomyocytes demonstrated impaired glycolytic and respiratory capacity. High-fat diet exposed cardiomyocytes had increased mitochondrial copy number and oxidative injury. Combination exposed offspring had diastolic/systolic dysfunction and mitochondrial injury that persisted into adulthood. This study demonstrates that a maternal high-fat diet contributes to developmentally programmed CVD in ODP through impaired cardiac metabolism and mitochondrial dysfunction.
