N2  - OBJECTIVE: Cardiovascular complications stemming from vascular dysfunction have been widely explored in the setting of gestational diabetes, hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, restricted fetal growth and preterm delivery. However, the impact of placental abruption, a strong indicator of microvascular disturbance, on the risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD) mortality and morbidity remains poorly characterized. STUDY DESIGN: We designed a population-based prospective cohort study of women that delivered singletons at ≥22 week's gestation in Denmark (1978-2010). Information on selected CVD events was ascertained by linking the National Patient Registry and the Registry of Causes of Death to the Danish Birth Registry. We estimated risks of CVD mortality and morbidity among women with a history of abruption from Cox proportional hazards regression following adjustments for putative confounders. RESULTS: Over the 33-year period (13,231,559 person-years of follow-up) 828,289 women had one or more deliveries, of which 15,336 (1.9%) were complicated by abruption. CVD mortality rates in women with and without abruption were 0.9 and 0.3, respectively (adjusted hazard ratio (HR) 2.50, 95% confidence interval (CI) 1.59, 3.92) (Table). The corresponding CVD complication rates were 16.5 and 10.0 per 10,000 person-years, respectively (HR 1.45, 95% CI 1.31, 1.60) for women with a history of abruption compared to women with no such history. In particular, the increased risks were evident for ischemic heart disease, acute myocardial infarction, and heart failure. These increased risks were substantially elevated when deliveries occurred at <34 weeks and 34-36 weeks. CONCLUSION: Findings from this prospective cohort study show increased hazards of premature cardiovascular morbidity and mortality in relation to abruption. These results underscore the importance of understanding the pathogenesis of distorted placental microvasculature that appears to be harbinger of other microvasculature disorders, including cardiovascular diseases later in life. (Table Presented).
