N2  - OBJECTIVE: There is a growing body of evidence that adverse pregnancy and perinatal outcomes are linked to future cardiovascular disease in the index child. We therefore sought to evaluate whether pregnancy associated hypertension (gestational hypertension or preeclampsia; PAH) and preterm birth (PTB) are associated with child cardiometabolic health at 5-10 years of age. STUDY DESIGN: Secondary analysis of index children born to women enrolled during pregnancy with either mild gestational diabetes mellitus or lesser degrees of glucose intolerance and followed up 5- 10 years after birth. Pregnancy outcomes were collected during the original study. At follow-up, children had anthropometric and blood pressure measurements and provided fasting blood for central laboratory measurements (triglycerides, HDL-cholesterol, glucose). Multivariable models were developed for the cohort overall and to test for interaction between adverse pregnancy outcome and child sex. RESULTS: This analysis included 980 children who were followed up at a mean age of 7±1 (SD) years. Overall, after adjusting for potentially confounding factors, systolic blood pressure and BMI zscore were significantly higher in the children who were born to mothers with PAH and were delivered at term, compared with those delivered at term to a normotensive mother (Table). These associations did not vary by child sex (p for interaction =.50 and .89, respectively). Furthermore, the association with systolic blood pressure was not explained by child BMI. No differences were observed for waist circumference, triglycerides, HDL-cholesterol, diastolic blood pressure or glucose. The cardiometabolic health of the children born preterm was similar to the children born at term to mothers without PAH. CONCLUSION: These data suggest that term PAH, which is considered to be a distinct clinical entity from preterm PAH, may be a risk factor for elevated systolic blood pressure and BMI in the index children. Preterm birth was not a risk factor for elevated blood pressure, high BMI or for any of the other measures of cardiometabolic heath in this population. (Table Presented).
