N2  - OBJECTIVE: Placental malperfusion lesions are associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes, but the maternal characteristics associated with these lesions have not been defined. Women with gestational diabetes (GDM) are at increased risk for pregnancy complications, so we characterized the prevalence and clinical correlates of malperfusion lesions in this population. STUDY DESIGN: This was a retrospective cohort study of 1186 women with GDM who had placental pathology available. Malperfusion lesions included infarcts, vasculopathy, syncytial knots, perivillous fibrin, and fibrin deposition. Baseline maternal characteristics and pregnancy outcomes were compared in women with and without malperfusion lesions, and multivariable logistic regression analysis was used to identify factors associated with malperfusion lesions. RESULTS: Malperfusion lesions were common in women with GDM (n=362, 30.5%), and they occurred more frequently in those women with GDM who also had chronic hypertension (CHTN) and excess weight gain. After adjusting for maternal characteristics including pre-pregnancy BMI and CHTN, excess weight gain was independently associated with an increased risk for malperfusion lesions (aOR 1.42, 95% CI 1.06-1.91, p=0.02). Stratified analysis demonstrated that malperfusion lesions were associated with greater weight gain in both overweight (33.3 ±16.0 vs 27.9 ±12.6 lbs, p=0.003) and obese (23.2 ±16.3 vs 20.1 ±16.4 lbs, p=0.04) women, and this finding persisted after women with hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (HDP) were excluded in sensitivity analysis. Placental malperfusion lesions were also associated with an increased risk of preterm birth (PTB) <34 weeks that was independent of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (see Table). CONCLUSION: Our results suggest that malperfusion lesions are common in women with GDM and this may be one pathway linking excess gestational weight gain to adverse pregnancy outcomes in women with GDM. Future studies are needed to identify metabolic factors that may be involved in this association. (Table Presented).
