N2  - Background: Maternal diabetes and obesity are both associated with increased circulation of metabolic fuels such as glucose and lipids that can cross the placenta. While it is known that children of diabetic mothers are more likely to suffer from newborn respiratory distress and that fatty acids regulate lung growth, little is understood about how glucose- and fatty acid-induced fetal programming of lung development occurs and if these events function in parallel or independent pathways. Objectives: Investigate the manner in which gestational diabetes and maternal high fat consumption adversely affect offspring pulmonary development. Design and Methods: A rat model was developed that simulates the human conditions by pharmacologically inducing gestational diabetes with streptozotocin to pregnant dams sustained on a high fat to study fetal lung development. Physiological (cardiopulmonary echocardiogram, respiratory mechanics), histopathological (epithelial and vascular cell markers, vessel quantification, mean chord length), and molecular (VEGF signaling, fatty acid status) outcomes were determined in offspring of diabetic mothers at birth and after 3 weeks of age. Results: Offspring of diabetic mothers on a high fatty acid diet had pulmonary hypoplasia and hypertension at birth followed by decreased pulmonary compliance and hysteresis with increased elastance and respiratory system resistance. Fewer pulmonary vessels (positive for von Willebrand factor) were enumerated in groups acclimated to the high fat diet regardless of diabetic status. Impaired vasculogenesis correlated with reduced levels of VEGF with possible compensatory phosphorylation of Akt. Glucose-dependent pulmonary expression of thioredoxin interacting protein (Txnip) increased in offspring of diabetic mothers yet was attenuated by a maternal high fat diet. Conclusions: Altered expression of angiogenic signaling pathways, including the Txnip-VEGF-Akt axis, may serve as primary molecular targets responsible for the pathologic associations linking maternal influences of diabetes and dietary fat composition with impaired pulmonary vasculogenesis and lung function.
