N2  - Introduction: Gestational diabetes (GD) is considered to be one of the major risk factors for shoulder dystocia and birth trauma. The aim of the present study was to evaluate the incidence of shoulder dystocia and traumatic birth injuries in gestational diabetic pregnancies and identify risk factors related to fetal injury in this population. Methods: We conducted a retrospective descriptive study over a 4-year period (2011-2014). We analysed 448 term singleton pregnancies complicated by GD, with fetuses in a cephalic presentation. 19 neonates who experienced birth trauma (study group) and 429 newborns without neonatal injuries (control group) were identified. We considered birth trauma the following diagnosis: fractures, neurologic injuries, subgaleal haemorrhage and intracranial haemorrhage. GD was diagnosed using the IADPSG criteria. Statistical analyses were performed using SPSS version 20.0 software. A p value <0.05 was considered significant. Results: Shoulder dystocia occurred in 3,1% of all term pregnancies complicated by gestational diabetes. Birth trauma occurred in 19 pregnancies, which corresponded to an incidence of 4,2%, 6,7% for vaginal deliveries, which is higher than the general population. The injuries identified were clavicular fractures (42,1%), brachial plexus injury (21,1%), subdural haemorrhage (5,3%), subarachnoid haemorrhage (5,3%) and subgaleal haemorrhage (5,3%). The two groups were similar with respect to mean maternal age (32,2 vs 32,9 years), pregestational body mass index (BMI) (28,6 vs 27,6 Kg/m2), weight gain during pregnancy, family history of gestational diabetes and prior GD. The group with birth trauma had more cases of macrosomia in previous pregnancy (15,8% vs 3,5%, p<0,05). There were not statistical differences between the two groups with respect to the time of diagnosis of GD, need of insulin therapy and gestational age of onset of insulin therapy. The mean gestational age was 39 weeks in both groups. There were more vacuum and forceps assisted delivery in the study group (50% vs 29%, P=0,06). Mean birth weights (3421 vs 3241 g) were higher in the birth trauma group. Conclusion: The incidence of shoulder dystocia and birth trauma was higher in gestational diabetic population. In our study, birth trauma was more frequent with assisted delivery, fetus with higher birth weight and history of macrossomia in previous pregnancy. However, the occurrence of shoulder dystocia and birth trauma cannot be accurately predicted by antenatal or intrapartum risk factors. Therefore all obstetric care providers should be able to promptly recognize shoulder dystocia and should be alert for birth trauma, especially in gestational diabetic population.
