N2  - The purpose of this is presentation is to discuss the established role of ultrasound in the management of pregnancy complicated by Diabetes Mellitus (DM), as well as new developments with regards to the use of ultrasound in this situation. We choose to explore the role of US in pregnancy complicated by DM in three areas: 1. Role of ultrasound in estimation of fetal weight. We will highlight the importance of using fractional limb volume in addition to standard biometry. I believe that such a technique can add accuracy to estimation of fetal weight and can be clinically useful. However, it goes without saying these technique should only be used by the experienced trained operator with the right machine. A presentation on this new technique may encourage more perinatologists to use it clinically 2. Role of ultrasound in diagnosis of congenital malformation. It is well established that the risk of fetal congenital malformation is much higher in gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) and pre-gestational diabetes mellitus (PGDM) compared to non-diabetic pregnant patients. In most publications, the incidence of fetal malformations in this condition can be as high as 10%. Fetal congenital malformation can affect virtually every single organ in the fetus in GDM and PGDM, however, this effect is more common in the central nervous system and cardiovascular system. Diagnosis of congenital heart defect is very difficult and requires great skill in scanning. There are many reasons for this poor detection rate, in view of this any new diagnostic modalities are highly welcomed to improve antenatal detection of congenital heart disease. In this presentation I will discuss the new 3D/4D based Spatio-Temporal Imaging Correlation (STIC). A presentation on this new technique may encourage more perinatologists to use it clinically which may improve the detection rate of congenital heart disease. 3. Role of ultrasound in monitoring diabetic pregnant patients. Doppler studies in fetal vessels such as Umbilical artery and middle cerebral artery which perform brilliantly in identifying placental insufficiency in non-diabetic pregnancy, are not and neither appropriate nor sufficient in diabetic pregnancy. This because of the added risk of acidaemia. The question remains which vessels to interrogate in diabetic pregnancy. Dusctus venosus and hepatic artery have a promising potential identifying adverse perinatal outcome in such circumstances. In this presentation we will explore the role of doppler in these two vessels to monitor diabetic pregnancy.
