N2  - 34-year primigravida was admitted to high risk pregnancy ward in 24th week with lower abdominal pain andpressure of baby pushing down. Pain was most intense under the right rib cage. Initial blood and urine tests werenormal. Analgetics did not help. She started complaining of skin pain on touch - dysaesthesia and migraineheadaches. Blood results showed haemoglobin drop, from 108 g/L to 87 g/L. No obstetrical or surgical cause wasfound. Neuropathic pain that was unresponsive to myriad of analgetics and unexplained haemoglobin drops led usto suspect acute intermittent porphyria (AIP). The diagnosis was confirmed in 27th week of gestation by testing 24hour urine sample for porphyrins. Porphyrias are rare inherited diseases caused by the deficiency of one of the enzymes in the haem synthesis. Thiscauses accumulation of neurotoxic precursors. Presentation of AIP varies. It reflects the impairment of theautonomic, somatic peripheral and central nervous system. Most common symptoms are abdominal pain, impairedconsciousness and neuropathy. Other symptoms are diarrhoea, nausea, vomiting, arterial hypertension, tachycardia,orthostatic hypotension and convulsions. Coexisting dehydration and hyponatraemia worsen the symptoms. Attacks of AIP are caused by several triggering factors, such as infections, stress, starvation, certain medicationsand drugs and female sex hormones. AIP is rare therefore we had no experience on how to treat our patient. Learning from the literature we institutedintravenous paracetamol, 20% glucose infusion and carbohydrate rich food. Maintaining the therapy waschallenged by gestational diabetes mellitus, infection at the site of central venous catheter and difficulty to keepcatheters patent. Despite the therapy successful pain control was difficult. Haemoglobin levels fluctuated between70 and 100 g/L. At 36 0/7 weeks caesarean section was performed for inability to control the pain.Surgery wasuneventful. Healthy girl was born, 2740 g, 48 cm, Apgar score 9/9. After the surgery patient was 5 days inintensive care unit, on carbohydrate rich diet and 20% glucose intravenous infusion. She was discharged on 10thpostoperative day. Four months after the delivery she has migraine headaches, but no abdominal pain. With this case we would like to raise awareness about AIP that can be first diagnosed in pregnancy. AIP cansignificantly affect pregnancy outcome and cause foetal and maternal death. For successful perinatal outcome earlydiagnosis is pivotal, followed by the therapy with carbohydrate rich food, 20% glucose intravenous infusion,avoidance of triggering factors and careful foetal and maternal surveillance.
