N2  - Introduction Gestational diabetes is a pathology we face very frequently, and it requires a close monitoring because of its impact on the fetus development. Children born to mothers with gestational diabetes have four times more risk of hospital admission than general population. It is also known its association with congenital malformations. Objetive To establish the disease frequency in infants born to mothers with insulin-dependent gestational diabetes in our environment. To study the use of glycated hemoglobin as a marker for population considered to be at risk. Method Retrospective, comparative, descriptive observational study based on the analysis of 198 infants born to mothers with insulin-dependent gestational and pregestational diabetes on a tertiary care hospital between 2009 and 2011. Analysis with SPSS. Results Gestational diabetes incidence: 1,46%. Pregestational diabetes 23 women (11,6%) and gestational 175 (88,4%). 36 infants were prematures (18,2%). Hospital admission in 27,3%. A 7,6% required intravenous treatment of hypoglycemia. 6,1% suffered intrauterine growth retardation and over 16,2% were macrosoma. The most common congenital malformation was cardiovascular (7,1% several cardiac malformation and 5,5% minor ones), above all septal hypertrophy in 7 children, IVC in 5 and 2 PDA hemodynamically significantly. Other congenital malformation: 4,5%. Congenital malformation are more frequent in infants who require intravenous treatment because of hypoglycemia (RR 11,413). No statistical relation was found betwen type of diabetes. The relationship is statistically significant between pathology echocardiography and prematurity (p=0'016), hospital admission (p=0.000), fetal well-being loss (p=0.025), acute respiratory distress (p=0.010) and hypoglycemia (p=0.000). Glycated hemoglobin is related to birth weight over p90 (p=0.000) and hospital admission for intravenous treatment of hypoglycemia (p=0,019). No correlation was found with congenital malformation. Findings Insulin-dependent gestational diabetes can result in severe manifestations. Diabetes type does not predetermine congenital malformations. Glycated hemoglobin can be used as a risk marker or metabolic disorder predictor in these infants. It is not related to the incidene of congenital abnormalities.
