N2  - Background: Malignant cardiac neoplasms are extremely rare in neonates. Prenatal diagnosis is often not available. Initial symptoms can mimic other cardiac and non cardiac diseases. Case report: We report a premature female newborn with birth-weight 2480g, 34 g.w, who underwent cardiac operation due to cardiac tumor. This was 3-rd pregnancy after two spontaneous abortions of mother with thrombophilia, gestational diabetes, hydramnion, retroplacental hematoma. The baby was admitted to NICU after birth with transitory respiratory failure, requiring brief O2- supplementation. Because of laboratory data for inborn infection: slightly elevated CRP, antibiotic therapy was started. In the first days the condition of the baby was stable, but CRP values rose dramatically (33.7 on day 5-th, 146mg/l on day 9-th) and remained high thereafter. From day 11-th progressive deterioration began: tachypnea, increased oxygen requirements, decreased reactivity. Serratia and Klebsiella (day 9-th), Enterococus spp. and Staphylococcus epidermidis (day 15-th) were isolated from peripheral specimens. Broad spectrum antibiotics were added according their sensitivity. Chest radiographs were unremarkable. On day 18-th life threatening deterioration with clinical symptoms of shock occurred, mechanical ventilation was started. Echocardiography showed a large tumor formation in the right atrium 2x2.3 cm, reduced blood flow in the right ventricle and pulmonary artery, small pericardial effusion. The baby was immediately transferred to the cardiology hospital. On day 19-th cardiac surgery was performed. A big tumor with myxomatous appearance, which occupied the entire cavity of the right atrium and infiltrated the ring of the tricuspid valve, was found intraoperatively. It was extirpated; the front wall of the right atrium was restored with patch. Hemodynamics was temporarily stabilized. On the next day a sudden deterioration with ventricular fibrillation and asystole occured, full cardio-circulatory resuscitation was performed but the outcome was lethal. The histological result confirmed congenital malignant tumor (myosarcoma) of the right atrium. Discussion and Conclusions: Inborn cardiac sarcomas are casuistic, the prognosis is extremely poor. Due to fast progression in third trimester they can be missed at earlier fetal echocardiography. The postnatal clinical course is aggressive, often not specific until invasive local infiltration and intracavity obstruction progress. In our case the sarcoma in the right atrium mimics an inborn infection, followed by nosocomial infection and septic shock. Despite low incidence inborn cardiac tumors should be kept in mind and echocardiography should be made if unclear symptoms with progressive deterioration in neonatal period occurred.
