N2  - Introduction: Gestational diabetes (GD) is the most common medical condition in pregnancy, affecting approximately 6-14% of the pregnant women. The causes are that placental hormones (progesterone, somatotropinecoriónic, GH variant and, especially, placental lactogen), together with the increase of cortisol, PRL and maternal sources of TSH and glucagon, produce an insulin resistance that results in an diabetogenic effect. This resistance usually begins between weeks 20-24 of pregnancy. According to the American Diabetes Association, one can speak of two types: • Pre-gestational diabetes (PGD): it requires preconception counseling and constitutes 10% of cases, including type 1 and type II diabetes. • Gestational diabetes (GD): constitutes about 90% of cases of diabetes in pregnancy. To know fetal complications in this pathology will allow us an early diagnosis and a proper perinatal monitoring to reduce them. Objectives: To address the recent evidence regarding: - The adverse outcomes for fetus in gestational diabetes. - The prenatal control to detect fetal complications Methodology: Reveaw in the databases Medline, Cochrane and UptoDate with the keywords diabetes gestacional and fetal development. Only reviews and clinical trials of the past 5 years were included. Results: In poorly controlled diabetes, the main fetal complications are: - Abortions and/or malformations in organgenesis period:specially hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Only in type 1 PGD, because birth defects occur before the 13th weeks gestation and insulin resistance in GD occurs between 20-24 weeks gestation. - Intrauterine Growth Restriction in maternal vascular disease. - Prematurity. - Macrosomia: fetus converts excess glucose into fat. This results in increased risk of dystocia and birth trauma. - Risk of loss of antepartum or intrapartum fetal wellbeing. - Fetal pulmonary immaturity: respiratory distress syndrome. - Metabolic disorders: hypoglycemia, hypocalcemia. - Blood disorders: polycythemia, hyperbilirubinemia. That's why a stricter prenatal control is needed. Besides the three basic ultrasound studies, they should also apply: - In GD: an ultrasonography between 28-32 weeks gestation, to detect the presence of macrosomia. - In PGD: echocardiography in 14-16 and in 28-32 weeks gestation, due to risk of cardiomyopathy. In addition, monthly ultrasonographies from the 28-30 weeks, Doppler if necessary. Conclusions: In type 1 PGD, the main fetal complications are abortions and defects in organgenesis period, specially cardiomyopathy. This requires echocardiography in 14-16 and in 28-32 weeks gestation and monthly ultrasonographies from the 28-30 weeks. On the other hand, the higher incidence the macrosomia in GD, besides other complicatons like intrauterine growth restriction or fetal pulmonary inmaturit, makes advisable an additional ultrasonography between 28-32 weeks gestation.
