N2  - Women with maternal diabetes, including type 1 (T1DM), type 2 (T2DM), and gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), have a greater prevalence of cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk factors than women with normoglycemic pregnancies. Additionally, a higher prevalence of placental vasculopathy has been reported among women with compared to women without diabetes in pregnancy. However, the relationship between placental vasculopathy and CVD risk factors in women with diabetes has not been elucidated. We retrospectively analyzed the CVD risk profiles of pregnant women with (T1DM [n= 25], T2DM [n= 34], and biochemically-confirmed GDM [n= 67]) and without placental vasculopathy (T1DM [n= 11], T2DM [n= 3], and GDM [n= 62]) with self-reported race/ethnicity and live, full-term singleton births. Placental vasculopathy included decidual and fetal thrombotic vasculopathy, chorangiosis, and placental infarct. Odds ratios [OR; 95% CI] measured the univariate associations between placental vasculopathy and CVD risk related factors, including age, body mass index, blood pressure, gestational weight gain, non-white race, smoking, and insulin use. Women with compared to those without placental vasculopathy had greater gestational weight gain (24.1 ± 12.9 vs. 18.9 ± 12.4 kg; p= 0.01) and insulin use (43.8 vs. 21.1%; p= 0.001). Gestational weight gain (1.03 [1.00, 1.05]; p= 0.03) and insulin use (OR [95% CI]: 3.37[1.81, 6.27]; p <0.001) predicted an increased risk of chorangiosis; non-white race (0.49 [0.28, 0.86]; p= 0.01) predicted a decreased risk of chorangiosis. Weight gain (1.04 [1.01, 1.06]; p= 0.01) and insulin use (2.92 [1.51, 5.64]; p= 0.001) also predicted an increased risk of any placental vasculopathy in women with diabetes. Maternal diabetes and placental vasculopathy are associated with CVD risk factors, specifically weight gain and insulin use. Further research on the implications of the intersection of maternal diabetes, placental vasculopathy, and CVD risk is warranted.
