N2  - Congenital heart defects represent the vast majority of structural birth defects. The prevalence of congenital cardiovascular anomalies varies between 4-50 per 1000 live birth according to different studies and depends on several factors. A Hungarian population-based study involving 2259 children showed that the birth prevalence of congenital heart diseases was 10,2 per 1000 live birth based on the pediatric cardiologic examination and/or the evaluation of autopsy report of each individual child. Congenital cardiovascular anomalies are responsible for 10% of infant mortality, but they are present in 25% of congenital anomalies with lethal outcome. Our aim was to study the different cardiovascular anomalies separately, according to an already accepted classification. For this purpose, we used the large dataset of the population-based Hungarian Case-Control Surveillance of Congenital Abnormalities. Here we analyzed the maternal age, birth/ pregnancy order, socio-economic status, drinking or smoking habit, maternal diseases and drug consumption at different gestational ages of the fetuses with ventricular septal defects. We further examined the birth outcomes of this anomaly and the possible preventive effect of multivitamin or folic acid intake during pregnancy. We showed that high-fever-related maternal diseases may have a role in the origin of ventricular septal defects, which is preventable with antifever drug therapy, and the high doses of folic acid in early pregnancy reduced the risk of the disease. Ventricular septal defect had mild female excess with a higher rate of preterm birth and mainly low birthweight indicating intrauterine growth restriction of affected fetuses. The mothers of cases had lower socioeconomic status and higher rate of smoking and particularly drinking habit. The evaluation of medically recorded pregnancy complications showed an association of gestational diabetes with a higher risk for the septal defect. In conclusion, the ventricular septal defect and obvious fetal growth restriction needs further explanation in these cases, while gestational diabetes, lower socioeconomic status and adverse lifestyle of pregnant women may have a role in the origin of this malformation. Our results may help to understand which risk factors could contribute to the pathophysiology of ventricular septal defect, which may help in their possible prevention.
