N2  - Gestational diabetes mellitus, gestational hypertension, and pre-eclampsia are important complications of pregnancy and are leading causes of both fetal and maternal morbidity and mortality as well as long-term health problems. Early prediction for these conditions remains difficult. Evaluation of arterial stiffness, shown to be an independent predictor of cardiovascular disease, represents a promising tool for the detection of vascular dysfunction underlying these obstetric conditions. The purpose of this study was to evaluate arterial stiffness as a predictive tool for the composite outcome of gestational diabetes, gestational hypertension, and pre-eclampsia. In this prospective longitudinal study of women with singleton highrisk pregnancies, arterial hemodynamics and stiffness were evaluated at each trimester, within 24 hours of delivery, and at 6 weeks postpartum using applanation tonometry (SphygmoCor, AtCor Medical Ltd, Australia). Of the 48 participants recruited (mean maternal age: 37.4±4.3, body mass index [BMI]: 26.0±6.9), 3 developed gestational diabetes, 3 developed gestational hypertension, and 3 developed pre-eclampsia. Between 10-14 weeks' gestation, women that went on to develop a composite outcome had significantly higher carotid-femoral pulse wave velocity (6.9 [95% CI: 6.7-7.0) vs. 6.4 [6.3-6.6], p<0.001) and lower carotid-radial pulse wave velocity (7.5 [7.5-7.6] vs. 8.2 [8.1-8.2], p<0.01). The augmentation index corrected for a heart rate of 75 beats per minute, a measure of pulse wave reflection, was also lower in women who developed a composite outcome (9.3 [8.5-10.0] vs. 14.1 [13.4-14.8], p<0.001). Subendocardial viability ratio, however, did not differ between the two groups in the first trimester (135.0 [132.9-137.2] vs. 136.5 [134.8-138.3]). Analyses were adjusted for maternal age and BMI. Within a high-risk population, in the first trimester, significant differences were observed in both central and peripheral arterial stiffness as well as wave reflection in women that went on to develop either gestational diabetes, gestational hypertension, or pre-eclampsia as compared to women who did not. Future work in this ongoing study will focus on continuing recruitment as well as compare the predictive value of arterial stiffness to that of uterine artery Doppler and angiogenic biomarkers.
