N2  - Objective: To compare three groups of women: (1) GDM diagnosed before 20 weeks (n=381), (2) GDM after 26 weeks (n=553) with normal oral glucose tolerance test before 20 weeks and (3) Type 2 diabetes (n=248). Method: Medical notes for women with diabetes in pregnancy from a multi-ethnic population in East London, delivered between August 2005 and July 2011, were reviewed as part of an audit by the Maternity Diabetes Service, Newham University Hospital. Median gestation at booking was similar in late (12 weeks) and early (11.29 weeks) GDM and Type 2 diabetes (12.43 weeks). Results: 32%-38% required English language support. Demographic parameters progressively increased in women with late GDM compared to early GDM, and when these were compared to Type 2 diabetes: median age (31 years, 33 years, 35 years), median body mass index (BMI) (28.8, 29.4, 30.8), mean parity (1.33, 1.63, 2.2). Other parameters also showed a similar trend: use of insulin/metformin (30%, 56%, 92%), pre-term delivery rate (14%, 21%, 35%), special care baby unit admission rate (7%, 13%, 22%) and emergency caesarean section rate (28%, 33%, 32%). The most striking findings of concern were the perinatal mortality rate (0.18%, 2.1%, 0.4%), fetal cardiac anomaly (0%, 1.8%, 3.42%) and post-delivery glucose intolerance rate (14%, 42%, NA). Conclusion: The audit results suggest that early GDM is much closer in the pathway to developing Type 2 diabetes compared to late GDM. Women with early GDM should be receiving maternity care of similar intensity to Type 2 diabetes. This has impact on current local guidelines which recommend that all GDM should be managed initially in the community after initial dietary education.
