N2  - OBJECTIVE: Regionalization of maternal care and transfer of women with comorbid risk factors to high-volume referral centers has been proposed as a means of reducing severe maternal morbidity and mortality. However, little national data is available on the prevalence of risk factors for maternal mortality by hospital size and volume. We sought to characterize the profile of maternal comorbidity and acute organ injury by hospital volume for patients delivering in the US. STUDY DESIGN: We utilized the Nationwide Inpatient Sample to identify a cohort of women who were hospitalized for a delivery between 1998 and 2010. Annualized hospital volume was categorized as: high volume (>2000 deliveries), moderate volume (1001-2000 deliveries), low volume (501-1000), and very low volume (100-500). Patient files were weighted to provide national estimates and permit generalizability. Prevalence of risk factors for severe morbidity and death were determined along with 15 measures of acute organ injury. RESULTS: A sample of 50,719,279 women was included. Women delivering across volume categories had similar rates of gestational diabetes and hypertension, mild preeclampsia, previous cesarean section, placenta previa, and fetal demise. Less common risk factors such as lupus, pulmonary hypertension, HIV, and congenital heart disease were more common at high volume hospitals; however many women with these conditions delivered at lower volume hospitals. While risk for major morbidities such as sepsis, shock, acute liver disease, acute heart failure, and shock was higher at high-volume hospitals, many women with these complications delivered at lower volume hospitals (Table 1). CONCLUSION: While transfer of patients with particularly high risk conditions may represent a means of improving maternal care for a small number of patients, many women who deliver at low volume hospitals will have common comorbid risk factors and experience severe morbidity. Initiatives to improve maternal safety tailored to lower volume hospitals may be required to reduce mortality at these centers. (Table Presented).
