N2  - OBJECTIVE: From 1990 to 2006, the rates of maternal mortality in the U.S. have increased. Cardiomyopathy (CM) and cardiovascular complications have become the leading cause of maternal death. In this study, we sought to identify perinatal outcomes in pregnant women with cardiomyopathy as compared to other types of cardiac disease. STUDY DESIGN: We identified a retrospective cohort study of women that delivered at a single academic institution from 2008-2013 (N=9,026). 143 patients (1.6%) were affected by cardiac disease. Perinatal outcomes were compared in women who carried a diagnosis of cardiomyopathy (N=20) to those with other cardiac diseases. Statistical comparisons used univariate and multivariable logistic analysis to control for confounders (maternal age, parity, race, ethnicity, DM), p<0.05, indicated statistical significance. RESULTS: 143 women were identified, 20(14%) had a diagnosis CM. 60% of patients with CM showed evidence of diastolic dysfunction despite normal left ventricular ejection fractions (systolic function) by echocardiogram. Compared to women with other cardiac diseases, women with CM were more likely to have a cesarean delivery (50% vs. 26%, p<0.05). Pregnancies were more frequently complicated by preeclampsia (20% vs. 7%, p< 0.05), and required longer mean maternal hospital stay (10 vs. 5 days, p<0.05) and ICU admission (40% vs. 10%, p<0.01). There were no differences in rates of gestational diabetes, postpartum hemorrhage, or chorioamnionitis. Women with CM were more likely to have a preterm birth (50% vs. 18%, p< 0.01) with smaller neonates, mean birth weight 2647 gms vs. 2969 gms (p=0.07) (Table 1). CONCLUSION: Among women with cardiac disease those with cardiomyopathy had a higher risk for adverse perinatal outcomes. Therefore, consideration to refer this population for multidisciplinary management at a tertiary care center should be advised. (Table Presented).
