N2  - OBJECTIVE: Emerging data identify pregnancy outcomes as predictors of a woman's future cardiovascular health. We investigated the association between maternal placental syndromes (PS) and the shortterm risk of incident cardiovascular disease (CVD). STUDY DESIGN: We conducted a population-based, retrospective cohort study using a maternally-linked, longitudinal database which includes >1 million live-born infants in Florida from 2004-2007. From this cohort we identified healthy first time mothers and used ICD-9-CM codes to identify the presence of one or more PS (preeclampsia, eclampsia, gestational hypertension, placental abruption and placental infarction). All women were followed until December 31, 2010 (mean follow up 4.9 ± 1.1 years). ICD-9-CM diagnosis and procedure codes were used to capture incident CVD defined as ischemic heart disease, stroke and peripheral vascular disease. We excluded multiple births and women diagnosed with CVD, diabetes mellitus, renal disease, or hypertension in the 5 years prior to pregnancy. Survival analysis was used to generate hazard ratios (HR) and 95% confidence intervals (CI) representing the association between PS and CVD. RESULTS: Among 302,638 women included in the study, 36,694 (12.1%) experienced PS and 2,740 (0.91%) experienced CVD during follow-up. When compared to women without PS, women with PS, were similar in age (24.9 ± 6.1 vs 25.0 ± 6.0 years, p= <0.01 ) and race/ethnicity (52.1 vs 49.3% non-Hispanic white, p= <0.01; 22.7 vs 17.8% non-Hispanic black, p= <0.01 and 21.4 vs 27.2% Hispanic p= <0.01). Women with PS had a 38% increased risk of early, incident CVD (HR= 1.38; 95% CI: 1.25-1.53), which persisted even after adjustment for pre-pregnancy BMI, gestational diabetes, hyperlipidemia, alcohol and tobacco use, and sociodemographic factors (HR=1.19; 95% CI: 1.07-1.32). CONCLUSION: Healthy women whose first pregnancies are complicated by PS are at increased risk of CVD in the years immediately following delivery. (Figure Presented).
