N2  - OBJECTIVE: Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP) may cause stillbirth from a fetal arrhythmia that is not reliably detected in conventional testing. Mechanical PR interval prolongation may identify fetuses at risk for an arrhythmia. Our goals were (1) to determine if fetal and neonatal PR intervals are different between pregnancies with ICP versus controls and (2) to determine if fetal PR intervals correlate with neonatal ECG PR intervals. STUDY DESIGN: This is a multicenter prospective cohort study. Patients with non-anomalous singleton gestations and planned delivery between 36 0/7 and 37 6/7 weeks were recruited. An ICP diagnosis required total bile acids >10 μmol/L. Control subjects did not have ICP. Women with preeclampsia, hepatobiliary disease, SLE or suspected fetal arrhythmia were excluded. Doppler echocardiography was performed between 36 0/7 - 37 6/7 weeks to obtain mechanical fetal PR intervals. Neonates underwent ECG within 48 hours of life. PR intervals were reviewed by a blinded pediatric cardiologist. Prespecified power calculation required 7 patients per group to identify a 14 msec difference. PR intervals were compared between groups using the Wilcoxon test. The Spearman correlation was used to compare fetal and neonatal PR intervals. RESULTS: Nine patients with ICP and seven controls were recruited. Maternal age, race, parity, gestational age at delivery, and presence of hypertension and gestational diabetes were similar between groups. The median neonatal PR interval was 115 msec in ICP patients and 110 msec in controls (p=0.91). The fetal PR interval was not different between groups (Table). There was no correlation between fetal (mechanical) PR interval and neonatal (ECG) PR intervals (rho=0.026). CONCLUSION: This study did not demonstrate PR interval differences between fetuses affected by ICP and controls, nor did it indicate a correlation between mechanical and ECG PR determinations. Findings contrast with published literature, and this discrepancy warrants further investigation. (Table Presented).
