N2  - OBJECTIVE: To determine if maternal glucose intolerance below the conventional gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) cut points is related to an increased risk of maternal metabolic syndrome (MMS) 5-10 years after delivery and if maternal factors at or before delivery can predict MMS. STUDY DESIGN: Women with a 1 hour glucose screen ≥135 mg/dL but normal 3-hour oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) were enrolled at 24-31 weeks in a prospective observational cohort. Women with a history of GDM, diabetes, chronic hypertension, asthma, renal or cardiovascular disease were excluded. MMS was defined as presence (or treatment) of ≥3 of the following: waist circumference ≥88 cm, triglycerides ≥150 mg/dL, HDL-C <50 mg/dL, blood pressure of ≥130/85 mmHg, or fasting glucose ≥100 mg/dL. The development of MMS with factors determined at or before delivery was evaluated using a multivariable logistic regression model yielding adjusted odds ratios (aOR) for maternal age, parity, race/ethnicity, smoking and OGTT values, body mass index (BMI) at delivery, weight gain during pregnancy, gestational hypertension/ preeclampsia and years between enrollment and follow-up. RESULTS: In the 359 women enrolled, fasting OGTT, 1 hour OGTT, smoking during pregnancy, and BMI at delivery were all significantly associated with developing MMS. Only smoking (aOR= 2.77, 95% CI (1.11, 6.89)) and BMI at delivery (aOR= 1.14, 95% CI (1.09,1.20)) remained significant after adjusting for other covariates. MMS developed in 22.6% of the overall cohort and increased by BMI category (kg/m2) at delivery: 7.1% for BMI< 25; 12.9% for BMI 25-<30; 21.0% for BMI 30-<35; 41.4% for BMI≥ 35. CONCLUSION: Lesser degrees of maternal glucose intolerance were not independently related to an increased risk of MMS, but smoking during pregnancy and BMI at delivery were related. 40% of women with BMI ≥35 and abnormal 1 hour glucose screen developed MMS 5-10 years after delivery; these women should be targeted for weight loss and other lifestyle interventions postpartum. (Table Presented).
