N2  - OBJECTIVE: Although gestational diabetes (GDM) resolves postpartum, women with retained gestational weight and obesity are at increased risk of type 2 diabetes (T2DM) later in life. Pregnancy is associated with a total increase in cholesterol and lipoproteins (HDL and LDL). Postpartum, LDL increases and HDL decreases. Reduced levels of gherlin are associated with obesity and reduced leptin with weight change. Oxidized LDL (ox-LDL) is a marker of oxidative stress-related inflammation and the risk of development of cardiovascular disease (CVD). Cardiovascular protection and weight loss are known benefits from metformin, especially in women with diabetes. The objective of this study was to compare changes in lipid profiles and biomarkers for obesity and weight change in the first 6 weeks postpartum among women with GDM treated with metformin or placebo. STUDY DESIGN: This is a secondary analysis of a randomized controlled trial of metformin versus placebo in women with GDM. Blood samples were collected within 24 hours of delivery and at 6 weeks postpartum. A change in the median plasma concentrations of total cholesterol, HDL, oxidized LDL, glucose, insulin, leptin, and ghrelin were compared between groups using Mann Whitney U. RESULTS: A total of 77 women were included, 35 in the metformin group and 42 in the placebo. In the primary study, there was no difference in weight change between groups. The reduction in HDL was significantly less in the metformin group compared to placebo. In contrast, the reduction in ox-LDL was significantly increased in metformin versus placebo. There was no difference in total cholesterol, glucose, insulin, gherlin and leptin between groups. Table 1. CONCLUSION: In the first six weeks postpartum, lipid alterations from pregnancy are restored in GDM women who received metformin compared to placebo. However, metformin had no effect on gherlin and leptin levels. (Table Presented).
