N2  - Objectives: To assess the fetal cardiac structure and function and to evaluate the efficacy of routine fetal echocardiography in detection of fetal cardiac abnormalities in women with normal pregnancy and in the presence of gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM). Methods: In this prospective study, we studied fetal cardiac structure and function in 302 singleton uncomplicated pregnancies and in 302 complicated by gestational diabetes mellitus. Fetal echocardiography (two-dimensional ultrasound and pulsed wave Doppler imaging) was used to assess functional parameters and to detect any cardiac structural abnormality. Data from clinical and echocardiographic evaluation of newborns at birth and 3months was obtained to confirm the antenatal findings. Results: Mean maternal age was 28.9 years (±5.0) in GDM group and was comparable to women with normal pregnancy. The mean HbA1c was6.3%andmeanbodymassindex (BMI) was 28kg/m2. The systolic function as assessed by EF increased significantly in the GDM group as compared with normal pregnancy group independent of glycemic control. The pulsed wave parameters E and E/A ratio were significantly different in two groups. The interventricular septum and fetal ventricular wall thickness were significantly increased in the presence of GDM (P< 0.001). No major fetal cardiac structure anomaly was detected in either of the groups in the study. On follow up after delivery all newborns were assessed clinically and using transthoracic echocardiography to rule out congenital defects. Conclusions: In our study, significant increase in thickness of interventricular septum and ventricular wall were detected in the presence of GDM.
