N2  - Case: A 33-year-old gravida 10 Para 8 was booked for consultant- led obstetric care in view of her history including previous preterm delivery, growth restricted babies, gestational diabetes, seizure in pregnancies with no cause identified and hypothyroidism. She was admitted at 29 weeks of gestation in previous pregnancy feeling unwell and managed in the intensive care unit (ICU) with the differential diagnoses of hyperthyroidism and dehydration. She was admitted at 20 weeks in the current pregnancy with back pain and vomiting. There was severe metabolic acidosis with lactate of 13.4 and was transferred to ICU. Husband informed that she was taking paracetamol with cocodamol. Renal and thyroid function tests were normal with raised bilirubin (57) and ALT (1310). Paracetamol level was 57. Scan confirmed fetal death and she delivered a macerated fetus after induction. USS abdomen, CT head, Chest X-ray and ECHO were normal. Advice was sought from National Poisons Information Service and they suggested that paracetamol or amitriptyline overdose were possible but such high lactate levels unusual. Leeds Liver unit advised to continue N-Acetyl cysteine until INR normalises. Toxicology screen, hepatitis serology, serum iron, ethanol levels, MRI brain, autoantibody screen and lumbar puncture results were normal. Obstetric, neurology and endocrinology follow-ups were arranged. Differential diagnoses were severe metabolic acidosis due to intrauterine fetal death, toxic encephalopathy and paracetamol overdose but the clinical picture was very mixed. She had some features of paracetamol toxicity like hepatotoxicity, lactic acidosis and encephalopathy. Tachycardia and dilated pupils were suggestive of amitriptyline overdose. She is now 20 weeks pregnant in her 11th pregnancy. It is still unclear whether there is any recurrent problem in her pregnancies leading to significant clinical deterioration.
