N2  - Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is defined as glucose intolerance with first recognition during pregnancy[1]. Offspring from GDM pregnancies have a higher risk of cardiovascular diseases in later life, most likely as a consequence of fetal programming[1,2]. We have previously reported that Nrf2 regulated redox signaling in fetal umbilical vein endothelial cells is impaired as a result of increased oxidative stress in GDM [3]. In the present study, we have characterized the phenotype of fetal umbilical artery smooth muscle cells (HUASMC) isolated from normal and GDM pregnancies. Following explant culture, normal and GDM HUASMC exhibited a similar phenotype based on basal intracellular GSH levels, reactive oxygen species production and protein carbonylation. However, transcriptional activation of the antioxidant gene heme oxygenase-1 in response to the lipid peroxidation product 4-hydroxynonenal was significantly diminished in GDM cells, suggesting that adaptive upregulation of antioxidant enzymes via Nrf2 may be compromised under oxidative stress during the pathogenesis of GDM. Furthermore, microarray analysis of the mRNA expression profile revealed an altered expression pattern of genes related to lipid metabolism, indicating that epigenetic modifications in GDM may underlie the increased vulnerability in response to stress, potentially contributing to an increased incidence of cardiovascular disease in the offspring of GDM mothers.
