N2  - Background/aims: Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is maternal glucose disorder that leads to an increased risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD). The endothelium of the feto-placental vasculature is exposed to hyperglycaemia and the pro-inflammatory environment of GDM. Versican is an extracellular matrix (ECM) protein which accumulates in atherosclerotic regions, and due to its interactions with distinct cell types it influences cell proliferation, migration and adhesion, and modulates cell phenotype. By binding to versican, fibrillin is involved in the maintenance of extracellular matrix structure. We hypothesize that the intrauterine, diabetic environment alters the feto-placental endothelial ECM expression by permanent epigenetic DNA modifications. Methods: Primary arterial endothelial cells were isolated from human term placentas of healthy (control AEC) and GDM pregnancies (GDM AEC) and cultured. Global gene expression and DNA methylation were analyzed by microarray (Affymetrix) and by 450K-methylation (Illumina) array, respectively. mRNAs with highest fold changes were analyzed by RT-qPCR. Results: Versican showed concordant DNA methylation and gene expression changes in GDM AEC. Versican gene expression was higher in GDM AEC by 41.1-fold (p<0.001) compared to control AEC. Analysis of the 4 versican isoforms (V0-V3) revealed that V0 was 300-fold upregulated and V3 was exclusively expressed in GDM AEC (p<0.001). The expression of TGFß and fibrillin1, both interacting with versican, was also higher in GDM AEC by 6.5- and 5.6-fold, respectively (p<0.001). Conclusion: ECM protein versican and its binding partners fibrillin1 and TGFß have altered expression and DNA methylation in GDM cells. As the increased versican expression associates with CVD in adulthood, these results suggests that the diabetes-induced changes indeed occur already in utero and define versican as a potential tool of fetal metabolic programming through epigenetic modifications.
