N2  - Problem: Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is the most common metabolic disorder in pregnancy. Based on new International guidelines for glucose tolerance test during pregnancy, close to 16% pregnant women are likely to be affected by GDM. Although GDM has been clinically managed by diet or insulin treatment, composite outcome profile of maternal and fetal morbidity and mortality still remain unaddressed which can have long term health consequences for both the mother and her child such as metabolic syndrome, type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease. Since GDM is diagnosed only after weeks 24-26 of pregnancy, it is of utmost importance that novel predictive assays and treatment modalities are invented. Methods of Study: Inflammation-associated metabolic syndrome resulting in excessive increase in insulin resistance and high glucose levels during pregnancy lead to GDM. The definitive cause of this pathologic inflammation and mild autoimmune type phenomenon associated with GDM is still unknown. As regulatory T cells (Tregs) are vital players in maintaining immune tolerance during pregnancy, we evaluated the phenotype and function of Tregs in the peripheral blood of GDMs and age-matched normal pregnancies. Results: Our data support the hypothesis that Tregs from GDM patients acquire an 'exhausted' phenotype, characterized by high expression of the immune-inhibitory programmed death-1 (PD-1) receptor as compared to normal pregnancy Tregs. Notably, PD-1 blockade upregulated Treg phenotype and rescued immunosuppressive activity of GDM Tregs ex vivo. High expression of PD-1 could be detected on Tregs even at 14-16 weeks of pregnancy in GDM patients. Mechanistically, the PD-1 locus undergoes epigenetic changes in GDM patients that lead to its over-expressed phenotype. Conclusions: These studies demonstrate for the first time that PD-1 expression negatively regulates Treg function in GDM and could serve as a predictive and therapeutic marker for this disease.
