N2  - Introduction: Although cardiomyopathy is common in offspring of diabetic mothers, fatalities are rare. We report the death of a full term macrosomic baby which illustrates important principles. History: Weighing 5.3 kg, the baby was floppy and gasping after NVD. Apgar scores of 2, 1 and 1 were assigned and cardio-pulmonary resuscitation continued until echocardiography confirmed asystole. There were no dysmorphisms, no hydrops, but the liver was palpable at 7 cm. No splenomegaly. Echocardiography revealed gross bilateral hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, very small ventricles and a narrow aortic outlet. The narrowest cardiac walls exceeded 10 mm, the aortic valve and the arch were < 0.5 cm. Mother was Samoan, G7 P6, 37 yrs, with weight 131kg and insulin dependent gestational diabetes. Although this had complicated previous pregnancies she did not attend ante-natal clinics other than at 25 weeks when foetal scan revealed no cardiac abnormalities. At 32 weeks, mother was admitted to ICU with respiratory failure due to asthma and was treated with ventolin and 7 days of prednisone. Her HbA1c was 8.3% and BGL reached 17 mmol/L. An ultrasound revealed no abnormalities in the baby. She did not represent for review until in labour. Discussion: Although some thickening of the intra-ventricular wall occurs in 13% of diabetic pregnancies, thought to be related to excess insulin, gross hypertrophy and fatalities are rare and not entirely related to control of BGL. Ullmo reports significant cardiomyopathy even with HbA1c < 7% in 50% of a neonatal series. The 32 week ultrasound was considered normal in this case, demonstrating that hypertrophic cardiomyopathy can develop rapidly in the third trimester, suggesting a benefit of late third trimester ultrasound and review, at least in uncontrolled diabetic mothers.
