N2  - Background: Maternal nutrition is a major factor in the intrauterine environment, implicated in the aetiology of many pregnancy disorders. Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) is one such disorder. Long-term consequences include: increased risk of offspring obesity, cardiovascular disease, and Type 2 diabetes. Metabolomics is an analytical approach which allows for appraisal of small molecular mass compounds in a biofluid; affected by dietary intake. The aim of this study was to generate hypotheses on relationships between early gestation metabolite profile, maternal diet and GDM development. Method: Maternal serum at 20 weeks gestation and dietary data were obtained from the New Zealand cohort of the SCOPE study. Metabolite profiling was performed using gas chromatography coupled to mass spectrometry (GC-MS); metabolites were identified using R software and an in-house library. Statistical analysis was performed using SPSS v21.0 and MetaboAnalyst. Results: A partial least squares discriminant analysis (PLS-DA) on the profile of unidentified compounds showed a clear separation between cases and controls. Cysteine (P = 0.006) and itaconic acid levels (P = 0.001) were significantly higher in women that subsequently developed GDM. Pre-pregnancy fruit intake and gestational intake of leafy green vegetables modified the risk of GDM development. Conclusions: The separation in the PLS-DA of unidentified compounds demonstrates potential for the early gestation, serum metabolite profile to differentiate between women that subsequently develop GDM, and controls with uncomplicated pregnancies. Raised cysteine and itaconic acid levels may demonstrate a role of early gestation inflammation in GDM development. The dietary data provides evidence of the benefit of fruit intake pre-pregnancy.
