N2  - INTRODUCTION: Although the adverse effect of maternal tobacco use has been well validated, its harmful impact in the setting of gestational diabetes is still unknown. Given the vasculopathy induced by tobacco use and the fetal growth stimulated by maternal insulin resistance, we aimed to assess the impact of maternal tobacco use on glycemic control and pregnancy outcomes in women with gestational diabetes. METHODS: Retrospective cohort study of all singleton pregnancies delivered at a single center with GDM from 2007-2012. Women were excluded for major maternal medical illness, fetal anomalies, and unknown maternal tobacco use status. The exposure of interest was self-reported smoking at any time during pregnancy. Outcomes of interest are included in table 1 and include use of glyburide, dose of glyburide, and use of insulin. Groups were compared using Studentâ€™s t-test, Mann-Whitney U, or chi-squared test, as appropriate. P-value of <0.05 was considered statistically significant. Logistic regression was used to refine point estimates while adjusting for the appropriate confounding factors. RESULTS: Of 760 subjects included in the analysis, 130 (17%) selfreported maternal tobacco use. The risk of select outcomes are seen in table 1. Smokers did not require a significantly higher dose of glyburide to attain glycemic control, nor did they more frequently require insulin. Neonatal acidemia was significantly more common in the smokers. The odds of a small for gestational age (SGA) infant was significantly increased, even after adjusting for obesity and chronic hypertension. (Table Presented) CONCLUSIONS: Maternal tobacco use in the setting of gestational diabetes is associated with increased risks for SGA infants and neonatal acidemia without associated decreases in the risk of macrosomia or birth injury.
