N2  - INTRODUCTION: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy(HCM) and cardiac dysfunction are common in neonates of diabetic mothers. Leptin is a known cardiac mitogen. The objective of this study is to determine if elevated neonatal leptin is associated with HCM and/or cardiac dysfunction in fetuses of diabetic mothers. METHODS: In a prospective cohort study, fetal echocardiograms (echoes) at 36 wks gestation in women with pre-gestational diabetes mellitus (DM) were compared to those in women without DM. HCM was defined as wall thickness of >5mm and cardiac dysfunction as a modified myocardial performance index > 0.43. Umbilical cord serum leptin levels were measured with ELISA. RESULTS: In the DM group, 34/57(60%) had abnormal fetal echoes. Cardiac dysfunction occurred in 22/57(39%) and HCM in 12/57(21%). None of the non-DM patients(20) had abnormal fetal findings. For DM women with abnormal vs normal fetal echoes, there were no significant differences in maternal age(33 ± 5.1 vs 34 ± 5.5 yrs), preconception BMI(32.9 ± 8 vs 32.1 ± 7 kg/m2),Type 2 DM(76% vs 78%),or glucose control(hemoglobin A1C 5.9 ± 0.8 % vs 5.8 ± 0.7%). Neonatal echoes on DOL 1 or 2 demonstrated 9/32(28%) cardiac findings had normalized, 11/32(34%) had hypertrophy, 5/32(16%) had ventricular septal defects, 4/32(12.5%) had patent ductus arteriosus, and 3/32(9.8%) had pulmonary hypertension. In neonates of DM mothers, birth weight was higher in infants with fetal HCM(4312 ± 705 g) compared to those without(3440 ± 788g, P <0.001) yet lower in those fetuses with cardiac dysfunction(3369 ± 948g) as compared to those without(3820 ± 716g, P <0.05). In neonates of DM mothers, leptin was elevated in those with fetal HCM(49.4 ± 35 ng/ml) as compared to those without(20.9 ± 24 ng/ml, P<0.05), whereas neonatal leptin levels were not elevated in those with fetal cardiac dysfunction(27.4 ± 26 ng/ml) as compared to those with normal function(26.8 ± 31 ng/ml). CONCLUSIONS: Elevated neonatal leptin and high birth weight are associated with fetal HCM but not cardiac dysfunction.
