N2  - INTRODUCTION: Women who develop certain common pregnancy complications have a greater chance of developing cardiovascular disease later in life. However, most healthcare providers do not provide postpartum cardiovascular risk counselling or follow-up. The Maternal Health Clinic was established to address this gap in care. It targets women at increased risk of cardiovascular disease in order to inspire lifestyle changes, encourage long-term follow-up, and initiate primary prevention. Here we summarize results from the first 17 months of completed clinic visits. METHODS: Patients experiencing at least one relevant complication in their index pregnancy were referred to the Maternal Health Clinic through standard postpartum order sheets. Patients underwent a complete assessment including screening history, physical examination, fasting bloodwork, and urinalysis. Lifetime and 30-year cardiovascular disease risk estimates, along with a metabolic syndrome calculation, were determined for each patient. RESULTS: Complications most commonly leading to referral were gestational diabetes or impaired glucose tolerance (32.7%), pre-eclampsia (29.3%), preterm birth (29.3%), and gestational hypertension (19.6%). The clinic analysis group (n=92) was compared to historical cohort groups from the Pre-Eclampsia New Emerging Team study. Patients in the clinic analysis group had significantly increased lifetime and 30- year cardiovascular disease risk estimates compared to healthy controls (p<0.0001). Furthermore, 17.4% of the clinic analysis group had metabolic syndrome, compared to 6.78% of healthy controls (p<0.05). CONCLUSIONS: This study demonstrates that the Maternal Health Clinic accurately identifies postpartum patients that have underlying cardiovascular risks which make them susceptible to cardiovascular disease. The clinic may serve as an effective primary prevention strategy.
