N2  - Introducion: The burden of congenital heart diseases (CHD) in India is enormous due to very high birth rate. CHD represent some of the more prevalent malformations among live births and remain the leading cause of death from congenital malformations. Disease prevention has been hampered by a lack of information about modifiable risk factors for abnormalities in cardiac development. Hereby we investigated putative environmental risk factors for CHD in DELHI NCR. Materials and Methods: The study included a total of 300 cases-neonates and infants of echocardiographically proven CHD and equal number of controls; hospital based study conducted at RML Hospital, New Delhi. Data was elicited through the interviews of mothers of children included in the study and analyzed based on case control approach for possible risk factors responsible for CHD (univariate approach). Results: The environmental risk factors associated with CHD were consanguineous marriage (P < .001), preterm baby (P = .012) occupational exposure (P < .001), low birth weight (P < .001), obesity in mother (P = .028), gestational diabetes mellitus (P < .001), passive smoking during gestational period (P = .002). No significant difference were observed with birth order, maternal age during pregnancy, type of diet of mother, quality of drinking water, maternal intake of folic acid, iron tablets, tetanus immunization during pregnancy. Conclusions: Low cost interventions like preventing consanguineous marriage, health counseling and testing during pregnancy, health education to women of child bearing age and improving obstetric care may lower the occurrence of congenital heart disease in India.
