N2  - OBJECTIVE: Fetal hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) and cardiac dysfunction are common in fetuses of diabetic mothers. IGF-I is a known cardiac mitogen. The objective of this study was to determine if elevated neonatal IGF-I is associated with fetal HCM and/or cardiac dysfunction in fetuses of diabetic mothers. STUDY DESIGN: In a prospective cohort study, fetal echocardiogram findings at 36 wks gestation in women with pre-gestational diabetes mellitus (DM) were compared to those in women without DM. HCM was defined as septal or free wall > 5mm and cardiac dysfunction as a modified myocardial performance index (MPI) > 0.43. Cord serum IGF-I levels at delivery were measured with ELISA. Neonates with abnormal fetal echocardiogram were followed until resolution or 6 months of life. RESULTS: 77 participants underwent fetal echocardiography (57 diabetics and 20 controls). In the diabetic group 34/57 (60%) had abnormal fetal echocardiograms with cardiac dysfunction in 22/57 (39%) and HCM in 12/57 (21%). None (0/20) of the control patients had abnormal findings. There were no significant differences in those diabetic women with normal versus abnormal fetal echocardiograms (Table). Follow-up neonatal echocardiograms performed at 24-48 hrs showed 9/32 (28%) neonatal echocardiograms had normalized 11/32 (34%) had HCM 5/32 (16%) had ventricular septal defects 4/32 (12.5%) had patent ductus arteriosus, and 3/32 (9.8%) had pulmonary hypertension. At 6 months of age 1/11 (10%) had persistent HCM. In fetuses with normal echocardiograms, mean neonatal IGF-I was 62±17 ng/ml compared to 60.7±19 ng/ml with dysfunction (P>0.05) and 80.3± 6.3 ng/ml with HCM (P <0.01). Although neonatal IGF-I was elevated in DM fetuses compared to controls (65.4±18.4 ng/ml vs. 51.4 ±12.6 ng/ml, respectively, P<0.01), this difference was not significant when adjusted for HCM. CONCLUSION: Elevated neonatal IGF-I is associated with fetal HCM in fetuses of diabetic women and may contribute to pathologic heart development in fetal life. (Table presented).
