N2  - Case Report: Right-sided isomerism, one of the heterotaxy syndromes, is a rare birth defect resulting from embryonic disruption of left-right axis determination. It is marked by cardiac defects, situs inversus or ambiguous, asplenia, midline liver, and other anomalies. We describe phenotypic discordance for right-sided isomerism in monozygotic twins. A 30 year old G2P1, pregnant with monochorionic monoamniotic twins, was found on routine prenatal ultrasound to have evidence of heterotaxy syndrome in only one of the twins. The liver of this twin was midline, stomach was right-sided, and the heart had multiple anomalies. Her pregnancy was otherwise notable only for development of diet-controlled gestational diabetes. She was admitted at 28 weeks for continuous monitoring given her risk of cord accident. She went into spontaneous preterm labor at 32 weeks and 5 days gestation, and was delivered via cesarean. The affected twin weighed 1510 grams, and her twin weighed 1870 grams. Postnatal abdominal ultrasound showed a leftsided liver, right-sided stomach, and absent spleen. Echocardiogram showed pulmonary atresia, a common atrium, double-outlet right ventricle with ventricular septal defect, right-sided aortic arch, and left-sided superior and inferior vena cava. Head ultrasound was normal. The neonate is now 10 days old, and genetic work up thus far has included a normal microarray. She remains in the intensive care nursery for monitoring of her cardiac status, respiratory distress, and prematurity. Cardiology ismaking plans for surgical correction of her cardiac anomalies. Phenotypic discordance inmonozygotic twins has been described in several conditions in which epigenetic and/or environmental factors are considered pathogenically important, such as gastroschisis and syndromes including Beckwith-Wiedemann, Goldenhar, and Klippel-Feil. Postulated mechanisms include epigenetic factors such as imprinting, skewed X-inactivation, post-zygotic mutation or mosaicism, and environmental influences. Interestingly, laterality defects are more prevalent in the right-sided twin of conjoined twins, and the twinning process is thought to influence laterality. Although most cases of heterotaxy are sporadic, phenotypic discordance in monozygotic twins is likely to result from a multifactorial process.
