N2  - Background: Some patients with type 2 diabetes (T2D) display impaired left ventricular (LV) diastolic filling and mild LV hypertrophy in the absence of coronary artery disease (diabetic cardiomyopathy). The role of pre-gestational diabetes in the development of diabetic cardiomyopathy is poorly understood. Methods: We performed a cross sectional comparison of measures of LV diastolic function andmorphology between T2D youth exposed to T2D throughout gestation (cases; n=2) and T2D youth exposed to normoglycemia during gestation (controls; n=3). LV diastolic functionwas determined fromDoppler-derived transmitralflowrates and tissue Doppler measures of LV relaxation. Co-variates assessed included 24-hour blood pressure monitoring, body composition, duration of diabetes and conventional cardiovascular risk factors. Results: Compared to controls, cases were of similar age (16 vs. 16 years), had similar systolic blood pressure loads (time spent > 95th %ile for sex and height = 45 vs. 52%), similar BMI Z score (1.2 vs.1.0), and had shorter duration of diabetes (2.5 vs. 5 years). Cases displayed 30% lower transmitral filling dynamics (E/A: 1.32 vs. 1.80) and reduced relaxation of the mitral annulus during early filling (E'/ A' = 1.18 vs. 1.76). No differences were observed in ejection fraction (70 vs. 65%), posterior wall thickness (9.4 vs. 8.9 mm), septal wall thickness (8.0 vs. 8.9 mm) or LV mass. Conclusion: Using a very unique, extensively phenotyped cohort of youth with T2D, these data provide rationale for further investigation of the role of fetal programming in the development of diabetic cardiomyopathy in youth with T2D.
