N2  - Objectives Obesity is one of the biggest challenges facing maternity services today. A pregnancy complicated by obesity increases the risk of preeclampsia, gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), thromboembolism, dysfunctional labour, operative intervention and postpartum haemorrhage. These can potentially be reduced with good quality clinical care. To compare current practice at Royal Derby Hospital with the RCOG/CEMACE guideline on management of pregnant women with morbid obesity. Methods A retrospective case-note audit of all women delivered at Royal Derby Hospital (RDH) between 01 September 2011 and 31 August 2012 with booking BMI ≥40 kg/m2. The data were collected using a standardised proforma and analysed using Microsoft Excel. Results There were 6233 deliveries at RDH with 140 (2.25%) patients with BMI ≥40 kg/m2. We analysed 134 case notes as insufficient data were available for the remaining six patients. The majority of patients had been 20-29 years of age (62.6%) and were multiparous (65%). A glucose tolerance test was performed in 95.5% of patients at 28 weeks of gestation with obese patients more likely to develop gestational diabetes (14.8% versus 2.1%). Fifty-six percent of the study population had an anaesthetic review. Fifty percent of women laboured spontaneously, 37.4% of labours were induced and 12.6% had elective caesarean section (CS). There was a higher emergency CS rate among the obese patients as compared with the general population (24.8% versus 17.1%). 65.8% of women had a normal vaginal delivery and the instrumental delivery rate was 9.4%. 87.7% of operative deliveries were either performed or supervised by ST6 and above. 96.2% had active management of third stage with major postpartum haemorrhage occurring more in this group as compared with general population (7.5% versus 3.1%). Obese patients were also more likely to have babies with a birth weight >4 kg (21.6% versus 10.6%). 50.8% had postnatal thromboprophylaxis with 73.4% on sufficient thromboprophylaxis for weight. Conclusion The audit demonstrated complication rates were higher in pregnant women with morbid obesity. The guidelines were not being adhered to, particularly for anaesthetic assessment and venous thromboembolism risk. All pregnant women with morbid obesity should be seen in consultant-led specialised antenatal clinics as prepregnancy and antenatal risk counselling is vital, with surveillance for preeclampsia in addition to GDM. Review of our local guideline with more training for healthcare professionals involved in the care of this group of high risk patients is required to improve maternal and neonatal outcomes.
