N2  - Case A 27-year-old female with BMI of 28.3 was booked under midwifery care. At 37 + 4 weeks, she was admitted with BP 150/ 100 mmHg, episode of reduced fetal movements and increased symphysio-fundal-height. She had gained 31 kg in her pregnancy increasing her BMI to 39.3. A routine CTG while the patient was asleep and snoring showed a decrease in fetal baseline heart rate with audible deceleration to 48 bpm. Patient was woken up and the fetal heart rate recovered to a rate of 120 bpm after 2.5 min and slowly recovered to previous baseline over 8 min. Pulse oximetry showed maternal oxygen desaturation to 86% during this episode increasing to 98% during normal breathing. As the patient was term, Labour was induced due to concerns about fetal well-being. Continuous CTG showed no further significant bradycardic episodes. Patient had a vaginal delivery of a male infant weighing 3.8 kg with umbilical cord arterial pH of 7.15; BE-2.8 mEq/L. Discussion Obstructive sleep apnoea (OSA) is the most common sleep-disordered breathing in young and obese women. The existence of this condition in pregnancy is often overlooked. OSA in pregnancy was first reported in 1978. Pregnant women suffering from OSA complain of increased snoring, sudden awakenings with gasping, excessive daytime sleepiness. Reported feto-maternal risks associated with OSA include pre-eclampsia, gestational diabetes mellitus, IUGR, stillbirth and acute events such as fetal heart rate decelerations. Berlin questionnaire and Epworth scales are commonly employed screening methods, while Polysomnography remains the definitive diagnostic tool for OSA. The severity of OSA is assessed by apnea-hypopnea index (AHI), and there is some evidence that pregnancy complications increase as the AHI rises. Nasal continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) has been successfully used on pregnant mothers suffering from OSA. Conclusion We therefore, suggest having a high index of suspicion of OSA in obese pregnant women and formulate a mechanism to identify OSA in the antenatal period to decrease risk of adverse maternal and fetal outcomes.
