N2  - Objectives To investigate the management and the outcome of gestational diabetes against the NICE guideline. Methods Design/setting: retrospective study between July 2010- May 2011. Sample: Total 52 patients. Data analysis from electronic patient records, delivery evolution records and the neonatal records. Main outcome measures: maternal weight gain, the mode of delivery, baby details: live birth/intrauterine death, baby weight, baby conditions, neonatal hypoglycaemia, admission to neonatal unit, postnatal GTT (glucose tolerance test). Results A total of 52 patients were identified, of which 49 records were available. Majority are White British but 10% are from Asian background. Age ranges between 21 and 35 years in majority but >15% are over 35. Half of them have BMI 30 and above. Average weight gain during pregnancy was 8.8 kg. Thirty-five percent of patients controlled their blood sugar with diet alone. Thirteen percent were given insulin and 29% was given medications (metformin). Total 23% received both insulin and metformin. Thirty-eight patients have normal vaginal deliveries. Eleven patients delivered by caesarean section and three required instrumental deliveries. Ninty-eight percent delivered live born babies and 2% have intrauterine fetal death. Thirteen babies were weight over 4 kg and three of them have shoulder dystocia. Ten babies developed neonatal hypoglycaemia and 3 were admitted to neonatal care unit. Total 7 patients continued to have abnormal postnatal GTT. Sixteen patients didn't have postnatal GTT. Conclusion Screening for diabetes was performed as per NICE guideline but cut off level for BMI >35 was used instead of 30. All patients received full health educations from the diabetes team. However, only 75% was seen by Dietician which is lower than NICE guideline. Average weight gain during pregnancy was 8.8 kg which is in normal range but weight was not recorded regularly. Majority of patients induced between 38 and 39 weeks and delivered vaginally. Only 20% required caesarean section which is equivalent to normal populations. One baby has congenital heart disease and one died in uterus. Three of 13 big babies had intrapartum complications. Twenty percent developed neonatal Hypoglycaemia which is higher than the acceptable level. Abnormal GTT are associated with increased risk of large vessel disease and with increased risk of progression towards overt diabetes. These patients needed to be follow-up with postnatal GTT but one-third of them didn't have their GTT.
