N2  - Introduction. The recent European Society of Cardiology classification of cardiomyopathies divides hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) into familial/genetic and non-familial/non-genetic forms. HCM in infancy recognizes a large number of aetiologies markedly heterogeneous. At the first evaluation infants with HCM usually present a heart murmur or symptoms of congestive heart failure such as dyspnea, poor feeding, excessive sweating and growth arrest. Natural history is still poorly known justifying the difficulties encountered in risk stratification. Objectives. The aim of the study was the clinical and morphological characterization of a population of infants with HCM. Genotypephenotype correlations, natural history and prognosis of each specific aetiology were carefully analyzed as well as diagnostic pathways and distinctive features (red flags). Materials and methods. 35 patients with HCM presenting in infancy followed at the Center for Pediatric Cardiology and Cardiac Surgery of S. Orsola-Malpighi Hospital in Bologna between 1975 and in 2010. For each patient, a 12-lead ECG, M-mode and B-mode echocardiography, and blood and urine tests were systematically performed. Diagnosis of HCM was based on the finding of regional or global left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH) based on strict criteria (wall thickness 2 > SD above the normal population mean for body surface area) in the absence of a defined hemodynamic cause such as hypertension, congenital heart disease, or exposure to drugs known to cause cardiac hypertrophy. Results. Among 35 patients (14 females) 60% (n=21) had idiopathic HCM (5 patients with genetic documentation of sarcomeric mutations), 11.4% (n=4) mitochondrial diseases, 8.5% (n=3) Noonan syndrome, 8.5% (n=3) Pompe disease, 8.5% (n=3) LVH due to maternal gestational diabetes, 1 patient (2.8%) had LVH due to twin-twin transfusion syndrome. It was conducted a careful research of specific red flags through anamnesis and family history collection, physical examination, ECG, and cardiac imaging which found a lot of correlations: for example facial dysmorphism in association with extreme right axis deviation on ECG and earlier development of heart failure were strongly suggestive for Noonan syndrome while the set of multiorgan involvement, biventricular hypertrophy, short PR interval and high voltage QRS complexes oriented toward a metabolic-mitochondrial disorder. The latter condition was invariably associated with a poor prognosis while LVH due to maternal diabetes or twin-twin transfusion syndrome showed a recovery within the first year of life and no associated mortality. Conclusion. HCM in the first year of life recognizes a particularly high number of underlying aetiologies. In this difficult scenario there is the need to have a cultural heritage which goes beyond single physician's specialties and to adopt a cardiomyopathy specific mindset in order to notice diagnostic clues (cardiac and extracardiac) and to guide rational selection of diagnostic tests, including molecular genetic analysis.
