N2  - Gestational diabetes (GDM) and Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) are common endocrine disorders which affect women of reproductive age. Both diseases are characterised by insulin resistance and associated with pregnancy-related problems as well as cardiovascular risk factors and long term complications like type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease. These women are often overweight or feature a metabolic syndrome. GDM is defined as any degree of glucose intolerance with onset during pregnancy and is associated with increased feto-maternal morbidity as well as long-term complications in mothers and offspring. In case of a pathological OGTT a strict metabolic control and fetal monitoring is mandatory to minimize perinatal morbidity. After delivery all women with GDM have to be reevaluated as to their glucose tolerance and every 2 years in case of normal glucose tolerance. All women with prior GDM should be instructed about their 7fold higher relative risk of type 2 diabetes at follow-up. PCOS affects about 5-10%of women of reproductive age and is one of the leading causes of female subfertility. PCOS is characterised by hyperandrogenic state, irregular menses, anovulation and hirsutism. Metformin therapy can improve pregnancy rates and early pregnancy loss. PCOSwomen are at increased risk ofGDMif they become pregnant. Women with GDM and/or PCOS should be instructed about possibilities for diabetes prevention, in particular weight management and increase of physical activity. Healthy lifestyle is recommended for these young women and their children to prevent obesity, diabetes and cardiovascular disease in this specific population at increased risk.
