N2  - Objective: A considerable proportion of women develop hypertension or diabetes during pregnancy. Although these conditions subside after delivery, many of these women are predisposed to future diabetes mellitus, hypertension and cardiovascular disease (CVD). Here, we set out to study the impact of a history of pregnancy-induced hypertension and diabetes on the risk of hypertension, diabetes and cardiovascular events later in life. Methods: Women were selected from the PROSPECT-EPIC study, a population based cohort study among 17,357 women aged 49-70 that were examined between 1993 and 1997. 15,175 women reported to have been pregnant, and were without known hypertension or diabetes before pregnancy, nor had an onset of hypertension or diabetes at inclusion. Medical history about pregnancy was obtained using a detailed questionnaire. At baseline, information was collected about CVD risk factors, hypertension, diabetes and prevalent CVD. Participants were followed up for the occurrence of new cardiovascular events and the development of hypertension and diabetes mellitus. Data were analysed by logistic regression with adjustment for age, smoking, BMI, CVD history, physical activity, age at menopause, social and lifestyle factors. Results: After adjustment for known confounders, a history of hypertensive pregnancy was associated with a 2.92-fold increase to develop chronic hypertension (P<0.001), a 1.25-fold increase in all CVD events (P<0.001) and a 1.29-fold increased risk for acute myocardial infarction and stroke (P<0.001). Similarly, gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) was associated with a substantial 4.70-fold adjusted risk of developing diabetes mellitus later in life (P<0.001). Conclusion: Pregnancy-induced hypertension and diabetes have a substantial impact on the overall risk for the development of cardiovascular events later in life. Pregnancy-related cardiovascular complications (e.g. diabetes and hypertension) may have a strong potential to improve CVD risk assessment algorithms needed to guide prevention and treatment recommendations for CVD in women.
