N2  - OBJECTIVE: Little data exist on multiparity in women with repaired tetralogy of Fallot (ToF). An uncomplicated first pregnancy is often considered a good prognostic indicator for future pregnancies. We assessed the impact of number of pregnancies on peripartum and long-term cardiac outcomes in this group of patients. STUDY DESIGN: Obstetric and cardiac data on women cared for at a tertiary institution over a 10-year period were analyzed. Pre-, peri-and post-partum echocardiographic studies were reviewed. The relationship between clinical variables and peripartum and cardiovascular outcomes was sought. Multivariable logistic regression was used to assess the impact of multiparity on a composite long-term adverse outcome defined as death, heart failure, or unanticipated cardiac surgery. RESULTS: There were 94 pregnancies (median 3.0 [1-10]) in 32 women resulting in 79 live births. All women except 1 were NYHA class I at onset of pregnancy. Forty (38/94) percent of pregnancies occurred in women with an RV to pulmonary artery (PA) conduit in place. Progressive valvar obstruction or insufficiency occurred in 23% & 55%, respectively. Cardiac complications occurred in 24 (25%) pregnancies including new onset heart failure (CHF) in 21, pulmonary emboli in 5, endocarditis in 1, and new arrhythmias in 9. Absence of complications with initial pregnancy did not correlate with risk with future pregnancies (r=0.3, p =NS). Obstetrical complications included preeclampsia in 6, gestational diabetes in 2 and PPROM in 2. Premature delivery occurred in 15 (19%), neonatal heart disease in 5 (6%), and perinatal death in 4 (5%). Long-term adverse outcome occurredin 15/32 (47%) women. Each subsequent delivery increased the odds of a composite adverse long-term outcome 4-fold despite adjustment for BMI and age at first pregnancy (OR 4.02, 95% CI 1.22,13.21). CONCLUSION: Women with repaired ToF often deliver preterm. Peripartum CHF is common. Multiparity appears to increase the risk of long-term adverse cardiac outcomes.
