N2  - OBJECTIVE: Fetal hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is a well-described and potentially life- threatening fetal cardiac anomaly thought to be related to maternal diabetes. In rodent models, uncontrolled maternal gestational diabetes results in poor obstetric outcomes including restriction of fetal cardiac growth (Reinking, 2009). As uncontrolled maternal diabetes does not reflect current obstetric practice, the aim of this study is to determine if moderately controlled gestational diabetes induces fetal HCM in a rat model. STUDY DESIGN: Timed pregnant Sprague-Dawley rats were treated with streptozotocin on day 12 (midgestation) to induce gestational diabetes mellitus (DM group). Control rats (C) received insulin injections. DM rats received twice daily insulin injections to maintain blood glucose at 200-400 mg/dl while C rats received saline. Rats were harvested on day 19 (term). A distribution curve of the heart:body weight ratio was created for the C fetuses and HCM was defined as a heart:body weight ratio of > 95th percentile. RESULTS: As displayed in Table, the DM rats (n = 7) had significantly higher morning and evening glucose levels compared to C (n = 8). The DM fetuses (n = 82) had significantly higher heart, body and heart: body weight ratios compared to C fetuses (n = 72). Notably, 0/72 C fetuses had HCM versus 12/82 (15%, P<0.001) of the DM fetuses. CONCLUSION: Moderately controlled gestational DM induces fetal HCM in a rat model. This compelling animal model provides an exciting platform to elucidate the role of potential cardiac mitogens in the pathogenesis of diabetes-induced fetal HCM. (Table Presented).
