N2  - Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is characterized by hyperglycemia and/or impaired glucose tolerance and affects 2-7% of pregnancies, with both the mother and offspring of GDM pregnancies having an increased susceptibility for diabetes and cardiovascular disease in later life. A suboptimal intrauterine environment can expose the developing fetus to enhanced oxidative stress resulting in fetal programming. We have previously shown that GDM impairs antioxidant defences mediated by the redox sensitive transcription factor nuclear factor erythroid 2-related factor 2 (Nrf2) transcription factor (Cheng et al., 2011). Our ongoing work has established that Nrf2 mediated upregulation of protective genes such as heme oxygenase-1 (HO-1) and NAD(P)H: quinone oxidoreductase 1 (NQO1) is significantly impaired in fetal umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVEC) from GDM pregnancies. In the present study, we examined whether the dietary isothiocyanate sulforaphane, a known electrophilic inducer of the Nrf2, modulates antioxidant stress protein expression differentially in HUVEC from normal and GDM pregnancies. Cells were treated with sulforaphane (2.5μM) for 0-24h, and cell lysates immunoblotted for HO-1 and NQO1 and mRNA levels determined by qRT-PCR. Basal levels of HO-1 and NQO1 mRNA and protein expression were lower in GDM HUVEC, however sulforaphane significantly upregulated mRNA (4h) and protein (12h and 24h) levels. Our findings suggest that compromised antioxidant defences in GDM resulting from fetal programming during development, may be rescued by sulforaphane. We hypothesize that pre-treatment of pregnant women with sulforaphane may potentially maintain antioxidant defences and thereby delay the onset and consequences of GDM.
