N2  - Women with pre-existing diabetes before pregnancy have a 5-fold increased risk of congenital heart defects in their offspring. Major congenital heart diseases involving malformation of the septum, ventricles, atria and great arteries have been reported in humans and diabetic animal models. Whether the coronary artery formation is affected by maternal diabetes remains elusive. In this study we investigated coronary artery formation in the offspring from mice with pre-gestational diabetes. Adult C57BL/6 female mice were treated with multiple low dose streptozotocin to induce diabetes. One week later, they became diabetic with elevated blood glucose levels, and were bred with C57BL/6 males. Their offspring was harvested at E9.5, E12.5 and P1. Hearts were serially sectioned to evaluate coronary artery morphology and immunostained for Wilm's tumor (Wt)-1 protein to study changes in proepicardium organ (PEO) and the epicardium. Our results showed that coronary artery size and total coronary artery volume were significantly decreased in the offspring of diabetic mice at P1. PEO at E9.5 was similar in size between the offspring of diabetic and non-diabetic mice. However, a significant reduction in the number of epicardial cells was observed in the diabetic offspring at E12.5. Moreover, these embryos showed reduction of myocardial mass. We conclude that pre-gestational diabetes induces coronary artery malformation possibly by disrupting epicardial development, which is critical for formation of coronary arteries through epithelial to mesenchymal transformation.
