N2  - Introduction: Letrozole is a third-generation selective aromatase inhibitor used initially and now widely recommended for the treatment of postmenopausal breast cancer. Recent reports have shown that letrozole can also be used as an ovulation induction agent. It has significant advantages over clomiphene (CC) due to the lack of peripheral antiestrogenic effects and is associated with similar or higher pregnancy rates as compared to CC and other ovulation inducing agents. One major concern is the possible teratogenicity of letrozole, possibly because of its popular use in cancer therapy. Aim of this study is to evaluate the incidence of congenital malformations among offspring of mothers born after CC or letrozole treatment for infertility. Materials and methods: The present study is a retrospective study conducted at the Institute of Reproductive Medicine, Kolkata, India to identify and follow 452 babies born to women conceived following usage of either CC or letrozole treatment between January 2007 and December 2011. Women undergoing ovulation induction or augmentation for timed intercourse or intrauterine insemination received either letrozole (5mg) or CC (100mg) administered orally for 5 days from day 3 to 7 of the cycle. The demographic data and pregnancy outcome from the medical files of both mother and baby were retrieved and verified with the parents by telephone calls. Telephonic inquiry was done about birth weight, congenital malformations, developmental milestones and neurodevelopmental outcome of the babies after the discharge from the hospital till their present age. Data could not be retrieved for 16 babies in letrozole group and 21 babies in clomiphene group. Complete data collection was possible in 452 babies. The definition of congenital malformation, deformations and chromosomal abnormalities as stated in Chapter XVII, ICD-10 World Health Organization, International Statistical Classification of Diseases and Related Health Problems was used. Statistical analysis was performed by using student t- test or fisher exact test as applicable. Statistical significance was accepted when P ≤ 0.05. Results : Both the groups were statistically similar with respect to age, obstetric history, family history of birth defects, presence of medical disorders or the simultaneous use of drugs, smoking, or alcohol during pregnancy. The birth weight of babies in the CC group were lower than babies in the letrozole group (2.563 ± 0.424 vs 2.473 ± 0.439, p = 0.028). Congenital malformations and chromosomal abnormalities were found in 5 of 201 newborns in the letrozole group (2.5%) and in 13 of 251 newborns in the CC group (5.2%), the difference being statistically insignificant (p = 0.23). One out of 201 babies (0.5%) in the letrozole group was diagnosed with ventricular and atrial septal defect. In the CC group, 3 babies were found to have congenital heart disease (1.2%), out of which 2 babies had PDA and one had total anomalous venous connection (NS, P = 0.32). One mother had gestational diabetes in CC group. Other anomalies in letrozole group were paraumbilical hernia, congenital deafness, talipus varus and albinism in one child each. Amongst the babies born after clomiphene treatment, one had neural tube defect. Three babies in this group had hypospadias and one each with down's syndrome, duplication of urethra, cleft lip & palate, hernia and talipus varus. Conclusions: Overall rate of congenital malformations was statistically similar in both the groups. The concern that letrozole use for ovulation induction could be teratogenic seems unwarranted. Short half life of letrozole (45 hrs) ensures complete washout of the drug before implantation and the earliest stages of embryogenesis when used in the early follicular phase.
