N2  - Background: Following institution of NICE and ADIPS guidelines in our unit we assessed the changes in clinician management. Method: We reviewed outcomes prior to (pre-2008) and after the institution of the above guidelines (2008-2009). This consisted of routine fetal heart rate (FHR) monitoring at 36 weeks and growth ultrasounds at 28, 32 and 36 weeks gestation. Insulin requiring women with unstable control were delivered by 39 weeks and those with optimal control were delivered at 40 weeks. Women with diet controlled gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) were delivered by 41 weeks. The latter were followed throughout pregnancy in a routine antenatal clinic with review by obstetricians, diabetes educators, and dieticians. Data were obtained from 7 October 2006 until 31 December 2009 (OBSTETRIX). Results: There were a total of 601 women; 211 women and 390 respectively. Women with insulin-controlled diabetes in pregnancy were delivered later (P = 0.036) with clinicians undertaking a more conservative approach to induction of labour in this group of women. There was a trend to a reduction in rates of IOL 57% vs. 44% (P = 0.059) and the majority of women in the second group laboured spontaneously. There were no differences between the two groups in birthweights >4000 g; or rates of shoulder dystocia, caesarean section, instrumental delivery, admissions to the nursery (NICU and SCN) and Apgar scores <7. Conclusions: Institution of the newer guidelines resulted in later IOL in the women requiring insulin with no increases in adverse outcomes with the change of management.
