N2  - BACKGROUND: Fertility medications are used for one percent of births yet may also lead to endothelial injury with long-term adverse consequence for the mother. We tested whether fertility therapy might contribute to subsequent cardiovascular disease. METHODS: We performed a population-based cohort analysis of women giving birth in Ontario between July 1, 1993 and March 31, 2010, distinguishing those who did and did not receive fertility therapy in the two years prior to delivery. The primary outcome was a composite cardiovascular end-point of death, non-fatal myocardial infarction, stroke or transient ischemic attack, thromboembolic or heart failure events. RESULTS: Among 1,186,753 women who delivered during the study period, 6,979 gave birth following fertility therapy. After 9.7 years of median follow-up, women who delivered following fertility therapy had fewer primary events than controls (103 vs. 117 events per 100,000 person-years, adjusted hazard ratio [HR] 0.52, 95% confidence interval [CI] 0.38-0.70, p<0.0001). Women who received fertility therapy also had lower risk-adjusted all-cause mortality (HR 0.48, 95% CI 0.30-0.76, p = 0.002) and thromboembolic events (HR 0.43, 95% CI 0.20-0.90, p=0.03). There was no significant difference in cardiovascular risk in those who developed multiple pregnancy, gestational diabetes, or gestational hypertension (p-interaction >0.2 for all). The use of fertility therapy was also associated with a lower risk of subsequent traumatic accidents, depression, alcoholism, self-harm, and sexually transmitted infections (p<0.001 for each). CONCLUSION: Women after successful fertility therapy may be at decreased risk for mortality and cardiovascular events later in life, perhaps because of associated healthier long-term behavior.
