N2  - Objectives: The most significant short- and long-term adverse consequences of Gestational Diabetes (GDM) on the fetus and the mother include the predisposition to the development of metabolic syndrome, Type 2 diabetes, maternal and childhood obesity, as well as cardiovascular diseases. The objectives are to explore those health hazards and identify preventive measures to avoid this disease and its consequences.Methodology: All recent literature on the screening and management of GDM, starting from the Hyperglycemia and Adverse Pregnancy Outcome (HAPO) study till today, were reviewed. Data on maternal obesity and its related variables such as physical activity were also critically assessed.Results: Much of the older literature on the management of GDM are level III, however, newer studies offer level I evidence and introduce new variables for better understanding and applicability. On the other hand, based on meta-analysis of the literature, it was estimated that the risk of developing GDM is about two, four, and eight times higher among overweight, obese, and severely obese women, respectively, compared with normal-weight pregnant women. It is obvious that physiological states of insulin resistance such as obesity and diabetes have been linked to abnormalities in female reproductive function. However, it is difficult to distinguish the direct effects of impaired insulin signaling from those of adiposity or hyperglycemia. Conclusion: Between the two epidemics, obesity and diabetes, maternal and child comorbidities got more complicated and, as a result, put management in a new dimension that passed the aggressive GDM therapy to a wide scale of preventive strategies.
