N2  - Introduction. Diabetes mellitus (DM) is one of the most common medical conditions complicating approximately 3-10% of all pregnancies; pregestational diabetes is found in the 20% of them. Maternal diabetes is known to increase the risk of fetal congenital heart diseases (CHD) which became 5-fold greater that in the general population in the offspring. The patterns of CHD encountered range from small septal defect to complex heart disease. Additionally pathologic ventricular hypertrophy (PVH), commonly referred as hypertrophy cardiomyopathy, is found in 40% of infants from diabetic mothers which causes symptoms in 5%. Aim of the study. To assess the occurrence and the spectrum of CHD and PVH among fetuses and newborns of mothers with pregestational or gestational DM referred to the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology in the last 3 years; to evaluate the postnatal cardiology follow-up. Methods. This retrospective study considered, from January 2007 to December 2010, 128 pregnancies complicated by DM types I-II and gestational diabetes (GD). Cardiac abnormalities were divided in CHD and PVH. CHD were fatherly classified as “severe” CHD if required significant counseling with the family, postnatal surgical intervention or postnatal mandatory cardiac follow-up; “mild” CHD: defects that were not likely to be hemodynamically significant but requiring postnatal follow-up. In the PVH group, the echocardiographic thickness of the interventricular septum (IVS) was measured postnatally before hospital discharge. Hypertrophy was classified as “severe” if Z-score was >3, “mild” for Z- score between 2-3. Results. Among 128 pregnancies complicated by diabetes mellitus, 41(32%) women had pregestational diabetes (36 DM type I and 5 DM type II), 87 (68%) had GD. We found 13/128 CHD (10.1%): 6/13 “severe CHD”: a heterotaxy syndrome with left atrial isomerism and complete atrioventricular canal defect and severe aortic and pulmonary valve stenosis resulted in the death of fetus at 19 weeks of gestation. In one case with fetal hydrops and congestive heart failure (CHF), severe mitral and tricuspid regurgitation and endomyocardial fibroelastosis the mother decided for therapeutic abortion. Four other cases were: double-outlet right ventricle with a ventricular septal defect (VSD) and pulmonary valve stenosis; tetralogy of Fallot, pulmonary atresia with intact septum and a severe pulmonary stenosis with mild right ventricular hypertrophy. In this group the DM/GD ratio was 4:2. Surgical correction or interventional procedure was necessary during the first month of life in all these four newborns without any complication until last cardiologic evaluation. In the “mild” CHD, 5 VSD were observed, in one case associated with mild PVH; a mild aortic insufficiency and a non-Ebstein dysplasia of the tricuspid valve. In this group the prevalence of DM vs GD was 4:3. We observed 26/128 (20.3%) PVH: severe hypertrophy 4/26 (15%), with DM/GD ratio 1:3, mild hypertrophy 13/26 (50%) with DM/GD ratio 5:8. The remaining 9/26 (34.6%) newborn had a IVS measure >5 mm but the z-score was <2 with DM/GD ratio 7:2. In our population all the PVH are transient with a resolution of hypertrophy in the first 6 months of life. Conclusion. In agreement with the literature, women with pregestational DM have a higher risk for CHD in the offspring than women with GD. Both DM and GD seem involved in the development of PVH but in our study severe PVH seems to be more related to GD. All women with DM should be strictly monitored during the periconceptional period for decreasing the risk of severe CHD. Any increased level of plasma glucose obtained with oral glucose tolerance testing is linked to the risk of developing GD with a possible negative pregnancy outcome.
