N2  - Introduction: HELLP syndrome is a severe and life-threatening complication of pregnancywith typical laboratory findings. Sibai et al., established laboratory criteria for the diagnosis: hemolysis by an abnormal peripheral smear, elevated bilirubin 1.2mg/dL, or elevated lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) >600 U/L; elevated liver enzymes by an AST >70 IU/L and LDH >600 U/ L; and low platelets (defined as <100.000/mm3). Objectives: To evaluate the statistics of HELLP syndrome from 2000-2009 in our obstetrics department. Methods: Retrospective study between January 2000 and December 2009. Results: HELLP syndrome was present in 65 cases; corresponding to 84.6% of the cases before delivery and 15.4% post-partum. The mean age was 28.5 ±4.538 [17-39] years old, with a mean gestational age of 32 weeks. The nulliparous' percentage was 64.6%. In previous pregnancies, 2 women had presented HELLP syndrome, 1 gestational hypertension, 1 preeclampsia, 1 preterm delivery and 1 intrauterine fetal death. Twenty percent of the women developed pregnancy hypertension, 4% preeclampsia and Intrauterine Growth Restriction (IUGR) and in 3% gestational diabetes. The average hospitalization-delivery time was 1,88 ± 287 [0-15] days and in 46% of the cases was less than 24 h. Dexamethasone was administrated in 44% and 48% took antihypertensive medication. In 94% the mode of delivery was caesarean section, with 2 stillbirths and 2 perinatal deaths. Eighty four percent of the newborn were admitted into the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit. There were 6.25% admissions in the Intensive Care Unit complicated by subcapsular hematoma in 3% and acute edema of the lung in 3%. The histological study of the placenta revealed chronic utero-placental ischemia in 65% of the cases. Comments: HELLP syndrome complicated 0.2% of the pregnancies, worsening maternal prognosis and perinatal outcomes.
