N2  - Objectives: To assess trend in incidence of, identify risk factors for, and examine associations of eclampsia with maternal and neonatal mortality and severe morbidity. Methods: We conducted a population-based cohort study of all women and their newborns delivered in Canada (excluding Quebec) from 2003 to 2008. The data were obtained from the Discharge Abstract Database of the Canadian Institute for Health Information. We used logistic regression analysis to examine risk factors for and effects of eclampsia on gestational age, caesarean delivery, obstetric embolism, maternal death, and fetal growth, birth asphyxia, intubation, admission to ICU and neonatal death. Results: The incidence of eclampsia declined dramatically from 12.4 per 10,000 pregnancies in 2003 to 6.1 per 10,000 in 2008. Among singleton pregnancies, maternal heart disease [adjusted odds ratio (aOR) 4.8; 95% confidence interval (CI) 2.9-7.3], anemia (aOR 2.6; 95% CI 2.1-3.3), nulliparity, teen pregnancy, diabetes and urinary tract infection all significantly increased the risk of eclampsia. Eclampsia was associated with increased risks of maternal death (aOR 33.1; 95% CI 11.8-92.7), requiring assisted ventilation, adult respiratory distress syndrome, acute renal failure, cardiac arrest/failure, obstetric shock, embolism, very early delivery, and caesarean delivery. However, eclampsia was associated with few neonatal morbidities except for small-for-gestational-age birth, birth asphyxia and admission to ICU. Conclusion: Despite reduced rates and improved perinatal care and management of women with eclampsia, the condition remains strongly associated with serious adverse consequences, especially for the mother.
