N2  - A total number of 5400 candidates, from 1987 up to 2010, were visited for egg donation program. Only 3000 entered the program. 2400 women were rejected during rigorous selection criteria (Antinori et al, 2000), established by the guidelines of WORLD ASSOCIATION OF REPRODUCTIVE MEDICINE. Oocyte donation was performed by IVF and embryotransfer (ICSI -IMSI) in 3000 women (3850 cycles) aged 40-65 years, 2145 were aged 40-50 (griup A) and 855 women were aged 50-63 (group B). A total number of 14850 oocytes were donated (mean±SD number of oocytes per recipient 3.8±0,60) and 13840 were fertilized (93%). 12420 embryos were transferred and mean number of embryos per patient was 3.2±0.82. Overall 1820 (47%) clinical pregnancies were achieved in 3850 recipient cycles. 1695 babies were delivered from 1550 pregnancies (40%). 270 were lost (14.8%). 1348 (87%) pregnancies reached full term. 202 were lost (13%) were premature deliveries (related to all deliveries). Multiple gestation were 140 (9%) and included 135 (8.7%) twins. Triplets were not frequent: 5 sets of triplet (0.3%) and no quadruplets. Antenatal complication occurred in 220 patients (14.1 of deliveries). The frequency of preterm deliveries was 13% (202 patients), gestational hypertension 10.6% (165 patients) preeclampsia 1.2% (19 patients), gestational diabetes 1.8% (28 patients), and abruptio placentae 0.5% (8 patients). Neonatal complications included growth retardation in 2 cases, and there were no neonatal or maternal deaths. In the case of the 63 years old woman she reached full term pregnancy in July 1994 and delivered a boy, at present 17 years old, in good health. The pregnancy rate did not differ significantly in the two groups. Group A n=1340 (46.4%), group B n=480 (45.7%) There were 148 out of 1240 (11.9%) preterm deliveries in group A compared to 54 out 410 (13.1%) in Group B. There was no difference between the two groups as to multiple pregnancies, including 104 out of 1190 in Group A (8.7%) and 36 out of 410 deliveries in group B (8.8%). Abortion rate was very similar in both groups: abortion occurred in group A (n=200: 14.9%) as compared with group B (n=70, 14.8%). Primary attention must concentrate on the outcome of gestation in postmenopausal women. In order to investigate pregnancy risks such as hypertension, preeclampsia/eclampsia, renal and hepatic disease, diabetes, abruption placentae, hemorrhage, cardiovascular disorders and premature deliveries, we analysed over 24 years our series of recipients (40-63 years old) in Italy and in various European and extraeuropean countries, in which 1820 clinical pregnancies were achieved and 1550 delivered 1695 babies, with 270 lost. 1369 (88%) of all deliveries were by Caesarian section. We are able to report that postmenopausal women do not have a greater risk if they do not experience any medical disorders. Many risks to the fetuses and mothers are correlated with multiple pregnancy. The incidences of pregnancy-induced hypertension and preeclampsia are increased in multiple pregnancies. Primigravida women with pregnancies involving twins have a five-time greater risk of severe preeclampsia than in the case of single pregnancies; the risk for multigravida women is 10 times greater. The risk of hypertension is reported to be greater in monozygotic twins. When applying the strict criteria of selection, the maternal and foetal risks during gestation and at birth are not much higher than the risks for women aged between 20-45 years. Pregnancy in postmenopausal women is nowadays a widespread reality all over the world.
