N2  - Objective: The Objective of this study is to assess the frequency of detection of congenital malformations of the fetal heart in women with diabetes. Methods: 44 primigravidae with pre-gestational diabetes were examined. All women were sent to the Institute of Obstetrics and Gynaecology, Ministry of Health of Azerbaijan Republic from antenatal clinics for a more detailed examination. These pregnant women during the initial ultrasound examination revealed a suspicion of congenital heart disease of the fetus. Of this group, pregnant women, who had a vested kinship marriages and family history of cardiac pathology were excluded. Also women with complicated obstetric case histories (TAA) and extragenital pathology were excluded. The ages of the pregnant women ranged from 21 to 31 years. They had singleton pregnancies and were at 18-20 weeks from the first day of last menstrual period. The study was conducted based at the Institute of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Ministry of Health of Azerbaijan Republic on an outpatient basis using Sono Scape S 6 which is equipped with Convex probe 7.5 MHz, mode of “fetal heart and Doppler mode (color and pulse). All studies were performed according to FMF (Foun Medicine Fetus). In a survey of 44 pregnant women, 27 (61.3%) fetii had congenital heart disease. Of these, 12 (44.4%) at 19 ± 6 day weeks, had ventricular septal defect and 8 (29.6%) cases had hypoplastic left heart and only 7 (26%) were diagnosed with transposition of the aorta. In 17 pregnant women detailed ultrasound scan did not confirm a diagnosis of congenital fetal heart disease. Conclusion: At result we have found that among women with diabetes who had cardiac pathology of fetus detected at 18-20 weeks of pregnancy ventricular septal defect was prominent.
