N2  - Aims: Gestational diabetes (GDM) affects 3-14 per cent of pregnancies depending on population and diagnostic criteria studied. WomenwithGDMare at increased risk for the development of Type 2 diabetes and possibly cardiovascular disease in later life. Our aimswere to ascertain the incidence of Type 2 diabetes in women 10 years post GDM diagnosis, and to compare metabolic and anthropometric measurements of women who did and those who did not develop Type 2 diabetes (Type 2 diabetes+ and Type 2 diabetes) respectively). Methods: Biochemical (including fasting lipid profiles) and anthropometric data from the Diabetes in Pregnancy Mother and Baby (DIPMAB) study were analysed. Results: Incidence of Type 2 diabetes at 10 years was 10.5 per cent (n = 95). HbA1c at diagnosis of GDM was significantly higher in the Type 2 diabetes+ group [5.31 per cent ±0.18 SD (range 5.1-5.6 per cent) vs. 5.17 per cent ±0.18 SD (range 4.6-5.6 per cent), P = 0.05]; random glucose (mmol/l) was higher (7.6 ± 1.9 SD vs. 6.2 ± 1.8 SD, P = 0.07). Pre-pregnancy weight was significantly greater in the Type 2 diabetes+ group (kg ± SD, 80 ± 9.4 vs. 68 ± 14.2, P = 0.02). Ten years after diagnosis of GDM,body mass index (kg/m2) (28.3 vs. 27.3), waist circumference (cm) (91.9 vs. 87.1) and waist:hip ratio (0.87 vs. 0.83) was higher in the Type 2 diabetes+ group (ns). Systolic blood pressure was 11 mm Hg and total cholesterol 0.3 mmol/l higher respectively in the Type 2 diabetes+ vs. Type 2 diabetes) groups (ns). Conclusion: These data show that women with GDM later developing Type 2 diabetes had higher HbA1c and pre-pregnancy weights at GDM diagnosis. Simple parameters of cardiovascular risk were non-significantly higher in this group suggesting that further research is required to examine these findings in more detail.
