N2  - Aim: The Aim of the present study is to determine the rate, distribution and natural history of cardiac disease that occurred in infants born to diabetic mothers (IDM) at neonatal period, prospectively. Materials and methods: During the period between February 2006 and July 2007, a total of 66 IDM were evaluated by echocardiography (postnatal 0-35th days). Fifty nine (89.5%) of 66 mothers had been diagnosed with insulin dependent diabetes mellitus and seven (10.5%) with gestational diabetes mellitus. Babies having cardiac disorder were followed until improvement of their echocardiographic findings. Results: Macrosomia, hypoglycemia and precordial murmur were established in 47%, 15.2% and 13.6% of babies, respectively. Major congenital heart disease and minor cardiac anomalies were found in 4.5% and 43.9% of babies, respectively. All infants with congenital heart disease had a ventricular septal defect as isolated or associated cardiac lesion. Asymmetric septal hypertrophy (ASH) was found in 54.5% of babies. All were asymptomatic, obstruction of left ventricular outflow tract was not found in any of them. At the sixth and the 18thmonth controls of babies, ASH improved in 86.1% and all of them. Major congenital extracardiac anomalies were not found in none of them. Minor abnormalities (biphid uvula, sacral dimple, capiller hemangioma and pelvic ectasia) were established in 6% of babies. Conclusions In our study, we were determined that the most common cardiac findings in IDM was ASH by using routine echocardiography. In all cases; ASH was asymptomatic and improved spontaneously until the 18th months of ages. IDM should be assessed carefully by means of cardiac findings as soon as possible after birth, and evaluated echocardiographically.
