N2  - Introduction: Coffin-Siris syndrome (CSS) or “Fifth Digit syndrome” (OMIM 135900) is a rare disorder of unknown etiology that was firstly reported in 1970 by Coffin and Siris in three unrelated children. Their phenotypic features were coarse facial appearance, low birth weight, retarded somatic and mental growth, and hypoplastic to absent fifth finger and toenails. Structural anomalies of the brain and congenital hearth defects are present in approximately 30% of reported patients. There are not available cytogenetic or molecular tests to confirm the diagnosis. So it is currently based solely on clinical findings. Case 1: A 1-month-old female patient was referred to our hospital for multiple congenital anomaly and developmental delay. She was born at 35 week gestation to healthy non-consanguineous 19-year-old mother and a 28-year-old father. The birth weight was 1900 g, but the birth length and occiputofrontal circumference (OFC) were not recorded. The family history was non-significant. A heart murmur was noted at the hospital, and the echocardiogram showed coarctation of the aorta (CoA), patent ductus arteriosus, and a secundum atrial septal defect. Neonatal course was marked by difficulty in sucking, requiring intermittent tube feeding, and failure to thrive. Her weight, length, and OFC were 2320 g (<3 centile), 43 cm (<3 centile), and 32 cm (<3 centile) respectively (with correction for gestational age). Physical examination revealed microcephaly, a narrow and high arced palate, coarse facial appearance with broad nasal root, wide mouth with full lips, absent nails of the fifth finger, extremely hypoplastic fifth toenails, absent terminal phalanges of the fifth fingers and fifth toes. Neurologic evaluation revealed hoarse cry and mild hypotonia. Radiographs showed missing terminal phalanges of the fifth digits. Classic type Dandy-Walker malformation and partial agenesis of corpus callosum were disclosed by brain magnetic resonance imaging. Thyroid function tests revealed the presence of primary hypothyroidism. A diagnosis of CSS was made based on the clinical and radiological findings. Cytogenetic analysis revealed a normal 46,XX karyotype. CoA was successfully corrected, oral thyroxin therapy was initiated, and patient was discharged without any post-operative complication. Case 2: A 3.5-year-old male child was referred to our hospital for the assessment of his failure to thrive and dysmorphic findings. He was born at 34 weeks of gestation to a healthy non-consanguineous mother and father. This was a multiple pregnancy and was obtained via in vitro fertilization. The pregnancy was uneventful except the maternal history of gestational diabetes mellitus and use of subcutaneous insulin. The birth weight was 1500 g. The birth length and head circumference were unknown. His female twin and 1.5 years old sibling were healthy. Patient's head control and supported sitting was adapted at 8 months of age, he was able to walk at 18 months of age. He had no speech. On a physical examination, weight, length, and head circumference of the patient were 14 kg (25 centile), 93.5 cm (10 centile) and 48 cm (3-10 centile), respectively. Dysmorphic findings were flat occiput, low set ear, micrognathia, anteverted nostrils and bilateral prominent antihelix. Distal phalanges of fifth digits and toes were hypoplastic bilaterally. Fifth toe of the left foot was rudimented and its toenail was absent. Right undescended testis and sacral dimple were detected. Radiographs showed missing terminal phalanges of fifth digits. Echocardiography demonstrated bicuspid aortic valve, ventricular septal defect, and duplicated superior vena cava. Brain and lumbar magnetic resonance imaging showed diffuse cerebral atrophy and tethered cord at L3. Hearing was evaluated with auto acoustic emission test, and the result was consistent with bilateral hearing loss. Based on these findings, a final diagnosis of CSS was made. Chromosomal analysis revealed a normal 46,XY karyotype. Conclusion: Present cases highlight the extremely variable phenotypic manifestation of CSS. Minor anomalies of the spinal canal were reported in this syndrome. Sacral dimple, spina bifida occulta, kyphosis, and scoliosis are fairly common. Tethered cord has not been described in patients with CSS. Primary hypothyroidism has not been described in CSS. Thus, it remains unclear whether primary hypothyroidism is an incidental finding not causally related to the CSS. The presence of primary hypothyroidism suggested whether the coarse facial appearance and retarded somatic and mental growth in previously reported patients with CSS were at least partially due to hypothyroidism. Other reported patients, even those previously reported, should be studied for the presence of primary hypothyroidism.
