N2  - With 6.5 billion people on the planet we often take it for granted that humans are fertile and traverse pregnancy and delivery with ease. The reality is quite different. About 15% of couples have difficulty conceiving and many require assistance. Of those who become pregnant up to 15% miscarry and another 25% experience preeclampsia, pre-term labour, gestational diabetes or deliver a growth restricted infant. These complications are life threatening to mother or baby in up to 6% of pregnancies and are more prevalent following assisted reproduction. Evidence shows that couples, rather than women, are at risk of poor fertility or pregnancy complications with factors such as family history of cardiovascular disease, pregnancy complications, chronic disease and obesity (in either parent) prior to conception contributing to risk. Poor fertility and pregnancy complications may be on a continuum of reproductive outcomes with similar or overlapping aetiologies. Genetic polymorphisms and epigenetic changes to DNA have recently been identified that predispose couples to poor fertility and compromise maternal and infant health. Gene families and specific pathways that are aberrant in poor reproductive outcomes include pro- and anti-inflammatory cytokines, the insulin and insulin-like growth factor axis, the renin-angiotensin system, one carbon metabolism and vitamin D pathways. De novo effects of a poor quality diet and obesity on the epigenome are emerging as potent regulators of reproductive success and long term health.
