N2  - Background: Obesity is now the second leading cause of death in the United States. During the past 20 years there has been a dramatic increase in obesity, with approximately 9 million adult Americans morbidly obese. Research has shown that as weight increases the risks of coronary heart disease, type 2 diabetes, hypertension, and stroke also increases. Obesity in females of reproductive age carries even further risks: infertility, birth defects, fetal and neonatal death, delivery of large for gestational age infants, delivery complications, gestational diabetes and preeclampsia. Obesity in women can cause serious pregnancy-related complications, but it is a risk factor that can be modified to improve birth outcomes. Hypothesis: With increasing BMI, there will be an increased prevalence of hypertension and hypertensive disorders of pregnancy in women who are morbidly obese. Methods: A descriptive study of a retrospective cohort looking at the prevalence of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy in morbidly obese patients between 10/2005 to present. Patients identified by search of electronic medical record looking at age, gravity, ethnicity, BMI, and comorbidities of DM and CHTN. Morbid obese patient population are then compared to values of the obese population. Results: Significant change in gestational hypertension from 6% to 7% and increase in preeclampsia from 6.5% to 7.5%. Conclusion: Women who are morbidly obese during pregnancy are at increased risk of gestational hypertension and preeclampsia. This information can be utilized by the healthcare provider to identify which patients need targeted intervention both prepregnancy as well as during the antepartum time period.
