N2  - Background. Congenital heart defects (CHD) are prevalent in up to 1.3 million Americans and occur in approximately 36 000 babies each year. Purpose. We assessed the impact of maternal exposure to environmental risk factors on CHD development in pediatric patients. Methods. Prospectively enrolled children <18 years old diagnosed with CHD were included. Frequency of prenatal exposure to environmental risk factors was gathered by intake questionnaires. Control data was sourced from the National Birth Defects Prevention Study (NBDPS). Associations were measured by odds ratios (OR). Results. Two thousand eighty-seven CHD patients were enrolled: conotruncal heart defects (CTD) 28.0%; leftto- right shunt lesions (LRS) 25.4%; left ventricular outfl ow tract obstructions (LVOTO) 24.1%; right ventricular outfl ow tract obstructions (RVOTO) 9.7%, valvular defects (VD) 4.6%; and others 8.2%. The frequency of gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) and advanced maternal age increased in the current era. Case-control comparisons revealed a signifi cant association between GDM with CHD (OR = 1.61, P = 0.002), in particular with LRS (OR = 1.80, P = 0.021), and LVOTO (OR = 1.82, P = 0.02). Advanced maternal age was associated with CHD overall (OR = 1.46, P < 0.001), and with CTD (OR = 1.46, P = 0.027), LRS (OR = 1.85, P < 0.001), and LVOTO (OR = 1.52, P = 0.025) in particular. Smoking was strongly associated with valvular defects (OR = 3.43, P < 0.001). Exposure to recreational drugs was also highest in VD (internal OR 6.42, P < 0.001). General anesthesia was highest in RVOTO (internal OR 2.73, P = 0.026). Conclusion. The increase in GDM and advanced maternal age during pregnancy suggests a rising environmental risk burden for CHD. More importantly, associations between other emerging maternal risk factors with specifi c CHD subtypes suggests an interplay between environmental and genetic risk factors and identifi es an at-risk population that may benefi t from prenatal screening for CHD.
