N2  - Results: The Diabetes Prevention Program (DPP) provided clear evidence that lifestyle intervention focusing on weight loss and increased physical activity can prevent diabetes in persons at high risk. In 2008, the Montana Department of Public Health and Human Services in collaboration with eight diabetes self-management education programs implemented an adapted, group-based intervention including the DPP 16 week core curriculum and 6 monthly after core sessions. Eligible participants had a BMI ≥ 25 kg/m2, and one or more of the following: pre-diabetes, IGT/IFG, a history of gestational diabetes or delivery of a baby >9 pounds, hypertension, or dyslipidemia. Participants were required to obtain medical clearance from their primary care provider, were assessed for readiness to change, and signed a behavior change contract; they were recruited through primary care practices and local media. Participants set goals to reduce fat intake, increase physical activity (>150 minutes/week) and lose 7% total body weight. A total of 1,037 participants were enrolled, 85% completed the core, and 60% attending one or more after core sessions. The mean age of participants was 53, 80% were female, and mean BMI at baseline was 35.8 kg/m2. Average weight loss was 6.7 Â± 5.4 kg (6.7%) and 7.5 Â± 7.3 kg (7.6%) at the end of the core and for those attending at least one after core session, respectively. Forty-six percent of participants met the 7% weight loss goal and 66% met the physical activity goal. Significant improvements were seen in cardiometabolic risk factors overall and among the high risk cohort. Cost per participant was $550. Our findings indicate it is feasible to identify and recruit participants who are at high-risk for diabetes and CVD and achieve similar outcomes to the DPP. This state-based model is a cost effective approach to implementing the DPP. (Table Presented).
