N2  - Results: Objective To estimate 3-year incidence of pre-diabetes or type 2 diabetes among residents aged 40 and over in Taichung city, Taiwan. Methods This was a prospective study with a random sample of 1,631 residents with normal glycemia and without any medication at baseline being followed for 3 years. New cases of pre-diabetes (fasting glucose 100-126 mg/dl) and type 2 diabetes (fasting glucose ≥126 mg/dl or medication) were ascertained from physical check-up and laboratory examination. Results The 3-year age- and gender-weighted cumulative incidence of pre-diabetes or diabetes was 6.4/100 person-year. Significant factors associated with 3-year incidence were history of cardiovascular disease and family history of hyperlipidemia in male and BMI> 25, hyperlipidemia, gestational diabetes or delivering a baby above 4000 gram, higher education level, and longer TV watching time in female. After multivariate adjustment, the odds of developing pre-diabetes or diabetes for males with history of cardiovascular disease was 2.43 times compared to that for male without history of cardiovascular disease (p<0.05). The odds ratio for family history of hyperlipidemia was 2.27 (p<0.05). For women, odds ratios for BMI> 25, hyperlipidemia, gestational diabetes or delivering a baby above 4000 gram, higher education level, and longer TV watching time were 2.15, 4.55, 2.02, 1.87, and 1.84, respectively (all p<0.05). Conclusion In summary, this study identifies different significant risk factors of pre-diabetes or diabetes incidence in men and women, which implying that different intervention programs should be planned separately for men and women aged 40 years and over.
