N2  - Background: Rheumatic heart disease although rare in Australia, remains prevalent in the Indigenous population due to high incidence of rheumatic fever. Aim: To evaluate the impact of rheumatic heart disease on maternal and foetal outcomes and its implications. Methods: 15 patients (age 25±10 years) with 20 completed pregnancies at the Townsville Hospital from January 2003 to April 2009 were studied. Results: The majority of patients were Indigenous or Torres Strait Islanders (85%). Obstetric high risk features included smoking (75%), previous miscarriages (40%), alcohol consumption (40%), multi-parity (30%) and gestational diabetes (10%). Mitral valve lesions were the predominant pathology including mitral regurgitation (50%), mixed mitral lesion (40%), and mixed mitral-aortic valve lesions (10%). No patient had an ejection fraction less than 40%. 60% had RVSP>28mmHg. 30% hospital admissions were cardiac-related. Average hospitalisation time was 10.7 days and time patient away from the community was 22.1 days. 50% patients showed a decline in NYHA functional class throughout pregnancy. 35% had congestive failure and 45% required at least 1 cardiac medication. 60% required Caesarean-section. Foetal complications include premature delivery (45%), low birth weight<2500g (43%) and IUGR (10%). 70% newborns required specialised neonatal care. No maternal or foetal mortality seen. Conclusion: Rheumatic heart disease remains predominant in the Indigenous population. Increased maternal and foetal complications during pregnancy are seen. Prolonged hospitalisation and time away from the community posed significant burden to healthcare system. Noncompliance with contraception remains an obstacle in the management of this sub-group of patients.
