N2  - BACKGROUND: Women with a history of gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) have increased risk of type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease. Postpartum and primary care visits in the year following delivery are frequently missed opportunities to screen for diabetes and counsel about risk reduction, as many women often do not attend these visits. Few studies have explored barriers to followup care in women with GDM. METHODS: Between July 2008 and March 2009, we recruited 23 patients with GDM during their third trimester of pregnancy from a single academic high-risk obstetric clinical practice in Baltimore, Maryland, using consecutive sampling. Inclusion criteria were insurance coverage during and beyond the 6-week postpartum visit and being English speaking. Participants completed a baseline questionnaire; we abstracted information from the medical record about pregnancy and delivery. We conducted semi-structured interviews either in-person immediately following the postpartum visit or if needed, by telephone. Interviews were audiotaped and transcribed. Two investigators independently coded transcripts. Codes, themes and memos were organized and explored using the Atlas.ti qualitative data analysis program. Quantitative analyses were conducted in STATA. RESULTS: We recruited 23 patients and completed 22 interviews, 13 in person and 9 by telephone. One participant declined to be interviewed. The mean age of participants was 31.8 (SD4.6), 61%were non-white, 48%were primiparous, the mean body mass index was 26.7 kg/m2 (SD 7.0), and six participants hadGDMin a prior pregnancy. All deliveries occurred between 36-40 weeks gestation; 11 women underwent cesarean delivery and 3 infants required admission to the neonatal intensive care unit. Nineteen women (83%) attended a postpartumvisit.We identified fourmajor themes addressed by every participant. Sub-categories (listed below eachtheme) illustrate barriers to followup medical care: 1) Experience being a patient with GDM Frustration with high level of self- and clinical monitoring. “In the beginning, I was like, 'Oh, I can,' and towards the end, I was in tears, and it's like, 'Okay,' dreading the needles, dreading all that.” Dissatisfaction with prenatal care. 2) Recent delivery and baby's health Delivery concerns and questions. “I didn't know that the reason they had to get him out so fast was that his heart rate dropped.” Complications with baby/NICU care. 3) Personal and family adjustment to the new baby Baby's unpredictable schedule. I had to make sure that she [the baby] was asleep so I could take a really fast shower and get dressed and get something in my stomach because if I wasn't able to do any of that I probably wouldn't have gotten here. Anxiety/feeling overwhelmed. 4) Concerns about postpartum and future health Fear of bad news of diabetes diagnosis. I was nervous. Just the whole unknowing, 'cause once you know, then it's like, “Okay, now what do you do?” Perception of good health. CONCLUSIONS: Women with recent GDM report multiple barriers to followup medical care. These results will inform the development of interventions aimed at enhancing communication and coordination of care to improve postpartum and primary care for these women.
