N2  - Gestational diabetes (GDM) is associated with an elevated risk of type 2 diabetes later in life. However, it is not yet established whether a history of GDM increases risk of other cardiometabolic disorders after the index pregnancy. We sought to investigate the association of GDM with subsequent risk of hypertension after the index pregnancy among 26,562 women in the Nurses' Health Study II. Subjects were included if they reported at least one pregnancy between 1989 and 2007, but did not report pre-existing hypertension, a cardiovascular disease event, type 2 diabetes, hypertension medication use, pregnancy hypertension, or GDM. Subjects were excluded in follow-up if they reported a cardiovascular disease event. There were 1,567 (5.9%) women exposed to GDM and 2,345 (8.8%) incident hypertension cases. A multivariate Cox proportional hazards model was used to estimate the risk ratio (HR) and 95% CI after the adjustment for age, BMI, family history of hypertension or type 2 diabetes, race, parity, BMI at age 18, subject's birth weight, physical activity, DASH Diet Score, smoking status, alcohol intake, analgesic use, and pregnancy hypertensive disorders. GDM was related to an elevated risk of hypertension, with a multivariate adjusted HR of 1.4 (95% CI: 1.3-1.7; p < 0.0001). As women with GDM are at a high risk of subsequent type 2 diabetes, we also examined the subgroup of women who developed diabetes after GDM exposure. Compared to women without GDM, women with both GDM and type 2 diabetes were 3 times more likely to develop hypertension (HR = 3.0, 95% CI: 2.1-4.2; p < 0.0001).These results indicate that women with GDM are at a significant increased risk of hypertension compared to women who do not have GDM.
