N2  - Because pregnancy in diabetic women is associated with increased maternal, fetal, and neonatal risks, prevention of unintended pregnancy as well as appropriate timing of planned pregnancy (e.g., when the patient is euglycaemic) assumes particular importance. However, contraceptive guidance as part of the pre-pregnancy counseling needs to be more widely implemented by general practitioners and in non-specialized obstetrical and gynaecological departments. Unfortunately up to forty percent of diabetic women do not use contraception. Women with diabetes are generally well motivated, but, concerns about fecundity and potential ambivalence about their desires for pregnancy maybe the reason. Today several effective contraceptive methods are available for women with diabetes. The barrier methods may prove both acceptable and reliable contraceptive agents for some of these women. When, however, a high risk of user failure can be predicted, the Cu-IUD or hormonal contraception including the LNG releasing system may be the only reversible alternatives. Use of currently available combined oral contraceptives (OCs) or progestin only contracetion (POP) as well as the LNG releasing IUS do neither impair metabolic control nor accelerate the occurrence of vascular disease in diabetic women. Likewise, use of combination OCs has not been found to precipitate type 2 diabetes. On the basis of evidence-based observations combined with clinical concerns related to the known increased risk of thrombosis with combined OCs, use of OCs appears appropriate for diabetic women younger than 35 years without late diabetic complications. This also applies to the ring, and the patch. The limited number of studies in certain clinical areas, especially considering contraception for women with type 1 diabetes and women with type 2 diabetes with vascular complications makes the choice of contraceptive a compromise. The balancing between pro and cons will from a clinical perspective depend on evidence of metabolic effects from hormonal contraception and pre-existing conditions such as high BMI, hypertension, coronary artery disease, nephropathy, retinopathy, or other vascular disease. In most cases POP, injectables or the LNG releasing system will be the safe hormonal methods as alternative to a Cu-IUD considering all reversible available methods.
