N2  - Brief Introduction: Peripartum cardiomiopathy is a serious form of dilated cardiomiopathy related to pregnancy. It is defined as unexplained left ventricular systolic dysfunction that needs to be confirmed echocardiographically. Usual treatments consist of standard therapy of heart failure. Some patients may require inotropic therapy, immunomodulation or immunosuppression. Materials and Methods: Literature review and clinical history. Clinical Cases or Summary Results: A secundigravidae 28-year old woman presented a pregnancy that curses with gestational diabetes with poor controls and fetal diagnoses of macrocosmia. At 35 weeks of pregnancy, the patient was admitted to the hospital in order to control glycaemia rates. During her stay in hospital she fainted. An echocardiography was realized. Peripartum cardiomiopathy was considered the main diagnostic possibility. Because of the peripartum myocardiopathy and gestational age, fetal extraction was decided. There is no consensus on the best way of delivery. Because of preterm birth and fetal diagnosis of macrosoma it was decided to perform a cesarean section After delivery, the evolution was satisfactory. An echocardiography was repeated and showed slight improvement and ejection fraction remained low limits of normal. Currently the patient still controls the Cardiology showing slight improvement in her evolution. Conclusions: Prognosis is highly related to reversal of ventricular dysfunction and future pregnancy is nowadays not recommended in patients who fail to recover normal heart function.
