N2  - Brief Introduction: Stroke associated with pregnancy is a feared complication contributing to maternal deaths. The objective of this study is to describe the risk factors and the perinatal outcomes associated to stroke and pregnancy and the puerper-ium. Materials and Methods: This is a retrospective observational study in the Universitary Maternal Infant Hospital of Canary. The period included was: January 2002 to December 2009. The variables studied: maternal age, previous patholgy, substance abuse, parity, single or multiple gestation, complications of pregnancy, type of delivery, perinatal outcomes. Clinical Cases or Summary Results: There were 58,452 deliveries. There were 9 cases of strokes. The incidence was 15/100,000 deliveries. Six of the strokes were intracerebral hemorrhage (67%), two were arterial infarct, 45% happened during pregnancy, and 55% in puerperium period. Forty-five percent of women died. Three women were >35 years (33%), 55% of women had excess weight. Twenty-two percent were smokers. We detected one case of gestational hypertension and three cases of preeclampsia (33%), and all of them were intracerebral hemorrhages. Two cases of gestational diabetes. In four cases, it carried out a cesarean section, three eutocic and two forceps deliveries. The gestational age at delivery was >38 weeks in six cases (67%). Three newborn were trasladated to intensve united care. Conclusions: The majority of the strokes were intracerebral hemorrhage and appeared in puerperium period. The risk factors associated were: age >35 years, excess weight, smoke, diabetes, preeclampsia, cesarean section, and forceps deliveries.
