N2  - Brief Introduction: In Spain an overall pregnancy induced hypertension frequency is 2.23%, 0.23% entitled to HELLP Describe the main clinical and epidemiological issues in gestations diagnosed as HELLP 2004-2009. Materials and Methods: Data of 30 pregnancies with HELLP extracted from data base medical records and registered in SPSS 15 for analysis. Clinical Cases or Summary Results: There were 17,408 deliveries, 7712 newborns; HELLP incidence was 0.17%. Maternal age mean of 30.6; main week at diagnosis was 33; all singleton gestations; 57.6% nulliparous;not prior history of hypertension in 90%, gestational diabetes in 2 cases. 24 women diagnosed of hypertension at current gestation at 31 weeks mean.Symptoms at admission: epygastralgia 26%, headache 20%, nausea or vomiting 20%, arterial hypertension 16%, abdominal pain 13.3%. Blood pressure increased in 87% at admission. Low platelet count, elevated LDH, GOT, GPT in 56%. 36.7% prompt delivered (<24 h) interval 2 h - 7 days and 1 case at 12th day.Summarized mode of delivery: 24 cesareans, 16.7% spontaneous, and 3.3% operative vaginal. Regarding postpartum morbidity: severe hypertension, vaginal hemorrhage, acute renal failure, liver hematoma. Neither placental abruption. 63.3% of newborns submitted to neonatal unit, main criteria was prematurity Conclusions: Although clinical issues are uncertain at diagnosis, some of them have been described frequently and help clinicians to promptly diagnosis HELLP for improve maternal and perinatal outcome.
