N2  - Brief Introduction: Uterine rupture during pregnancy is a serious complication for both mother and fetus. Materials and Methods: Case report. Clinical Cases or Summary Results: Pregnant woman of 33 weeks gestation who complains of intermittent abdominal pain. Refer 6 h ago fetal movements. Background of interest: Gestational diabetes controlled with diet. Multiple abdominal myomectomy 2 years ago without endometrial cavity opening. Exploration: Constants normal. High uterine tone. No metrorrhagia. Closed cervix. Ultrasound: fetus in cephalic, estimated weight 1700 g with no cardiac activity. Normal placenta with no signs of detachment. No hemoperitoneum. Hemoglobin 10.6 g/dl. Normal coagulation. At 2 h, warn of increased abdominal pain, tachycardia and hypotension, with hemoglobin 8.4 g/dl. Emergency cesarean section is performed, noting significant hemoperitoneum. After fetal extraction, a uterine rupture is observed and a small intestine rupture that was attached to the uterus. Subtotal hysterectomy was performed and resection with simple-to-end anastomosis of small intestine. The pathologic reports of uterine rupture with placenta percreta that affects bowel segment resected. Conclusions: In pregnant patients with previous multiple abdominal myomectomy is necessary to take special attention, because the uterine rupture is more frequent. In this case also, the placenta had invaded the small intestine through the previous uterine scar.
