N2  - Background: Higher homocysteine levels are associated with higher risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD) in adults and if present in pregnancy associated with low-birth-weight. This in turn increases the offspring's risk of future CVD. Both folic acid (FA) and B12 are inversely associated with homocysteine levels and their deficiency also increases neural-tube-defects (NTDs). In addition, low B12 in pregnancy is associated with higher incidence of gestational diabetes and higher adiposity in the offspring. However, to reduce NTDs, as B12 deficiency was considered rare, only mandatory FA fortification was introduced in North America (1997-98). In the UK, though voluntary FA fortification of cereals exists, mandatory fortification is being considered. Aim: To assess the FA and B12 status in the UK population and to test the ''interplay'' between them on homocysteine levels using the National Diet and Nutrition Survey data (NDNS). Methods: NDNS was a cross-sectional survey (2000-2001) of representative UK adult population (19-65 years; n = 1297). Results: Prevalence of FA and B12 deficiency were 2.1% and 10.5%. In women of child bearing age (19-39 years), B12 deficiency was higher (11.8%). FA was the strongest determinant of lower homocysteine levels, followed by B12 (28.7&18.6% contribution to the variance). Individuals with highest FA and B12 levels had the lowest homocysteine levels. However, in B12 deficiency (< 148pmol/ L), higher FA levels (>32.6nmol/L) were associated with higher homocysteine levels compared to normal B12 (11.29vs8.99pmol/L; p = 0.003). Conclusions: These data highlight the importance of B12 levels in addition to FA. Increasing FA levels in B12 deficient mothers may increase the CVD risk of their offspring. Therefore, if mandatory FA fortification is introduced, B12 fortification/supplementation must be considered, especially in pregnancy.
