N2  - Description of Case: A 24-old woman, post para comes in with left sided chest pain. Three weeks earlier, she had a caesarean due to fetal distress. No significant past history, except gestational diabetes and panic anxiety. Mobile since caesarean. vs. Tachycardic (136), tachypnic (26), normal BP and normal oxygen saturation on room air. ECG normal. Laboratory studies revealed cTnI (Beckman assay) of 0.4 mcg/l (normal <0.04). In ED, pain free. Normal physical exam. Given this laboratory abnormality, patient was hospitalized for 9 days with multiple investigations and unnecessary treatments. At discharge cTnI 0.3. Still no obvious explanations for elevated cTnI. Patient had two repeat cTnIs post discharge. First one at a private lab which revealed <0.01. Second test 0.1 at our pathology. The blood test was sent to QLD pathology which showed cTnI 0.0. There was interference in the Beckman assay due to heterophile antibodies. Issues of Interest in the Case: (1) Highlight the underreported issue of false positive cTnI secondary to heterophile antibodies (2) Raise awareness of the large cost and patient risks associated with false positive cTnI (3) Knowledge about incidence, clinical correlation, and clinical significance of falsely elevated cTnI due to heterophile antibodies are important in the current era of widespread and indiscriminate use of cardiac Troponins.
