N2  - Case details: A 39yo G2P1 at 27 weeks gestation was brought to the emergency room after a gunshot wound to the chest. She was intubated. Blood, fluids, and vasopressors were started immediately. A fetal heart rate in the 130's was appreciated. Bilateral chest tubes were placed and a unilateral hemothorax was confirmed. The patient was taken to the operating room where a thoracotomy, repair of myocardium, and extraction of bullet was performed. The fetal heart rate remained in the 130's. The patient was extubated on postoperative day 3. She remained afebrile. Chest tubes were removed on day 5. However the patient continued to require low doses of vasopressors. Each time vasopressors were weaned, the patient had episodic symptomatic hypotension. On postoperative day 9, a morning serum cortisol was 0.04 micrograms per deciliter. A serum thyroxine level was less than 0.7 micrograms. The patient was diagnosed with Sheehan's syndrome during pregnancy. She was started on prednisone and L-thyroxine. Her insulin regimen for known gestational diabetes was modified. She was discharged home. The fetus was growing normally at 31 weeks. Pregnancy outcome information is not yet available. Discussion: Hypopituitarism during pregnancy is an endocrine emergency. The differential diagnosis of this rare condition includes pituitary necrosis and lymphocytic hypophysitis. Pituitary necrosis during pregnancy can be caused by an enlarging sellar mass such as prolactinoma, a spontaneous infarction in type 1 diabetics, or a subarachnoid hemorrhage. Symptoms of severe disease include headache, hypoglycemia, or hypotension. Symptoms of mild disease include postural hypotension, tachycardia, fatigue, anorexia, and weight loss. Failure to recognize mild disease has been a cause of maternal mortality. To our knowledge, this is the first reported case of pituitary infarction in pregnancy due to massive antepartum hemorrhage. As in this case, management includes thyroid and corticosteroid replacement. Rarely, patients may develop diabetes insipidus and require vasopressin. There are 51 reported cases of pregnancies with live births in women with hypopituitarism. An increased incidence of transverse lie and small-forgestational age infants was noted. Otherwise no increase in pregnancy complications was noted. During delivery and during any times of stress, high dose steroid supplementation is necessary.
