N2  - OBJECTIVE: Pregnancy complications, such as preeclampsia and gestational diabetes can herald maternal cardiovascular disease later in life. Studies investigating the sole effect of pregnancy on later maternal vascular function are lacking. The objective of this study was to test the hypothesis that pregnancy alone is a factor that alters maternal vascular function later in life. STUDY DESIGN: Virgin CD-1 female mice were placed on a standard fat diet, and at four months of age were randomly allocated to breeder or non breeder group. The breeder group proceeded through a normal pregnancy and delivery. At ten months of age, the mice were sacrificed and the right carotid artery was used for in-vitro vascular reactivity studies (n= 4-6 per group). Concentration-response curves to contractile agents: phenylephrine (PE), thromboxane (TBX), and serotonin (5-HT), and relaxant agents: acetylcholine (Ach), sodium nitro-prusside (SNP) and isoproterenol (ISO) were obtained. Student t-test and Mann-Whitney Rank Sum tests were used for statistical analysis (significance: p<0.05). RESULTS: There were no significant differences between the breeder and non-breeder groups with respect to weight, weight of abdominal fat, percent body fat, heart weight, or abdominal fat/body weight ratio. The breeder group had increased contractility to TBX (figure) and 5-HT. The parous mice also had an impaired relaxant response to SNP and to Ach at higher concentrations (10-7 - 10 -5 ), while demonstrating a better relaxation to Ach at lower concentrations (10-9) compared with the nulliparous. There were no differences in responses to PE and ISO between the two groups. {Figure presented} CONCLUSION: Pregnancy itself has a direct and long-lasting effect on the vasculature that persists beyond the postpartum period. Together, these changes can contribute to abnormal cardiovascular function later in life.
