N2  - Introduction: Reduction in the relative frequency of fetal macrosomia caused by fetal hyperinsulinemia in patients with gestational diabetes mellitus has been rather unsuccessful under the present management strategy of trying to maintain glucose levels within specified limits. In most reports, the relative frequency of macrosomia has remained essentially unchanged. Our concern about fetal hyperinsulinemia has increased since the demonstration that insulin containing micropump implantation in the fetus of rhesus monkeys produced proliferation of fat cells, and myocytes of the myocardium, thus providing explanation for childhood obesity and hypertension of children born macrosomic to mothers with gestational diabetes. The other approach to reduce fetal macrosomia has been to shorten the exposure to hyperinsulinemia by accelerating the maturation of the fetus with intraamniotic administration of thyroxin, and delivery before the conclusion of the 36th week of gestation. This approach apparently in being used only by one institution, namely the University of Puerto Rico. At the annual meeting of The Society for Gynecologic Investigation in 1999 they presented 16 cases in which the fetus had received 2 or 3 weekly doses of 500 micrograms of thyroxin intraamniotically, which produced L-S ratio of amniotic fluid of 2.5 or more. All patients were delivered before conclusion of the 35th week. Mean birth weight was 3208 g, with the largest newborn who was 36 weeks old, weighing 3523 g. No newborn required respiratory assistance, and there was no case of neonatal hypoglycemia. When one patient with a macrosomic fetus (estimated weight by sonography of about 3,400 g) at 34 weeks of gestation, and an L/S of 1.17 refused the proposed treatment with thyroxin, she was offered an attempt to accelerated fetal maturation by increasing fetal release of TRH by producing fluctuations if fetal P02 by uterine contractions. After 6 hours of oxytocin infusion daily the L-S ratio after 48 hrs. had increased to 2.20 and after further 48 hours to 3.25. Such progression in L-S ratio had required 3 weekly administrations of intraamniotic thyroxin. On the fifth day the fetus was delivered by cesarean section. The newborn received an Apgar score of 9, and was cared for in the regular nursery. Based on this information, our Institutional Review Board approved a study to accelerate fetal maturation in gestational diabetes with oxytocin produced uterine contractions leading to increased release of endogenous T3 and T4 by the fetus. Material and Methods: Fifteen patients with gestational diabetes volunteered to participate in the study. Maternal age ranged between - and - years, and the parity between - and -. Six (insert the correct number) patients had given previously birth to macrosomic fetuses. The gestational age at the time of enrolment ranged between 33 and 35 weeks, based on the last menstrual period. First trimester sonographic evaluation of the fetus gave values between 33 weeks and 37 weeks. Sonographic evaluation of the fetus was performed prior to stimulation of the uterus. Oxytocin infusion was given for 6 to 8 hours every day for 5 days. In 2 cases it was given for 7 days to reach the L-S ratio. The dose of oxytocin was adjusted to produce contractions noticeable by the patient. Some patients reported the contractions as painful. Fetal heart rate and uterine contractions were recorded continuously during the period of stimulation. Pelvic examination was performed before and at the conclusion of the oxytocin infusion. No cervical dilatation was noted. Lecithinsphingomyelin ratio of amniotic fluid was determined in the afternoon of the 5th day, and in two cases again on the 7th day. In our laboratory a ratio of 1.75 indicates sufficient fetal maturity as not to require any respiratory assistance to the newborn. Labor was induced with oxytocin and Cytotec after obtaining the report of L/S of 1.75 or more Cesarean section was required in 3 cases in which there was prolonged failure of labor to progress. In two patients cesarean section was done because of previous cesarean section. (Table presented). Results: Relevant variables of 15 patients with gestational diabetes mellitus in whom fetal maturation was accelerated with endogenous release of T3 and T4 brought about by fluctuations in fetal oxygenation caused by oxytocin produced uterine contractions are presented in Table 1. In all but one patient (TL), in whom the initial L-S ratio was 1.5, the L-S for all others was 1.1 to 1.3. Evaluation of the newborns: Although none of the 15 newborns had RDS, four newborns had short episodes of tachypnea. Three had hyperbilirubinemia. All had good tissue oxygenation as expressed in the arterial pH values ranging between 7.35 and 7.45. All had high values of Hgb, which ranged between 22.0 g and 29.0 g per deciliter, and peripheral venous Hct above 65 percent. To rule out hemoconcentration due to dehydration of the mother, all received oral liquids, including milk, within 2 to 4 hours after birth. All newborns were discharged home with their mothers.
