N2  - Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is a common metabolic disorder of mid-to late-pregnancy that impairs fetal formation and increases risk of maternal type 2 diabetes, Adult consumption of fast food (high fat diet; HFD) has been linked to increased risk of multisystem diseases, and is believed to contribute to poor pregnancy outcome. However, the rote of gestational HFD in risk of GDM and fetal malformation is incompletely understood, Animal models of diabetes during pregnancy are crucial to help us more completely understand the pathophysiologic aspects of human GDM; however, currently available animal models do not closely reflect the human condition of GDM, The present study endeavors to develop a mouse model using HFD that closely simulates the common metabolic abnormalities seen in human GDM. This model will then be used to explore effects of HFD and resulting oxidative stress (OS) on placental vascular formation, as a putative explanation for HFD-induced fetal malformation, Additionally, this model will be used to explore potential benefits of dietary antioxidants to reduce adverse effects of HFD and GDM. In this study, female C57BU6J mice were fed a specially formulated rodent chow containing maeronutrients mimicking typical fast food (including 32.1% saturated fat), for 4 weeks prior to breeding and throughout pregnancy. Female body weight, blood glucose, and non-fasting plasma insulin levels were recorded before and after HFD treatment and during pregnancy at gestational days 0, 10, 19, and compared with females fed standard rodent chow, GD19 placental weight and placental OS were also measured, and placental vasculopathy was evaluated by histopaihological analysis and CD31 immunofluoresence staining of endothelium. HFD significantly increased body weight, blood glucose and plasma insulin compared with control, and those dams developed clinical signs of GDM during pregnancy Additionally, GD19 placental weight of HFD-GDM dams was decreased proportionately to increased placental OS, with decreased viability of trophoblasts and endothelial cells. Reduced CD31 immunofluorescence staining intensity supported HFD-induced placental vasculopathy. Quereetin antioxidant supplementation reduced HFD-induced hyperglycemia, insulin resistance and placental vasculopathy, Taken together, our study suggests that murine gestational HFD increases risk of GDM and placental damage, and quercetion supplementation may reduce these risks.
