N2  - Results: The intensive lifestyle intervention used in the Diabetes Prevention Program (DPP) not only prevented diabetes but reduced CVD risk factors. In 2008 the Montana Diabetes Project (MDP) funded and trained lifestyle coaches from 4 health care facilities to implement the 16 week DPP core curriculum for groups followed by 6 monthly sessions. Adults (N = 355) at high risk for diabetes and CVD were recruited. Participants obtained medical clearance from their primary care physicians and were eligible if they were overweight and had one or more additional factors: pre-diabetes, a history of gestational diabetes or delivery of a baby > 9lbs, hypertension, low HDL for gender or TG > 200 mg/dl. At entry participants were formally assessed as ready to make changes in their diet and physical activity levels and signed a contract indicating their willingness to participate. Participants set targets to reduce fat intake, increase physical activity (>150 minutes per week) and achieve 7% weight loss. Of the 355 participants enrolled 293 (83%) completed the 16-week core curriculum. The mean age of participants was 53.6 ± SD 9.7, 20% were male, and mean BMI at baseline was 35.9 kg/m ± 6.5. At the end of the core curriculum, participants completing the program lost an average of 6.7 ± 4.0 kg or 6.7% of initial body weight (p <0.0001). Forty-five percent achieved the 7% weight loss goal and 67 percent achieved 5% weight loss. In multivariate analysis, factors independently associated with achieving the 7% weight loss goal were: age >60 years, physician diagnosis of hypertension, increased frequency of dietary fat self-monitoring, and having met the physical activity goal. Weight loss was similar across all 4 sites, and there were no differences in achieved weight loss between the 2 sites charging a modest fee (or provided a scholarship) for participation compared to those who did not. It was feasible to identify, recruit and retain participants at high risk for diabetes and cardiovascular disease in a 16 week DPP group lifestyle intervention. Diabetes educators were effective lifestyle coaches and participants achieved short term weight loss and activity goals comparable to those achieved in the DPP.
