N2  - Metabolic disturbances of GDM are responsible of increased morbidity for both the mother and the offspring. At short term, new onset hypertension and pre-eclampsia may be present in advanced pregnancy. Adverse perinatal outcome is explained by the continuous relationship between maternal blood glucose levels and the risk of fetal morbidity. At long term, the woman may experience GDM recurrence, progression to IGT/DM, hypertension, metabolic syndrome and cardiovascular disease. For the offspring, increased risk of obesity, IGT, and DM have been recognized. Between 1992 and 1996, a total of 1523 diabetic pregnancies (including 1290 cases of GDM) were attended at the Diabetes and Pregnancy Clinic at our Center. Women underwent an intensive therapy program, which followed the recommendations of the ACOG. Mean weight gain for the whole pregnancy was 9.5 kg; 66% of women required insulin treatment for at least sometime. Maternal and fetal outcomes for pregestational and gestational diabetes, as well as non-diabetic pregnancies attended during the same period, are presented for comparison. We have also investigated the relationship of potential predictors with the risk of congenital malformations (CM) in a cohort of more than 2000 infants of mothers with GDM. The CM rate was 6% for minor and 3.8% for major malformations (1,4 % heart; 0.8% renal/urinary; 0.7% skeketal; 0.3% hypospadias; 0.2% central nervous system; 0.2% cleft/lip/palate; 0.1% digestive tract; 0.3% others). The bivariate analysis showed a higher rate for CM with increasing fasting blood glucose (FBG). A positive relationship was observed regarding previous hyperglycemia, prepregnancy BMI, lower gestational age at diagnosis, number of abnormal blood glucose values in both the diagnostic and postpartum OGTT, and higher HbA1c at diagnosis. The logistic regression analysis identified the following predictors of CM : 4 abnormal values in the diagnostic OGTT, and/or overt diabetes during pregnancy (odds ratio, 4.93). By the forward analysis, pre-pregnancy BMI was the single predictor of major CM (positive association). Gestational age at diagnosis presented a negative association. Specific associations with distinct congenital lesion will be discussed. We have also investigated the influence of early presentation of hyperglycemia in pregnancy. The study include 2.049 offsprings; pregnancies were classified into 3 groups, according to the gestational age at the time of diagnosis. Pregnancy induced hypertension more commonly appeared at the second period. Need of insulin therapy and cesarean section rates increased with earlier GDM diagnosis.Regarding fetal outcomes, gestational age was associated with 6/11 parameters of morbidity (Apgar score, perinatal mortality, major malformations, hyperbilirrubinemia, respiratory distress, prematurity). We have performed a prospective follow up research protocol after partum in a group of 982 women and 70 control pregnancies. In the GDM group, cumulative risk for diabetes/abnormal glucose tolerance (AGT) was 13.8 and 42.4%, respectively after 11 years, compared with 0 and 2.8% in controls (p< 0.05). Five women (11.4% of all diabetes cases) presented with classical DM-1A. Multiple regression and ROC were investigated, and independent predictors were identified. The risk of developing the metabolic syndrome (MS) was also analysed in a prospective follow-up of a cohort of 262 women with previous GDM and a control group of 66 healthy women. We estimated insulin sensitivity and secretion with HOMA. Main Conclusions: 1) Prevention of adverse maternal and perinatal outcomes in GDM are based in acheiving maternal blood glucose as close to normal as possible. Precise glycemic thresholds remain undetermined. 2) Prepregnancy BMI, duration and severity of maternal hyperglycemia during pregnancy, are most important predictors of the progression to abnormal glucose tolerance/diabetes in the follow up. 3) In GDM, severity of hyperglycemia and prepregnancy BMI are predictors of CM. BMI is the main predictor of most prevalent malformations. 4) Earlier diagnosis of GDM is associated with a less favorable outcome, specially for the newborn 5) SGA neonates from pregnancies of gestational diabetic women receiving intensive therapy, depicted increased risk for adverse outcomes. 6) Women with former GDM have similar sensitivity but lower insulin secretion and disposition index than controls. 7) Age and insulin resistance (defined as HOMA index) are predictors of the metabolic syndrome, being marginal the contribution of former GDM.
