N2  - Gestational diabetes (GDM), defined as glucose intolerance during pregnancy, increases the risk of type 2 diabetes and endothelial dysfunction in offspring (Buchanan et al., 2005). We previously reported that GDM is associated with altered fetal endothelial signaling (Sobrevia et al., 1995). As an impaired redox balance contributes to endothelial dysfunction and the development of diabetes (Thomas et al, 2008), we hypothesize that comprised antioxidant defenses against oxidative stress in fetal endothelium contribute to the pathogenesis of cardiovascular disease in later life. In the current study, we have examined basal and 4-hydroxynonenal (HNE) induced expression of NAD(P)H quinine oxidoreductase-1 (NQO1), a phase II defense enzyme regulated by the redox sensitive transcription factor Nrf2. NQO1expression and Nrf2 nuclear translocation were correlated with intracellular glutathione (GSH) levels in human umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVEC) isolated from normal and GDM pregnancies. HUVEC were equilibrated in M199 (1% FCS) for 4 h and then treated with HNE (20μM) for 0-24h, lyzed, then analyzed for intracellular GSH and protein levels of NQO1, Nrf2 and DJ-1. In normal HUVEC, HNE increased GSH levels (60.7 ± 10.1 to 96.8 ± 9.9 nmol/mg protein, S.E.M., n=5, p<0.05), NQO1 expression (2-fold, n=3, p<0.05) and nuclear translocation of Nrf2. These adaptive antioxidant responses were significantly abrogated in HUVEC isolated from GDM pregnancies. As GDM is associated altered maternal metabolism and endothelial function, our findings provide novel insights into the molecular mechanisms underlying altered redox signaling the fetal vasculature in gestational diabetes which may have significant implications for 'fetal programming'.
