N2  - The remarkable observations made by Barker et al and Hales et al [6-9] and others linking size at birth and early postnatal growth to risks of adult disease have moved on from being questions for the intellectually curious to being fundamental to current public health concerns about increasing rates of obesity and T2DM in children. Recent reports have highlighted the increasing prevalence of type 2 diabetes in childhood [137], and this mirrors the increasing rate of postnatal weight gain and childhood obesity [25]. The risk of diabetes associated with obesity is particularly evident in ethnic populations, in which there has been a dramatic improvement in nutrition, as evident in immigrant groups [138], and in countries where there has been a move from rural to urban environments [139]. These trends may reflect changes in nutritional status of mothers "programmed" by fetal and early postnatal under nutrition. Studies in South Asian populations and, most recently, in East Germany where there have been dramatic recent improvements in health and nutrition show that the first effect of increased maternal nutrition is greater maternal pregnancy weight gain [140]. Maternal weight gain is itself associated with increased risk for gestational diabetes, and this constitutes a risk for T2DM in the offspring [19]. It is likely that these same populations also may have a high prevalence of "thrifty genes," which have resulted in improved survival during periods of poor nutrition, but the same metabolic and endocrine adaptations may prove detrimental and increase risk of adult disease when nutrition improves and obesity becomes more prevalent. The study of the mechanisms and genetic factors involved in the association between small size at birth and adult disease may provide us with the method for identifying those at highest risk and introducing targeted early interventions. © 2005 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
