N2  - Diabetes mellitus is becoming one of the major health problems of the 21st century. The actual status of the epidemiology of diabetes type 2 and its implications on public health are discussed. Following items are presented: diagnostic criteria, size of the problem, epidemiology of diabetes type 2 in Belgium, the importance of complications for public health, possibilities for prevention of diabetes type 2. Diagnostic criteria have recently been adapted by the American Diabetes Association (ADA) and the World Health Organization (WHO). Up to now, diabetes is one of the most frequent non-infectious diseases on a worldwide scale; it rises as well in industrialised as in developing and newly industrialised countries. This increase is not only due to demographic changes, improved treatment and consequently a better survival of patients with diabetes, but also to an increase in classical risk factors for diabetes type 2 development i.e. increasing obesity (also in youth), lack of physical activity and unhealthy eating habits. This is documented with own research. The relatively scarce data on epidemiology of diabetes type 2 itself in Belgium are discussed. Long-term complications of diabetes type 2 occur mainly in the cardiovascular field: ischaemic heart diseases are the most important cause of death in patients. Recently a number of studies have revealed the possibility of preventing the development of diabetes type 2 in high-risk individuals.
