N2  - Hypoglycemia is the most common acute complication of diabetes (10% with recurrent episodes) and it's defined by a decrease in plasma glucose concentration (below 45-50 mg/dl) that determines the presence of symptoms that will disappear with the plasma glucose levels normalization. This complication is more common in the pregnant woman with type 1 diabetes due to intensive insulin treatment and counter regulation impairment. At this moment, the management of type 1 diabetes during pregnancy requires a very accurate plasma glucose concentration control before conception and during pregnancy; physician must prescribe intensive insulin treatment that may result in frequent and serious hypoglycemia episodes. Hypoglycemia can appear with non-specific symptoms, like many other clinical conditions and diseases, but generally, we can classify them in two groups: adrenergic symptoms and neuroglucopenic symptoms. There are several therapeutic approaches on hypoglycemia episodes, depending on their severity. On the other hand, hyperglycemic ketotic complications, especially diabetic ketoacidosis are metabolic emergencies that may lead to fetal or mother decease. Diabetic ketoacidosis is a severe metabolic complication with a very high morbility and mortality on mother and fetal. It typically appears in type 1 diabetic patients, but it can also appear in type 2 diabetes and gestational diabetes during stress situations or infections. This complication has dramatically decreased in recent years, due to the accurate control of diabetes during pregnancy.
