N2  - Diabetes mellitus is the endocrine disease associated most often with pregnancy. Insuline Resistance increase during pregnancy is the main cause of Gestational Diabetes. Both, overt Diabetes mellitus and Gestational Diabetes give a poorer prognosis to pregnancy, but the later only because the higher frequency of fetal macrosomia. Clinical Diabetes Mellitus in pregnancy is associated with higher frequencies of preeclampsia, hydramnios, preterm labor and delivery, miscarriages, fetal malformations, alterations of fetal growth (mainly fetal overgrowth) and of fotal maturity, fetal hypoxia and fetal an neonatal death. Good metabolic control since before and during preguancy is the best profilaxis of these problems although additional benefits comes from a diet rich in antioxydants, folic acid and Calcium supplements and, in selected cases, platelet antiagregants (aspirine). Screening of fetal cromosomopaties (its risk seems also higher in gestational diabetes) and fetal malformations rest on sonographies performed at 12 and 20 weeks respectively, and that of fetal growth deviations in sonographies at 28 and 34 weeks. Fetal welfare is followed by weekly non stress test since weeks 34 or 37, the later in those cases of gestational diabetes or clinical diabetes with good metabolic control. In interpreting fetal heart rate patterns is important to consider that these fetuses have also a maturative delay in their vegetative system. Now, threatened premature labor is best managed with Atosiban, so the metabolic effects of betamimetics could be avoided. In pregnancies with good metabolic control and normal fetal growth delivery is planned at 40 week. In those cases with deficient metabolic control or with fetal growth acceleration delivery is otherwise planned at an estimated fetal weight of 4000 g., but in any case we try to reach, at least, the 38 week. Then if the estimated fetal weight is above 4500 g we choose a elective cesarean section.
